update public.species set body = $v$Amano shrimp are larger, hardier, and far better algae-eaters than Neocaridina, but they're very hard to breed because larvae need brackish water. They're a workhorse cleanup crew for planted tanks. Never use copper medications or plant products in their tank, since copper is deadly to shrimp.$v$ where slug = $v$amano-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set body = $v$Instead of grazing, it perches in the flow and unfurls feathery fans to strain food from the water, so it needs good current and a mature tank with suspended particles. Completely peaceful and fascinating to watch. Provide a strong filter outlet to sit near. Never use copper medications or plant products in their tank, since copper is deadly to shrimp.$v$ where slug = $v$bamboo-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set body = $v$Hardier than its graded crystal descendants but still prefers soft, slightly acidic water and cool temperatures. Keep 10 or more in a mature, planted tank with stable parameters and no large fish. Can crossbreed with crystal and Taiwan bee shrimp. Never use copper medications or plant products in their tank, since copper is deadly to shrimp.$v$ where slug = $v$bee-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set max_size_in = 1.2 where slug = $v$bloody-mary-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set max_size_in = 1.2 where slug = $v$blue-diamond-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set max_size_in = 1.2 where slug = $v$blue-dream-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set max_size_in = 1.2 where slug = $v$blue-rili-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set temp_max_f = 76, social = $v$Colony$v$ where slug = $v$blue-tiger-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set also_known_as = '{}'::text[], ph_max = 8.0, max_size_in = 1.2, social = $v$Colony$v$, min_group_size = 10 where slug = $v$blue-velvet-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set max_size_in = 1.2 where slug = $v$carbon-rili-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set max_size_in = 1.2, body = $v$Cherry shrimp are forgiving algae- and biofilm-grazers that breed readily in a stable, copper-free tank. The many color forms (blue, yellow, orange, green and more) are all the same species, Neocaridina davidi. Keep pH between 6.5 and 8 with some hardness (GH 4 to 14) so they molt cleanly.$v$ where slug = $v$cherry-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set max_size_in = 1.2, body = $v$Crystal reds are graded by pattern and need carefully controlled soft, acidic water, usually remineralized RO over an active soil. They are rewarding but far less forgiving than cherry shrimp. Never use copper medications or plant products in their tank, since copper is deadly to shrimp.$v$ where slug = $v$crystal-red-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set max_size_in = 1.2 where slug = $v$fire-red-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set body = $v$Ghost shrimp are inexpensive, transparent, and useful scavengers, though larger ones can occasionally nip tiny fish or fry. Quality and source vary widely since many are sold as feeders. Never use copper medications or plant products in their tank, since copper is deadly to shrimp.$v$ where slug = $v$ghost-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set max_size_in = 1.2 where slug = $v$painted-fire-red-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set max_size_in = 1.2 where slug = $v$red-rili-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set temp_max_f = 76, social = $v$Colony$v$, min_group_size = 10, body = $v$A Caridina shrimp that prefers cooler, softer, slightly acidic water than cherries. Breeds in freshwater and grazes biofilm and algae. Keep a colony in a stable, mature tank away from fish that would eat them. Never use copper medications or plant products in their tank, since copper is deadly to shrimp.$v$ where slug = $v$tiger-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set ph_min = 5.5 where slug = $v$dwarf-rasbora$v$;
update public.species set ph_min = 5.5 where slug = $v$hengels-rasbora$v$;
update public.species set min_tank_gal = 55 where slug = $v$scissortail-rasbora$v$;
update public.species set body = $v$A peaceful giant that perches in the flow and unfurls feathery fans to catch suspended food, so it needs good current and a mature tank. Despite the name, it is gentle and shy, often changing color from blue to pink. Provide hiding spots and steady water flow. Never use copper medications or plant products in their tank, since copper is deadly to shrimp.$v$ where slug = $v$vampire-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set body = $v$Larger and more predatory than ghost shrimp, it will catch small fish, fry, and dwarf shrimp. Keep with medium sized peaceful fish and provide plenty of cover. Unlike most prawns its young develop fully in fresh water, so it can breed in the aquarium. Like all shrimp, it is very sensitive to copper, so never use copper medications in its tank.$v$ where slug = $v$whisker-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set max_size_in = 1.2 where slug = $v$yellow-shrimp$v$;
update public.species set body = $v$Assassin snails burrow in sand and prey on pest snails like bladder and ramshorn, making them a living solution to outbreaks. They breed slowly and won't explode in numbers like the snails they eat. Keep pH at 7 or above with some hardness (GH 8 or more) so the shell stays smooth and strong, and avoid copper medications, which kill snails.$v$ where slug = $v$assassin-snail$v$;
update public.species set body = $v$MTS turn over and aerate the substrate and clean up detritus, but they breed prolifically when overfed and are nearly impossible to fully remove. Many keepers value them; others consider them a pest, depending on goals. Keep pH at 7 or above with some hardness (GH 8 or more) so the shell stays smooth and strong, and avoid copper medications, which kill snails.$v$ where slug = $v$malaysian-trumpet-snail$v$;
update public.species set body = $v$Mystery snails come in gold, blue, ivory and more, and unlike many apple snails they leave living plants alone. They need calcium-rich, harder water for strong shells and lay clutches above the waterline. Keep pH at 7 or above and avoid copper medications, which kill snails.$v$ where slug = $v$mystery-snail$v$;
update public.species set body = $v$Nerites are champion algae grazers whose larvae require brackish or salt water, so they never become a pest in a freshwater tank. They may lay harmless white eggs on hard surfaces, and prefer harder water for healthy shells. Keep pH at 7 or above and avoid copper medications, which kill snails.$v$ where slug = $v$nerite-snail$v$;
update public.species set gh_min = 6, body = $v$A handy cleanup snail that eats algae, leftover food, and decaying plant matter. It breeds readily, which is great for cleanup but can mean managing numbers by limiting excess food. Available in attractive red and blue color morphs. Keep pH at 7 or above with some hardness (GH 6 or more) so the shell stays smooth and strong, and avoid copper medications, which kill snails.$v$ where slug = $v$ramshorn-snail$v$;
update public.species set ph_min = 6.0, min_tank_gal = 15, summary = $v$A slender, peaceful small fish with a bold dark side stripe and red-tinted fins that color up when males display.$v$ where slug = $v$beckford-pencilfish$v$;
update public.species set ph_min = 6.0 where slug = $v$black-neon-tetra$v$;
update public.species set ph_min = 5.5, min_tank_gal = 20 where slug = $v$cardinal-tetra$v$;
update public.species set ph_min = 5.5 where slug = $v$dwarf-pencilfish$v$;
