-- Breeding guides
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Egg scatterer (adhesive eggs, no parental care)", "sexing": "Ripe males show white breeding tubercles on gill covers and front fin rays; females are rounder", "group": "One female to 2 or 3 males", "breeding_tank": "Large tank or pond with spawning mops or fine plants and strong filtration", "conditioning": "Varied foods, including frozen or live foods, after a cooler winter period", "trigger": "After a short winter rest in the low 60s°F, water warming to about 68 to 72°F with longer days", "spawn": "Commonly several hundred to over a thousand eggs", "eggs": "Hatch in about 3 to 6 days at 68 to 72°F; cooler water is slower", "fry": "Free swimming about 2 to 3 days after hatching", "first_foods": "Liquid or powdered fry food and infusoria, then baby brine shrimp", "grow_out": "About 1 inch in 2 to 3 months; color change can take months longer", "parents": "Eat eggs; move mops to a hatching tank or remove adults after spawning"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting Up for Spawning", "text": "Goldfish usually spawn in their main tank or pond, so the setup is about catching eggs. Hang several spawning mops or add fine leaved plants where the fish can push through. Keep filtration strong in the adults' tank, but prepare a separate hatching tank of 10 to 20 gallons with a sponge filter and water from the main tank.\n\nThe site's everyday care range for goldfish is 65 to 74°F with harder, alkaline water, and the spawning temperatures of 68 to 72°F sit inside it. A natural pattern works best: a few weeks of slightly cooler water in winter as a rest period, then a steady warm up into spring."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and Sexing", "text": "In breeding season, males develop small white bumps called breeding tubercles on the gill covers and the leading rays of the pectoral fins. They also chase females. Females are rounder from above and may show a slightly protruding vent. Outside the season, sexing is unreliable, so don't rely on body shape alone.\n\nCondition fish well in late winter with a varied diet: quality pellets, gel food, frozen bloodworms and brine shrimp, and blanched vegetables. Healthy fish of a year or older, with full bodies, spawn most reliably."}, {"heading": "Spawning: What You'll See", "text": "As the water warms to about 68 to 72°F and the days get longer, males start following and nudging the female, often pushing at her belly and vent. Spawning usually starts early in the morning and can be frantic.\n\nThe female scatters eggs through the mops and plants while the males fertilize them. The eggs are sticky and cling wherever they land. Chasing can be rough on the female, so watch for damage and make sure she has room to rest. Spawning can repeat every week or two through spring."}, {"heading": "Eggs and Fungus Prevention", "text": "A large female can release several hundred to over a thousand eggs. Lift the mops into the hatching tank as soon as spawning stops, or remove the adults if you're using a separate breeding setup. Goldfish eat their own eggs eagerly.\n\nAt 68 to 72°F the eggs hatch in about 3 to 6 days; cooler water takes longer. Infertile eggs turn white and fuzzy. Add gentle aeration and pick out fungused eggs with tweezers or a pipette so the fungus doesn't spread to good ones."}, {"heading": "Raising the Fry", "text": "Newly hatched fry cling to the glass and mops, then swim freely about 2 to 3 days later.\n\n- First days of swimming: liquid or powdered fry food, infusoria or green water\n- From about day 3 to 5: newly hatched baby brine shrimp, several feeds a day\n- Weeks 3 to 6: baby brine shrimp plus crushed flake or fry powder\n\nGoldfish fry produce a lot of waste. Change 20 to 30 percent of the water every 1 to 2 days once they're eating well, matching temperature carefully. Keep them around 68 to 72°F."}, {"heading": "Growing Out and Culling", "text": "Fry reach about 1 inch in 2 to 3 months with heavy feeding and frequent water changes. Most start out dark bronze and change to gold, red or white over the following months; some never change.\n\nA spawn can be far larger than you can house. Thin the batch early and move fish into bigger tanks or a pond as they grow. Fancy varieties often produce many fry without the parents' traits, so keep the best and plan humane culling or rehoming for the rest. Never release goldfish into ponds or waterways."}, {"heading": "Common Mistakes", "text": "- Leaving eggs with the adults overnight\n- Keeping too many fry in too little water, which stunts growth\n- Skipping the cool winter period, then wondering why fish won't spawn\n- Letting fungus spread through a mop of eggs\n- Releasing extra fish outdoors instead of rehoming them"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$common-goldfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Cave spawner (eggs stuck to the ceiling of a tile, pot or pipe and guarded by the male)", "sexing": "Breeding males have dark heads, bumps on the snout and a pad behind the head; females are plain", "group": "1 male to 2 or 3 females, or a group with one spawning site per male", "breeding_tank": "20 gallons, clay pots, split PVC pipes or slate tiles as spawning caves, sponge filter", "conditioning": "Frozen or live foods plus flake for 1 to 2 weeks", "trigger": "Longer days and water warming to about 70 to 77°F", "spawn": "Commonly 50 to a few hundred eggs per spawning", "eggs": "Hatch in about 4 to 6 days at around 75 to 77°F", "fry": "Free swimming soon after hatching; small", "first_foods": "Infusoria or liquid fry food, then baby brine shrimp and microworms", "grow_out": "About 1 inch in 6 to 10 weeks", "parents": "Male guards and cleans the eggs; adults may eat fry, so move eggs or fry out"}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$rosy-red-minnow$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Cave spawner (both parents guard)", "sexing": "Females have orange or gold scales on the belly; males are larger with a steeper forehead", "group": "One pair", "breeding_tank": "About 40 gallons, caves or flowerpots, sand or fine gravel, sponge filter", "conditioning": "Pellets plus frozen or live foods for 1 to 2 weeks", "trigger": "A bonded pair with a cave and water around 76 to 80°F", "spawn": "Commonly 100 to 300 eggs", "eggs": "Hatch in about 3 days at 78 to 80°F", "fry": "Free swimming about 4 to 6 days after hatching, guarded by both parents", "first_foods": "Baby brine shrimp and crushed flake or fry powder", "grow_out": "About 1 inch in 6 to 8 weeks", "parents": "Excellent parents; fry can stay with them for several weeks"}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$convict-cichlid$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting Up the Breeding Tank", "text": "A 10 gallon or larger tank works well. The key feature is space above the water: lower the water level 2 to 4 inches and fit a tight lid. Females crawl out to lay their eggs on the glass, the lid or the rim, and the clutch needs warm, humid air to develop.\n\nKeep water within the site's ranges of 68 to 82°F, pH 7 to 8.5 and GH 8 to 20. Hard, alkaline water matters for snails because they build their shells from calcium. Use a sponge filter or a covered intake so hatchlings aren't pulled in."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and Choosing Breeders", "text": "Mystery snails have separate sexes, but you can't reliably tell them apart by shell color or size. Some keepers check by gently holding a snail upside down until it extends from its shell: a male shows a sheath under the mantle on the right side. Since that takes practice, most breeders keep 4 to 6 snails and let nature sort it out.\n\nFeed well with algae wafers, sinking pellets and blanched zucchini or spinach. Add a calcium source such as cuttlebone or a mineral supplement for strong shells."}, {"heading": "Mating and Egg Laying", "text": "Mating looks like one snail riding on top of another's shell, often for hours. Females can store sperm and may lay several clutches from one mating.\n\nEgg laying usually happens at night. The female climbs above the waterline and lays a clutch of soft eggs, which hardens into a light pink cluster within a few hours. Clutch size varies widely, from roughly 50 to a few hundred eggs. Don't move it while it's still soft."}, {"heading": "Caring for the Egg Clutch", "text": "At 72 to 80°F the clutch usually hatches in about 2 to 4 weeks. It gets darker and duller as the babies develop, and you may see tiny shapes inside near the end.\n\nThe clutch must stay humid but out of the water. If it dries out the eggs die, and if it is submerged they drown. A tight lid usually keeps humidity right. If the clutch looks dry, gently mist it, or place a damp paper towel nearby. Some keepers lift a hardened clutch into a small container over water; handle it gently if you do."}, {"heading": "Raising the Hatchlings", "text": "Hatchlings chew out of the clutch and drop into the water. They are tiny, fully formed snails and start grazing at once.\n\n- First weeks: biofilm and algae, plus crushed algae wafers and fine sinking food\n- Add blanched vegetables once they are a few millimeters across\n- Keep calcium available and water changes small and regular, about 10 to 20 percent weekly\n\nFish will eat newborn snails, so raise them in a fish free tank or a breeder box."}, {"heading": "Growing Out", "text": "With plenty of food, hatchlings grow quickly and reach around 1/2 inch in 1 to 2 months, with adult size of about 2 inches in several months. Shells should look smooth and solid; thin or pitted shells point to low calcium or soft water.\n\nOne clutch can give you more snails than you can house. If you don't want more, simply remove clutches before they hatch. Never release snails or put unwanted clutches anywhere they could reach natural waterways; apple snails can become serious pests outdoors."}, {"heading": "Common Mistakes", "text": "- Filling the tank to the brim, leaving no place to lay\n- Letting the clutch dry out or fall into the water\n- Soft, acidic water that erodes shells\n- Raising hatchlings with fish that eat them\n- Hatching more clutches than you can house\n- Copper based medicines or plant products, which kill snails"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$mystery-snail$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Egg depositor (female carries eggs briefly, then attaches them to plants)", "sexing": "Males have a notched dorsal fin and a larger, squarer anal fin; females have a smaller one", "group": "1 male to 2 or 3 females, or a small group", "breeding_tank": "10 gallons, floating plants or spawning mops, sponge filter", "conditioning": "Small live or frozen foods plus fine flake, fed 2 to 3 times a day", "trigger": "About 13 or more hours of light and water around 72 to 77°F", "spawn": "Often 10 to 30 eggs a day per female during the season", "eggs": "Hatch in about 7 to 14 days; nearer 7 to 10 days at 77°F", "fry": "Tiny at hatching; swim near the surface right away", "first_foods": "Green water, infusoria and fine powdered fry food, then baby brine shrimp", "grow_out": "About 3/4 inch in 2 to 3 months", "parents": "Adults eat eggs and fry; move eggs or plants to a hatching container"}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$medaka-ricefish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Fragments: any trimmed piece regrows", "light": "Low to moderate; more light means denser, faster growth but more algae risk", "co2": "Not needed; added CO2 speeds it up. Use liquid carbon lightly if at all, since some mosses melt from it", "substrate": "None needed; attach to wood, rock or mesh rather than burying it", "timeline": "New growth visible in 1 to 3 weeks; a fuller mat in 2 to 3 months", "first_steps": "Trim a healthy green clump and tease off a pinch of strands to attach"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "How Java Moss Propagates", "text": "Java moss is a moss, so it has no true roots and never flowers or sets seed. It spreads by fragmentation: every snipped strand can keep growing and send out small rhizoids that grip whatever it touches. That is why stray bits end up stuck in filters and on the glass.\n\nIn practice, propagation means taking pieces from a healthy clump and giving them a surface to anchor to. Over a few weeks the strands branch, grip on and thicken into a mat."}, {"heading": "What You Need", "text": "- A healthy parent clump, bright green rather than brown\n- Sharp aquarium scissors\n- Cotton thread, fishing line or a plant-safe gel glue\n- Something to attach to: driftwood, a rock, stainless or plastic mesh\n- A tank around 70 to 78°F with gentle to moderate flow\n\nThe moss does not need fertile substrate, CO2 or strong light. It does appreciate clean water and a little flow that keeps debris from settling on it."}, {"heading": "Step by Step", "text": "Trim a section from the parent clump. Rinse it in old tank water and pick out any debris or algae.\n\nSpread a thin layer over the surface you want covered. Thin is better than thick: a layer you can almost see through grows in faster and does not rot underneath.\n\nTie it down with cotton thread or fishing line wrapped every half inch or so, or dab small spots of gel glue and press the moss on. Cotton slowly breaks down once the moss has gripped; fishing line can be cut away later.\n\nFor a moss wall, sandwich a thin layer between two pieces of mesh and fix it to the back glass with suction cups."}, {"heading": "Caring for New Mats", "text": "For the first few weeks, keep flow gentle so pieces are not torn loose and keep the light moderate. Most growers see new bright green tips within 1 to 3 weeks.\n\nTrim the mat every few weeks once it fills in. Trimming keeps it dense, stops the inner layers from going brown, and gives you more pieces to start new mats. Clean trims out of the tank so they do not clog the filter intake.\n\nIf you dose a liquid fertilizer, a light dose is plenty, and in shrimp tanks choose one labeled shrimp safe or very low in copper. A gentle swish or turkey baster during water changes keeps mulm from building up in the mat.\n\nJava moss is happiest from about 70 to 78°F. Above that, growth slows and the mat can turn brown, so in a warm breeding room keep it in the coolest tank you have."}, {"heading": "Common Mistakes", "text": "- Piling on too much moss: thick layers block light and flow and the bottom turns brown\n- Too much light without enough plant mass: java moss traps algae easily, so balance light and nutrients\n- Leaving loose trimmings to drift: they jam filters and grow where you do not want them\n- Expecting it to grow fast in warm, still water: it does best below about 78°F with some flow\n- Burying it in the gravel: buried moss rots, so attach it on top of hardscape instead"}, {"heading": "Using Java Moss for Fry and Shrimp", "text": "Propagating java moss pays off in a breeding room. Shrimp graze biofilm from it all day, newborn livebearers hide in it, and egg scatterers will drop eggs into a dense clump where adults have trouble reaching them.\n\nA spare tank with a few mats growing on mesh squares gives you ready-made spawning mops and fry cover. Keep a starter clump going in a low light tank and you will always have pieces to share with other hobbyists or swap at club meetings."}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"q": "How fast does java moss grow?", "a": "It is a slow to moderate grower. Expect new tips within a few weeks and a full mat in a couple of months, faster with more light and some fertilizer."}, {"q": "Can java moss grow without being tied down?", "a": "Yes, it will grow loose, but it tends to drift into filters and corners. Tying or gluing it gives you a tidy mat that stays where you want it."}, {"q": "Why is my java moss turning brown?", "a": "Usually it is too thick, too warm, starved of light, or smothered by debris. Thin it out, trim the brown parts and improve flow."}, {"q": "Does java moss need CO2?", "a": "No. It grows fine without CO2. Adding CO2 can speed growth, but it is not necessary. Go easy with liquid carbon products, since some mosses melt from them and they need care around shrimp."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$java-moss$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "How Hornwort Propagates", "text": "Hornwort grows as long, branching stems covered in whorls of stiff needle-like leaves. It does not form true roots. Instead, it absorbs nutrients directly from the water through its leaves and stems.\n\nWhen a stem breaks or is cut, each piece keeps growing. Side branches also form along the stem, so a single stem quickly becomes a bushy clump."}, {"heading": "What You Need", "text": "- Healthy green stems, not ones that are brown and shedding\n- Sharp scissors\n- Moderate to bright light\n- Water anywhere from about 60 to 82°F; it handles cooler temperatures well\n- Optional: plant weights or a stone to anchor stems\n\nNo CO2 or substrate is needed. In a tank with fish, there are usually enough nutrients for strong growth."}, {"heading": "Step by Step", "text": "Choose a healthy stem and cut off a section several inches long, ideally a growing tip.\n\nDecide whether to float it or anchor it. Floating works well and keeps it near the light. To anchor it, slip the base of the stem gently under a rock or plant weight, or push it lightly into the substrate. Be aware that buried sections may rot.\n\nThe parent stem will branch from the cut point, so you get more growth on both sides of the cut.\n\nEach cut stem can be cut again once it grows. A good rule is to take tops about 4 to 6 inches long and keep the lower part in the tank to branch. Repeat every week or two and one bunch becomes a dozen."}, {"heading": "Caring for New Stems", "text": "New pieces usually show fresh green growth at the tips within days. Trim regularly once it gets going, or it will crowd out the surface and shade other plants.\n\nHornwort sometimes drops a lot of needles after being moved to a new tank. This is common and usually settles once it adjusts. Net out the fallen needles so they do not clog the filter.\n\nHornwort uses a lot of nutrients. In tanks with very few fish, a light dose of liquid fertilizer keeps it green. In heavily stocked tanks, it often grows fast enough that you will be removing a bunch every week. Pale or yellow tips usually mean it is short on nutrients or light."}, {"heading": "Common Mistakes", "text": "- Burying stems deep in the substrate, where they can rot\n- Moving it between very different tanks and panicking at the needle drop\n- Letting it take over the whole tank and shade slower plants\n- Using it in tanks with plant-eating fish that strip it\n- Forgetting to remove shed needles, which can clog intakes\n- Dosing liquid carbon products, which can make hornwort melt"}, {"heading": "Using Hornwort in Breeding Tanks", "text": "Hornwort is a favorite in breeding setups. Its dense stems give egg scatterers somewhere to drop eggs and give livebearer fry a place to hide. It also uses up nitrate and helps limit algae in tanks with heavy feeding.\n\nKeep a spare floating bunch in a grow-out tank and pull stems as needed. Because it grows so fast, it is also great trade material for club meetings."}, {"heading": "Moving Hornwort Between Tanks", "text": "Hornwort can be sensitive to sudden changes in temperature, hardness and pH. When moving cuttings to a new tank, float them in a bag for a while and then release them, the same way you would acclimate fish.\n\nRinse new stems from other people's tanks well and check for snails, hydra or pest eggs. A short quarantine in a bucket or spare tank is a good habit, especially before adding hornwort to a fry tank where hydra could harm tiny fry."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$hornwort$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting Up the Breeding Tank", "text": "A 10 gallon tank is plenty for one pair. Keep the water fairly shallow, around 6 to 8 inches, so the male does not have to work as hard to keep eggs in the nest and fry can reach the surface easily.\n\nAim for 79 to 82°F, soft water and a pH in the 5.5 to 7 range the species likes, toward the lower end for spawning. Floating plants and a few leaves help the male anchor a nest and give the female hiding spots. Use a gentle sponge filter and a snug lid so warm, humid air sits over the water."}, {"heading": "Choosing and Conditioning a Pair", "text": "Males show blue-green iridescent scales and the red crescent at the edge of the tail that gives the species its name. Females are paler, often show horizontal or vertical bars, and have a rounder belly with a visible egg spot when ready.\n\nFeed both fish live or frozen foods like brine shrimp, daphnia and bloodworms for 1 to 2 weeks. Imbellis males are much less aggressive than domestic bettas, but watch the female for torn fins and give her cover."}, {"heading": "Spawning: What You Will See", "text": "The male builds a bubble nest, often under a floating leaf or plants. When the female is ready, he leads her under the nest and wraps around her in an embrace. Eggs fall and the male gathers them in his mouth and places them in the nest.\n\nThis repeats many times over a few hours. A typical spawn is commonly reported from several dozen to around 200 eggs."}, {"heading": "Eggs and the Nest", "text": "After spawning, remove the female so the male can focus on the nest. He will pick up falling eggs and put them back.\n\nEggs usually hatch in about 24 to 48 hours at around 80°F. Hatched fry hang from the nest with yolk sacs for about 3 days. Once they swim horizontally and leave the nest, remove the male to protect them.\n\nDo not disturb the nest during these days. Bettas can eat eggs if stressed, and big water changes or bright lights near the tank are common triggers. Keep a lid or cling wrap over the tank so the surface stays calm and warm."}, {"heading": "Raising the Fry", "text": "- Week 1: infusoria or paramecium cultures several times a day\n- Week 2: add microworms and newly hatched baby brine shrimp\n- Weeks 3 to 4: mostly baby brine shrimp, plus finely crushed food\n- Keep the lid on to protect the developing labyrinth organ\n- Small water changes of 10 to 20 percent, matched for temperature, a few times a week"}, {"heading": "Growing Out", "text": "Fry grow steadily on frequent feeding. Juveniles start showing color around 6 to 8 weeks, and adult size comes at roughly 4 to 6 months.\n\nBecause imbellis males are relatively peaceful, many breeders grow them out together longer than domestic bettas, but watch for nipping and separate males if fights break out.\n\nGrow-out tanks should be at least 10 gallons for a typical spawn, ideally 20. Feed 3 to 4 times a day and do larger water changes, 25 to 30 percent weekly, once the fry are past a month old. Sort out larger fish if smaller siblings are falling behind."}, {"heading": "Common Mistakes", "text": "- Leaving the tank uncovered, which can harm fry as their labyrinth organ develops\n- Starting with baby brine shrimp, which is too large for newly free swimming fry\n- Big water changes that chill or shock the fry\n- Leaving the female in with the male after spawning\n- Hard, alkaline water that discourages spawning"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$peaceful-betta$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "How Dwarf Hairgrass Propagates", "text": "Dwarf hairgrass, Eleocharis parvula, is a small spikerush. It spreads by thin runners under or along the substrate, and new tufts of blades rise up from points along those runners. Over time the tufts link together into a carpet.\n\nWhat you propagate is really the runner network. Splitting a clump into many small tufts gives the runners lots of starting points.\n\nEleocharis parvula stays shorter than its larger cousin Eleocharis acicularis, usually just a few inches tall, which makes it popular for low carpets. Both spread the same way."}, {"heading": "What You Need", "text": "- A healthy pot or tissue culture cup of dwarf hairgrass\n- Fine grained substrate, nutrient rich or with root tabs\n- Moderate to high light\n- CO2 injection if possible\n- Long planting tweezers\n- Scissors\n\nWithout CO2 it can still spread, but expect slower, thinner growth and more risk of algae on the blades. If you do add CO2, raise it slowly, run it only while the lights are on, and watch fish and shrimp for gasping.\n\nTissue culture cups are popular because they are free of pests and algae. Potted plants work fine too but need careful cleaning before planting."}, {"heading": "Step by Step", "text": "Remove the plant from its pot and gently clean off rock wool or gel.\n\nSplit it into small tufts of a few blades each. Small tufts spread faster than big clumps.\n\nTrim the blades to about an inch if they are long, so light can reach the base.\n\nPlant each tuft with tweezers, pushing the roots in and leaving the blades above the substrate. Space tufts roughly an inch apart in a grid across the area you want to carpet.\n\nWork in a drained or half-drained tank if you can, which makes it much easier to see what you are doing. Refill slowly so the tufts are not knocked loose."}, {"heading": "Caring for New Plants", "text": "New plants often look sparse for the first couple of weeks while they root in. Keep the light on a steady schedule and do frequent water changes to limit algae while the plant establishes.\n\nOnce runners appear, usually within a few weeks in good conditions, the carpet fills in steadily. Trim the top to encourage dense, low growth, and vacuum gently above the carpet rather than through it.\n\nAmmonia from fresh nutrient substrate can trigger algae in the first weeks, and it harms fish and shrimp, so in a new soil tank wait until ammonia and nitrite read zero before adding animals. Large water changes, 30 to 50 percent a couple of times a week for the first month, help a lot. Keep the light at 6 to 8 hours a day while it settles."}, {"heading": "Common Mistakes", "text": "- Planting big clumps instead of small tufts\n- Low light and no CO2 while expecting a fast carpet\n- Loose, coarse gravel that will not hold small tufts in place\n- Fish that dig and uproot young tufts\n- Not trimming, which lets older blades shade the base and turn brown"}, {"heading": "Keeping a Hairgrass Carpet Healthy", "text": "Once established, a hairgrass carpet needs regular trimming and consistent nutrients. Feed with root tabs or a nutrient rich substrate, and keep the CO2 steady if you use it.\n\nOld carpets sometimes build up dead material underneath. Thinning and replanting sections every so often keeps the carpet fresh and gives you more tufts for trading."}, {"heading": "Without CO2", "text": "Dwarf hairgrass can be grown in low-tech tanks, but expect it to spread slowly and stay sparser. Use a nutrient rich substrate or root tabs, moderate light and patience. Trimming less often helps, since the plant needs all its blades to feed itself.\n\nIf algae starts on the blades, reduce the light period and keep up with water changes. Many low-tech growers report a decent lawn after several months."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$dwarf-hairgrass$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Cave spawner", "sexing": "Mature males grow branched bristles across the snout; females have few or none, mostly along the edge", "group": "One pair or 1 male to 2 females", "breeding_tank": "30 gallon, caves sized to fit the male snugly, driftwood, good filtration", "conditioning": "2 to 3 weeks of vegetables, algae wafers and some protein like bloodworms", "trigger": "A large, slightly cooler water change, often after a period of heavy feeding", "spawn": "Commonly 20 to 100 or more eggs, depending on the female", "eggs": "Hatch in about 4 to 7 days at around 77°F; male fans and guards them", "fry": "Stay in the cave on their yolk sacs for several days, then emerge and graze", "first_foods": "Algae wafers, blanched zucchini, cucumber and biofilm on driftwood", "grow_out": "About 1 inch by roughly 2 months; 2 inches in about 3 to 5 months", "parents": "Male guards eggs and young fry; parents rarely harm fry"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting Up the Breeding Tank", "text": "A 30 gallon tank, in line with our care data, works well for a pair or trio. Add caves that fit the male snugly, with one opening. Clay or ceramic caves are common. Add driftwood for grazing and good filtration, since plecos produce a lot of waste.\n\nThe library range is 72 to 80°F and pH 6 to 7.8. Bristlenose are adaptable and breed in a wide range of water, so you do not need to change much.\n\nMany breeders use several caves so the male can choose. A cave around 1.5 to 2 inches wide for an adult male, closed at one end, works well. Place caves in a quiet, slightly darker part of the tank."}, {"heading": "Choosing and Conditioning a Pair", "text": "Mature males have long, branched bristles across the snout. Females have few or no bristles, mostly along the edge of the head. Males are often a little larger with a broader head.\n\nCondition for 2 to 3 weeks with vegetables, algae wafers and some protein like bloodworms. A well fed female looks rounder from above.\n\nBristlenose usually mature by about 9 to 12 months. A pair with a well fed female will often spawn every few weeks once they start."}, {"heading": "Spawning: What You Will See", "text": "The male claims a cave and cleans it. When the female is ready, she joins him in the cave and lays a clump of sticky, orange eggs on the ceiling or walls.\n\nShe leaves, and the male stays to guard the eggs. Clutches commonly range from about 20 to 100 or more eggs depending on the female.\n\nYou may not see the spawning itself. Often the first sign is the female out and about again looking thinner, while the male stays tucked in the cave, fanning his tail at the entrance."}, {"heading": "Eggs and the Male's Care", "text": "The male fans the eggs with his fins and guards the cave entrance. Eggs usually hatch in about 4 to 7 days at around 77°F.\n\nAfter hatching, fry stay in the cave on their yolk sacs for several days. Avoid disturbing the cave, as a stressed male may abandon or eat eggs. A good, steady water flow helps.\n\nIf the male abandons the eggs or keeps pushing them out, some breeders hatch them in a container with gentle aeration. This is a backup plan, not the normal route."}, {"heading": "Raising the Fry", "text": "- Once they emerge, offer algae wafers and blanched zucchini or cucumber\n- Driftwood provides grazing surface and fiber\n- Feed small amounts several times a day\n- Remove leftover vegetables within a day\n- Regular water changes of 20 to 30 percent weekly\n\nFry need wood and plenty of greens. Adding a few pieces of driftwood to the grow-out tank helps with growth and digestion."}, {"heading": "Growing Out", "text": "Fry grow steadily on a vegetable based diet. They commonly reach about an inch by around 2 months and about 2 inches in roughly 3 to 5 months, depending on feeding and water changes.\n\nMove them to a grow-out tank if the breeding tank gets crowded, and keep driftwood in there too.\n\nBristlenose are prolific, so plan ahead. A good pair may spawn again every few weeks, and fry need room. Have a home for the young fish, whether through local clubs, auctions or fish stores."}, {"heading": "Common Mistakes", "text": "- Caves that are too large for the male to defend\n- Disturbing the cave while he guards eggs\n- Not enough driftwood or vegetables for the fry\n- Overcrowding and poor water quality\n- Underfeeding the female before spawning\n- Too few hiding places, which leaves the female stressed and less willing to spawn"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$bristlenose-pleco$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting Up the Breeding Tank", "text": "A 10 to 20 gallon tank works well. Use a bare bottom or fine sand, a sponge filter and a few plants.\n\nThe library range is 72 to 80°F and pH 6 to 8. Keep the water clean and well oxygenated.\n\nBronze corydoras are hardy and breed in a wide range of water. A sponge filter is best so eggs and fry are not pulled in. Some fine leaved plants or a spawning mop give the female more surfaces to choose from, though most still pick the glass."}, {"heading": "Choosing and Conditioning a Group", "text": "Females are larger and rounder, especially when viewed from above. Males are slimmer.\n\nA ratio of 2 males to 1 female works well. Condition for 1 to 2 weeks on bloodworms, brine shrimp and sinking pellets.\n\nThe Venezuelan orange is a color form of Corydoras aeneus. Breed it with the same form, not with other color forms or species, if you want true to type fry.\n\nMature fish, usually around 1.5 to 2 inches, breed best. A well conditioned female looks noticeably broader across the body when viewed from above."}, {"heading": "Spawning: What You Will See", "text": "After a large, slightly cooler water change, the males chase the female. A male positions himself across her head in the famous T-position.\n\nThe female holds eggs in her pelvic fins and then sticks them to the glass, plants or decor. This repeats over a few hours. Spawns often total dozens to a few hundred eggs.\n\nIf a spawn does not follow the water change, repeat it daily for a few days. Spawning often happens in the morning or after the water change, and a group can spawn every week or two once they get going."}, {"heading": "Eggs and Fungus Prevention", "text": "Adults will eat eggs, so move the eggs or the adults after spawning. Eggs can be rolled off the glass gently with a finger or razor blade.\n\nEggs usually hatch in about 3 to 5 days around 75°F. Remove fungused eggs promptly and keep gentle aeration nearby. Some breeders use a small amount of methylene blue in a separate hatching container.\n\nEggs are about the size of a pinhead, clear at first and then darken. Unfertilized eggs turn white within a day or two. A small hatching container with an airstone, kept in the main tank to stay at the same temperature, works well."}, {"heading": "Raising the Fry", "text": "- Days 1 to 3: fry live on their yolk sacs\n- Days 3 to 10: microworms and crushed sinking foods\n- From about week 2: baby brine shrimp\n- Keep the bottom clean to prevent bacterial problems\n- Small water changes of 10 to 20 percent every few days\n\nCory fry are sensitive to dirty substrate. Siphon uneaten food daily and avoid deep gravel in the fry tank."}, {"heading": "Growing Out", "text": "Fry grow steadily on frequent small feedings. They reach about an inch by 2 to 3 months.\n\nA bare bottom tank makes it easier to keep clean during grow-out.\n\nA 20 gallon grow-out tank with weekly 25 to 30 percent water changes works well. Feed 3 to 4 times a day with crushed pellets and baby brine shrimp. Raise them in a group, since corydoras are social.\n\nThe orange coloring often shows within the first few weeks and deepens as the fry grow. Juveniles can join a community tank once they are too large to be eaten, usually around an inch."}, {"heading": "Common Mistakes", "text": "- Leaving eggs with the adults\n- Not removing fungused eggs quickly\n- Dirty bottoms that harm the fry\n- Overfeeding\n- Skipping the cool water change trigger"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$venezuelan-orange-cory$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 10 to 15 gallon tank is enough. Serpaes spawn best in soft, slightly acidic water. The library range runs from pH 6 to 7.5, and for spawning most breeders aim for about pH 6 to 6.5 and low hardness, using RO or rain water mixed with tap water. Set the heater to 77 to 80°F.\n\nKeep the light dim: cover the sides with paper or use floating plants. Add fine leaved plants, java moss or spawning mops, and cover the bottom with mesh or marbles so eggs fall out of reach. A small sponge filter on low flow is all you need."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a pair", "text": "Sexing serpaes takes a careful look. Males are slimmer and usually show deeper red color. Females are rounder and often a little paler, and a ripe female is clearly full through the belly. Comparing fish side by side in a group is the easiest way.\n\nSeparate the sexes for 1 to 2 weeks and feed well with frozen bloodworms, brine shrimp, daphnia and a good flake. Put the best pair, or two males with one female, into the spawning tank in the evening."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "Spawning usually happens the next morning. The male displays and drives the female into the plants, and the pair press together and scatter eggs in short bursts. The session can last a couple of hours.\n\nRemove the adults as soon as the female looks slim. If nothing happens after 2 or 3 days, a small change with soft, slightly cooler water often triggers them. If they still will not spawn, condition them for another week and check that the water is soft enough."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "Eggs hatch in about 24 to 48 hours at 77 to 80°F. Serpae eggs are sensitive to light, so keep the tank dim for the first few days. Infertile eggs turn white and can grow fungus. Remove them with a pipette when you can, keep gentle aeration running, and avoid large water changes during this stage.\n\nAfter hatching the fry hang on the glass and plants for several days while they absorb the yolk."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "The fry swim freely about 3 to 5 days after hatching and are very small.\n\n- Week 1: infusoria, green water or a fine liquid or powdered fry food several times a day.\n- Week 2: microworms and newly hatched brine shrimp.\n- Weeks 3 to 6: brine shrimp, crushed flake and small granules.\n\nStart small water changes of 10 to 20 percent every day or two from the second week, using water matched in temperature and softness. Sudden jumps in hardness or pH are a common cause of losses. Keep the light gentle for the first couple of weeks."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry grow steadily with frequent meals. Expect about half an inch at 6 weeks and a sellable 1 inch at roughly 3 to 4 months. Red color develops gradually over the first couple of months. As they grow, you can slowly shift them toward your normal tap water. Keep them in a good sized group with plenty of space, which helps reduce the nipping serpaes are known for."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Water that is too hard. Eggs may not develop well.\n- Bright light on the eggs and fry.\n- Leaving the adults in the tank after spawning.\n- Offering brine shrimp before the fry can eat it.\n- Changing large amounts of water with a different pH or hardness."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$serpae-tetra$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 5 to 10 gallon tank is plenty for a breeding group. The most important feature is a fine sand bottom at least 1 to 2 inches deep. Baby assassin snails spend their early life buried, and coarse gravel makes this much harder for them.\n\nKeep the water warm and hard, matching the library range of 72 to 82°F, pH 7 to 8 and GH 8 to 20. Calcium is important for shell growth, so do not use soft RO water without remineralizing it. Add rocks, wood or glass surfaces for egg laying and use a sponge filter. Avoid fish that pick at snails, along with any loaches or puffers."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a group", "text": "Assassin snails are not hermaphrodites. Each snail is male or female, and there is no reliable way to tell them apart by looking. The simplest approach is to keep a group of five or more adults, which almost always includes both sexes.\n\nFeed them well. Their natural diet is other snails, so add a steady supply of small pond, bladder or ramshorn snails from a separate culture. They also take sinking protein foods such as bloodworms, shrimp pellets and fish food pellets. Well fed adults breed far more often than hungry ones."}, {"heading": "Mating and egg laying", "text": "Mating pairs lock together, one riding on the other's shell, and can stay that way for several hours. Do not separate them.\n\nAfter mating, females lay small, clear, roughly square capsules on hard surfaces such as glass, rocks, wood and filters. Each capsule holds a single egg, and you can see a small yellow dot inside. Expect only a few capsules at a time, spread out over weeks. This slow pace is why assassin snails never overrun a tank."}, {"heading": "Eggs and development", "text": "Development is slow and depends on temperature. Breeders commonly report hatching in about 3 to 6 weeks, quicker at the warmer end of the range. You can watch the dot inside each capsule grow and take shape.\n\nLeave the capsules where they are. They are tough and rarely bothered by other snails. Avoid scraping the glass where they sit, and keep water parameters steady."}, {"heading": "Raising the babies", "text": "Newly hatched snails are tiny and dig straight into the sand. You may not see them again for weeks or months. They feed on detritus, leftover protein food and very small snails.\n\n- Keep feeding the tank lightly with sinking protein foods even when no adults are hungry.\n- Keep small prey snails in the tank so their tiny babies are available as food.\n- Do not vacuum the sand deeply; skim only the surface.\n- Do small, regular water changes and keep calcium up.\n\nThe juveniles usually reappear when they are around a quarter inch long, often months after hatching."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Growth is slow compared to most snails. Juveniles are commonly reported to take several months to emerge and longer to reach adult size of nearly an inch. Their banded shells are visible once they surface. They are ready to rehome once they are actively hunting and around half an inch. Count your young carefully before selling, since many may still be buried."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Gravel instead of fine sand, which makes it hard for babies to survive.\n- Soft water with little calcium, leading to weak shells.\n- Deep vacuuming that pulls out hidden babies.\n- Underfeeding, so adults stop laying.\n- Giving up too soon. Babies can stay out of sight for months.\n- Copper based medicines or plant products, which kill snails."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$assassin-snail$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Egg carrier: female carries eggs under her tail (berried)", "sexing": "Males have hard, forward-pointing first swimmerets (gonopods); females have a round sperm receptacle", "group": "One male and one female, paired only for mating", "breeding_tank": "20 gallons or more per adult, many caves and pipes, secure lid", "conditioning": "Varied sinking foods, vegetables and calcium; stable water", "trigger": "Introducing a mature male to a mature female after she molts", "spawn": "Commonly reported 30 to 150 eggs, more from large females", "eggs": "Carried about 3 to 5 weeks, depending on temperature", "fry": "Juveniles cling to the mother for 1 to 2 weeks before walking off", "first_foods": "Crushed sinking pellets, blanched vegetables, leaf litter and biofilm", "grow_out": "About 2 to 3 months to an inch, faster in warmer water", "parents": "Mother protects eggs; remove her once young leave or she may eat them"}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$electric-blue-crayfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Egg carrier: female carries eggs, which hatch into free-swimming larvae", "sexing": "Females larger with a deeper underside and green eggs when ripe; males smaller and slimmer", "group": "A group of 5 to 10 with both sexes", "breeding_tank": "5 to 10 gallons, sponge filter, plants and hides", "conditioning": "Varied sinking foods, algae wafers and blanched vegetables", "trigger": "Mature, well fed females in a stable tank with males", "spawn": "Commonly about 20 to 50 eggs", "eggs": "Carried about 2 to 3 weeks at 72 to 78°F", "fry": "Hatch as tiny larvae that swim in open water; commonly reported 1 to 3 weeks before adult form", "first_foods": "Infusoria, green water and very fine powdered food", "grow_out": "About 2 to 3 months to an inch", "parents": "Adults eat larvae; move berried females to a larvae tank before hatching"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Keep a group of 5 to 10 ghost shrimp in a 5 to 10 gallon tank with a sponge filter, plants and hides. Water inside the library range works well: 72 to 78°F for breeding, pH 7 to 8 and GH 5 to 20.\n\nSet up a second small tank, around 2 to 5 gallons, for the larvae. Use an air stone or a sponge filter on very low flow, no gravel, some java moss and the same water as the main tank. Let it age a couple of weeks to grow some biofilm."}, {"heading": "Sexing and conditioning", "text": "Females are larger, with a deeper underside where they carry eggs. Mature females often show a green saddle of developing eggs in the body. Males are smaller and slimmer.\n\nFeed a varied diet: sinking pellets, algae wafers, blanched spinach or zucchini and frozen foods. Well fed females in stable water breed regularly.\n\nBe aware that shrimp sold as ghost shrimp are sometimes mixed species. Some types need brackish water for larvae. If your larvae fail despite good care, that may be the cause."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "After mating, the female carries her eggs under her tail, attached to her swimmerets. They are small, green and easy to see through her clear body. She fans them often.\n\nThe eggs are carried for about 2 to 3 weeks. When you see tiny dark eyes in the eggs, move the female to the larvae tank. Use a cup, not a net, to reduce the chance she drops her eggs."}, {"heading": "Hatching and larvae", "text": "The eggs hatch into tiny swimming larvae that look nothing like adults. They drift and swim in the open water. Return the female to the main tank once the eggs are gone, since adults eat larvae.\n\nThe larvae go through several stages before taking adult form, which breeders commonly report takes about 1 to 3 weeks, depending on temperature. During this time, they cannot handle filter intakes and need very fine food."}, {"heading": "Raising the larvae", "text": "- Days 1 to 7: infusoria, green water and very fine powdered fry food several times a day in small amounts.\n- Week 2: continue fine foods; add crushed flake once they settle and look like small shrimp.\n- Weeks 3 to 6: crushed pellets, algae wafers and blanched vegetables.\n\nKeep the water clean with small changes of 5 to 10 percent a few times a week, dripped in slowly. Keep the temperature steady at 72 to 78°F. Once they look like tiny adults, they can live on the bottom and graze biofilm."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Young shrimp grow quickly once past the larval stage. Expect about an inch in 2 to 3 months. Keep them in the larvae tank until they are big enough not to be eaten by fish, then move them to a grow-out tank or back with the adults.\n\nSurvival from larvae is often low on the first attempts, so do not be discouraged if only a handful make it. Each batch teaches you how much fine food to give and how gentle the flow must be. A berried female usually comes along again within a few weeks, so you will get plenty of practice."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Leaving larvae in the main tank, where adults and fish eat them.\n- Strong filters that suck up larvae.\n- No fine food, so larvae starve.\n- Moving berried females with a net.\n- Not knowing your shrimp species, since some need brackish water for larvae.\n- Copper in medications or plant products, which kills shrimp and larvae."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$ghost-shrimp$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Egg carrier: female carries eggs under her tail (berried)", "sexing": "Males have hard, forward-pointing gonopods and longer claws; females have a wider tail", "group": "1 male to 2 females, or a small colony in a larger tank", "breeding_tank": "10 gallons or more, many hides, moss, sponge filter, tight lid", "conditioning": "Sinking pellets, blanched vegetables, leaf litter and calcium", "trigger": "Mature adults in stable water; mating often follows a molt", "spawn": "Commonly about 20 to 60 eggs", "eggs": "Carried about 3 to 4 weeks at room temperature", "fry": "Juveniles cling to the mother for several days before walking off", "first_foods": "Biofilm, crushed sinking pellets, blanched vegetables and leaf litter", "grow_out": "About 2 to 3 months to half an inch; adult at around 4 to 6 months", "parents": "Mother protects eggs; adults may eat young, so offer plenty of cover"}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$dwarf-mexican-crayfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Non-annual killifish: egg depositor in mops or fine plants", "sexing": "Males colorful with red dots and bright fin edges; females plain brown with small dots", "group": "1 male to 2 or 3 females", "breeding_tank": "5 to 10 gallons, spawning mop, sponge filter, tight lid", "conditioning": "Live and frozen foods for 1 to 2 weeks: bloodworms, brine shrimp, daphnia", "trigger": "Conditioned fish with a mop; they spawn a little most days", "spawn": "Commonly about 10 to 30 eggs a day", "eggs": "Hatch in about 14 to 21 days in water at 72 to 75°F, or on damp peat", "fry": "Free swimming right after hatching; large for killifish fry", "first_foods": "Newly hatched brine shrimp or microworms", "grow_out": "About 2 to 3 months to adult color and size", "parents": "No care; adults eat eggs and fry, so pick eggs daily"}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$gardneri-killifish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Egg depositor (non-annual killifish): lays eggs among floating plants or mops near the surface", "sexing": "Males have a red to orange throat and stronger dark bars; females plainer with clear fins", "group": "1 male to 2 or 3 females", "breeding_tank": "5 to 10 gallons, floating plants or a floating mop, sponge filter, tight lid", "conditioning": "Live and frozen foods such as fruit flies, brine shrimp and bloodworms", "trigger": "Conditioned adults with floating plants; they spawn a little most days", "spawn": "A few eggs a day over many days, not one big batch", "eggs": "Commonly reported to hatch in about 8 to 14 days at around 75°F", "fry": "Free swimming at hatching; stay near the surface", "first_foods": "Infusoria or vinegar eels, then newly hatched brine shrimp within days", "grow_out": "About 2 to 3 months to adult color", "parents": "No care; adults eat eggs and small fry, so remove eggs or fry"}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$dageti-killifish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Egg depositor: female carries eggs briefly, then deposits them on plants", "sexing": "Males brighter with red-edged fins and longer dorsal and anal fins; females plainer and rounder", "group": "1 male to 2 or 3 females", "breeding_tank": "5 to 10 gallons, fine plants or moss, sponge filter", "conditioning": "Small live and frozen foods plus quality flake", "trigger": "Conditioned group in stable water; spawns most mornings", "spawn": "Commonly about 10 to 30 eggs at a time", "eggs": "Commonly reported to hatch in about 1 to 2 weeks at 75 to 80°F", "fry": "Free swimming at hatching; very small", "first_foods": "Infusoria or fine powdered food, then newly hatched brine shrimp", "grow_out": "About 2 to 3 months to adult color", "parents": "No care; adults eat eggs and fry, so collect eggs or plants"}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$daisys-ricefish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Non-annual killifish: egg depositor in mops or fine plants", "sexing": "Males colorful with lyre-shaped tails; females plain brown with rounded fins", "group": "1 male to 2 or 3 females", "breeding_tank": "5 to 10 gallons, spawning mop, sponge filter, tight lid", "conditioning": "Live and frozen foods for 1 to 2 weeks", "trigger": "Conditioned fish with a mop; they spawn most days", "spawn": "Commonly about 10 to 20 eggs a day", "eggs": "Hatch in about 14 to 21 days in water at 72 to 75°F, or on damp peat", "fry": "Free swimming right after hatching", "first_foods": "Newly hatched brine shrimp or microworms", "grow_out": "About 2 to 3 months to adult color", "parents": "No care; adults eat eggs and fry, so pick eggs daily"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 5 to 10 gallon tank is plenty for one male and two or three females. Use a sponge filter on gentle flow, a heater set to about 72 to 75°F and one or two spawning mops, either floating or weighted on the bottom. A bare bottom or a thin layer of sand makes it much easier to see and collect eggs.\n\nThe library range of pH 6 to 7 and GH 2 to 12 suits breeding. Many breeders use soft, slightly acidic water around pH 6 to 6.5, mixing RO or rain water with tap water, and some add a little peat or leaf litter to soften and tint it. Keep a tight lid on the tank, because lyretails are strong jumpers. Moderate light and a few floating plants help them settle and show color."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a group", "text": "Sexing is easy. Males are colorful, usually orange to chocolate with red dots, and have the lyre-shaped tail with bright edges and extended tips that gives them their name. Females are plain brown with a few small dots and short, rounded fins.\n\nKeep one male with two or three females so no single female is chased too hard. Condition for 1 to 2 weeks on live and frozen foods such as bloodworms, brine shrimp, daphnia and white worms. They will take flake, but well fed fish on meaty foods lay more eggs, and the eggs hatch better. Keep only one male per small tank, since rival males fight."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "Once settled, lyretails spawn most days. The male spreads his fins and shimmies beside a female, then leads her into the mop. The pair press together side by side and she lays one or two eggs at a time, deep among the fibers. Over a day, a female commonly lays 10 to 20 eggs.\n\nCheck the mop every day or two. Eggs are small, clear and surprisingly firm, so you can pick them out with your fingers. Fertile eggs stay clear, while infertile eggs turn white within a day or two."}, {"heading": "Incubating the eggs", "text": "Killifish keepers use two methods with this species.\n\n- Water: place eggs in a small container of tank water with a drop or two of methylene blue. Remove white eggs daily so fungus does not spread. At 72 to 75°F, eggs hatch in about 14 to 21 days.\n- Peat: store eggs on damp, not wet, peat in a sealed container at room temperature for about 2 to 3 weeks, then add water once you can see eyes in the eggs.\n\nIn water, a batch may hatch over several days. Check every day and move new fry to a small growing tank so they are not left without food."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Lyretail fry are free swimming as soon as they hatch and are large enough to take newly hatched brine shrimp or microworms right away.\n\n- Weeks 1 to 2: newly hatched brine shrimp or microworms two or three times a day.\n- Weeks 3 to 4: add crushed frozen foods and fine flake.\n- Weeks 5 to 8: frozen bloodworms, brine shrimp and small pellets.\n\nChange 10 to 20 percent of the water twice a week, matching temperature and softness carefully. A shallow fry tank of a few inches of water makes it easier for fry to find food. Sort fry by size every couple of weeks, since bigger fry may eat smaller siblings."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "With good feeding, males start showing color and tail extensions at about 6 to 8 weeks, and the young reach adult color at around 2 to 3 months. Separate young males if they begin to fight. Lyretails are not long lived, so keep a few pairs from each generation to continue your line. Keep each population labeled with its name or locality and never mix two different strains."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Leaving eggs in the mop for days, where the adults eat them.\n- Hard, alkaline water that lowers fertility and hatch rates.\n- No lid, so adults jump out.\n- Not sorting fry by size.\n- Mixing populations or strains, which ruins clean lines that other keepers rely on."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$lyretail-killifish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Non-annual killifish: egg depositor in mops or fine plants", "sexing": "Males have red stripes on a blue-green body; females plain brown", "group": "1 male to 2 or 3 females", "breeding_tank": "5 to 10 gallons, spawning mop, sponge filter, tight lid", "conditioning": "Live and frozen foods for 1 to 2 weeks", "trigger": "Cool, soft water and conditioned fish with a mop", "spawn": "Commonly about 10 to 20 eggs a day", "eggs": "Hatch in about 2 to 3 weeks in water at 68 to 72°F, or on damp peat", "fry": "Free swimming right after hatching", "first_foods": "Newly hatched brine shrimp or microworms", "grow_out": "About 2 to 3 months to adult color", "parents": "No care; adults eat eggs and fry, so pick eggs daily"}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$striatum-killifish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Livebearer", "sexing": "Males have a gonopodium and a sword extension on the lower tail; females have neither", "group": "1 male to 2 or 3 females", "breeding_tank": "30 gallons or more, plants or a separate nursery tank", "conditioning": "2 to 3 weeks of quality flake plus frozen and vegetable foods", "trigger": "Mature, healthy fish breed on their own; females store sperm", "spawn": "Commonly 20 to 80 fry, large females well over 100", "eggs": "Gestation about 4 to 6 weeks at 75 to 80°F", "fry": "Born large and swimming, ready to eat at once", "first_foods": "Crushed flake and baby brine shrimp", "grow_out": "1 inch in about 2 to 3 months; males show swords from about 3 months", "parents": "Adults eat fry; plant heavily or move the female before birth"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting Up the Breeding Tank", "text": "Swordtails are active swimmers, so use at least 30 gallons for a breeding group. Match the site's care numbers: pH 7.0 to 8.4, GH 12 to 30, and 75 to 80°F for breeding. Good filtration is important, and swordtails are known jumpers.\n\nPlant one end heavily with floating plants, hornwort or Java moss for fry cover, or keep a 10 gallon nursery tank with a sponge filter ready for the births.\n\nLet the tank cycle fully before adding fish, and keep a mature sponge filter running in a spare tank so you can set up a nursery at short notice. A tight lid stops adults jumping."}, {"heading": "Choosing and Conditioning a Breeding Group", "text": "Mature males are easy to sex: they have a gonopodium and the long sword on the bottom of the tail. Females are larger bodied with a fan-shaped anal fin and no sword. Young males can develop late, so a fish without a sword is not always a female until you check the anal fin.\n\nKeep 1 male to 2 or 3 females, because males chase hard and can fight each other. Condition for 2 to 3 weeks with flake, frozen bloodworms or brine shrimp and some vegetable flake.\n\nKeep up weekly water changes of 25 to 30 percent while conditioning. Clean, stable water and a varied diet give bigger, healthier broods."}, {"heading": "Mating and Pregnancy", "text": "The male swims in front of the female with his sword showing, then darts in to mate with the gonopodium. Females store sperm, so they can produce several broods from a single mating.\n\nA pregnant female grows deep and boxy, and the dark gravid spot near the vent becomes larger and darker as birth gets close."}, {"heading": "Gestation and Birth", "text": "Gestation is about 4 to 6 weeks at 75 to 80°F. Broods are commonly 20 to 80, and big, mature females can drop well over 100.\n\nMove the female a few days ahead to a heavily planted nursery tank, or use plenty of floating plants in the main tank. Small breeding boxes stress swordtails, so keep any time in them short. Return the female once she has finished giving birth."}, {"heading": "Raising the Fry", "text": "The fry are large and start eating right away.\n\n- Week 1: crushed flake and baby brine shrimp, four or five times a day.\n- Weeks 2 to 4: add microworms, crushed frozen foods and vegetable flake.\n- Change 25 to 30 percent of the water two or three times a week.\n- Keep 76 to 80°F for steady growth.\n\nFeed small amounts often and siphon any leftovers, because uneaten food fouls a fry tank quickly. Fry that are fed well in the first month grow into bigger, healthier adults, while stunted fry rarely catch up."}, {"heading": "Growing Out", "text": "Fry reach about 1 inch in 2 to 3 months. Some males show swords from around 3 months, but others take much longer, and these late developers often become the largest males. Separate males and females once you can sex them if you want control over pairings. Keep the best shaped fish with the strongest color as your next breeders.\n\nThin out crowded tanks as the young grow, and keep notes of birth dates and which parents produced each brood. Those records help you pick breeders and plan future broods."}, {"heading": "Common Mistakes", "text": "- Too many males in a small tank, leading to fighting.\n- Leaving fry with adults in a bare tank.\n- Culling swordless fish too early, when some are late males.\n- No lid on the tank.\n- Selling platy crosses as pure swordtails.\n- Releasing unwanted fish outdoors. Swordtails have become invasive in warm waters in several countries."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$swordtail$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Substrate spawner (flat stone, both parents guard)", "sexing": "Males larger with longer, pointed fins and more blue spangling; check breeding tubes at spawning", "group": "One pair, ideally formed from a group of juveniles", "breeding_tank": "75 gallons or more, sand, flat stones, rockwork", "conditioning": "2 to 3 weeks of quality pellets, frozen krill, mysis and bloodworms", "trigger": "A large water change and a cleaned flat stone", "spawn": "Commonly 500 to 800 eggs, sometimes over 1,000", "eggs": "Hatch in about 2 to 4 days at 78 to 82°F", "fry": "Free swimming about 4 to 6 days after hatching", "first_foods": "Baby brine shrimp", "grow_out": "About 1 inch in 6 to 8 weeks", "parents": "Devoted parents; watch for the male turning on the female"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting Up the Breeding Tank", "text": "A breeding pair needs at least 75 gallons, matching the site's minimum, and more room helps the female escape if the male gets rough. Use sand, a large flat stone or slate, and rock caves that create separate territories.\n\nKeep pH 7.0 to 7.5, GH 8 to 15 and 78 to 82°F for breeding, all inside the site's ranges. Use strong filtration, since this is a messy, heavily fed species.\n\nSet the tank up and let it run for a few weeks before adding the fish, so the filter is fully cycled. Fry tanks get a lot of food, and a mature sponge filter handles that load far better than a new one. A heater guard and a tight lid are worth adding too."}, {"heading": "Choosing and Conditioning a Pair", "text": "Adult males are usually larger, with longer and more pointed dorsal and anal fins and more blue and green spangles. Females are smaller, with shorter fins and usually less spangling. The breeding tubes at spawning time are the surest check.\n\nPairs are safest formed by growing 5 or 6 juveniles together, then removing the rest once two pair off. Condition for 2 to 3 weeks with pellets, frozen krill, mysis and bloodworms.\n\nFeed small amounts two or three times a day rather than one large meal, and keep up water changes of 25 to 30 percent weekly while conditioning. Clean, stable water does as much to bring fish into spawning condition as rich food."}, {"heading": "Spawning", "text": "A ready pair darkens almost to black with bright spangles, digs pits and cleans a flat stone. The female lays rows of eggs and the male fertilizes them. A large water change often sets them off.\n\nClutches are commonly 500 to 800 eggs and can exceed 1,000. Both parents defend the site and will charge your hand."}, {"heading": "Eggs and Fungus", "text": "Eggs hatch in about 2 to 4 days at 78 to 82°F. The parents move the wrigglers to pits, and the fry become free swimming roughly 4 to 6 days after hatching.\n\nThe parents remove fungused eggs. To hatch artificially, move the stone to a tank with matching water, an airstone beside it and a little methylene blue."}, {"heading": "Raising the Fry", "text": "- Week 1: newly hatched baby brine shrimp, three or four times a day.\n- Weeks 2 to 3: baby brine shrimp plus crushed flake.\n- Week 4 onward: crushed pellets and frozen foods.\n- Change 30 percent of the water twice a week, as fry produce a lot of waste.\n- Keep 78 to 80°F.\n\nHatch baby brine shrimp daily so fresh live food is always on hand; fry that miss meals in the first week rarely catch up. Feed only what they clear in a few minutes and siphon leftovers, because uneaten food fouls small tanks quickly. Orange bellies after feeding mean the fry are eating well."}, {"heading": "Growing Out", "text": "Fry grow quickly to about 1 inch in 6 to 8 weeks. With hundreds of fry, you will need grow out space fast. Split them into larger tanks to avoid stunting and aggression. Juveniles sell best at 1.5 to 2 inches.\n\nSeparate fry by size if some race ahead, since bigger siblings can outcompete or bully smaller ones. Keep notes of spawn dates, hatch dates and growth; they make future spawns easier to plan."}, {"heading": "Common Mistakes", "text": "- Putting two random adults together, which can end in injury.\n- A tank too small for the female to escape.\n- Not planning grow out space for hundreds of fry.\n- Hands in the tank during guarding, which can lead to bites.\n- Crossing with other cichlids. Never release fish or fry outdoors."}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"q": "How many eggs do Jack Dempseys lay?", "a": "Clutches are commonly 500 to 800 eggs and can exceed 1,000 from large females."}, {"q": "How long do Jack Dempsey eggs take to hatch?", "a": "About 2 to 4 days at 78 to 82°F, with free swimming fry about 4 to 6 days later."}, {"q": "Do Jack Dempseys protect their fry?", "a": "Yes. Both parents guard the eggs and fry closely and will attack tankmates and even your hand."}, {"q": "What size tank do Jack Dempseys need to breed?", "a": "At least 75 gallons for a pair, and more room helps if the male becomes aggressive."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$jack-dempsey$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set intro = $g$Acei are one of the easiest Lake Malawi mouthbrooders to breed, and one of the calmer ones, so a harem in a 55 gallon tank often spawns with little help from you. The real work is sexing them, since males and females look alike, and keeping a holding female stress free for about three weeks. Seeing a female spit out a cloud of perfect miniature acei never gets old.$g$, facts = $g${"method": "Maternal mouthbrooder (female holds eggs and fry)", "sexing": "Hard by color; males grow larger with longer fins and more egg spots. Venting is most reliable", "group": "1 male to 3 or 4 females", "breeding_tank": "55 gallons or more, rockwork caves, open swimming space, hard alkaline water", "conditioning": "2 to 3 weeks of spirulina flake and pellets, small amounts of protein", "trigger": "Stable water at 78 to 80°F, enough females to spread out the male's attention", "spawn": "Commonly 10 to 30 eggs, larger females can hold more", "eggs": "Held about 21 to 28 days at 78 to 80°F", "fry": "Released fully formed, about 3/8 inch, free swimming and feeding at once", "first_foods": "Baby brine shrimp, crushed spirulina flake, fine fry pellets", "grow_out": "About 1 inch in 2 to 3 months; colored and sellable around 1.5 to 2 inches", "parents": "No care after release; adults eat fry, so move the holding female or provide dense rock"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 55 gallon or larger tank suits a harem of acei. They grow to about 6 inches and like to swim in the open, so give them length and stack rockwork along the back with plenty of caves and gaps. Sand or fine gravel is fine.\n\nKeep the water like the rest of your Malawi tanks: 76 to 82°F, pH 7.8 to 8.6 and hard water with a GH of 10 to 25. For breeding, aim for the middle, around 78 to 80°F and pH 8.0 to 8.4. Strong filtration and weekly 30 to 50 percent water changes matter more than anything else here."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a harem", "text": "Males and females look almost identical, which is the main hurdle. Males usually grow larger, have longer pelvic and dorsal fins and show more and brighter egg spots on the anal fin, but none of that is foolproof. Venting a calm, netted fish is the most reliable method, or buy a group of 6 to 10 juveniles and let nature sort it out.\n\nThe best ratio is 1 male to 3 or 4 females. Feed a spirulina based flake or pellet two or three times a day in small amounts for 2 to 3 weeks. Acei are herbivores, so keep high protein foods as an occasional treat to avoid bloat."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "A ready male darkens and shakes in front of a female, leading her to a flat rock or cave. The pair circle, she lays a few eggs, picks them up in her mouth and nips at his egg spots, which helps fertilize them.\n\nThe giveaway afterward is a female with a swollen, boxy throat who refuses food and hides. Note the date so you can plan the release."}, {"heading": "Holding time", "text": "Females commonly hold for about 21 to 28 days at 78 to 80°F. You have two choices:\n\n- Leave her in the main tank if there is plenty of rockwork and the male is not harassing her. Some fry survive in the rocks, but many get eaten.\n- Move her gently to a 10 to 20 gallon tank with the same water about a week into holding. Moving her too early is the most common cause of spitting.\n\nSome breeders strip the fry by hand once they are well developed. If you do, use clean, matched water and be gentle. Most beginners do better letting her release naturally."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry come out about 3/8 inch long and eat right away. Week 1: baby brine shrimp and finely crushed spirulina flake twice a day. Weeks 2 to 4: add fry pellets and keep up the brine shrimp. Hold the fry tank at 78 to 80°F and change 25 to 50 percent of the water two or three times a week with matched water. A sponge filter is ideal.\n\nReturn the female to the harem after a few days of rest and good food so she can regain condition."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Expect fry to reach about 1 inch in 2 to 3 months and 1.5 to 2 inches in 4 to 5 months with good feeding and frequent water changes. Move them to a larger grow out tank as they grow, since crowding slows growth and raises aggression. Shift the diet toward spirulina pellets as they get bigger.\n\nYoung males start to show longer fins and stronger egg spots at around 2 inches, which is a good time to start sorting them if you plan to sell or trade groups."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Buying adults without venting and ending up with all males or all females.\n- Moving a holding female in the first few days, which causes her to spit the eggs.\n- Feeding rich protein foods every day and causing bloat.\n- Overcrowding fry and skipping water changes, which stunts growth."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$acei-cichlid$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Maternal mouthbrooder", "sexing": "Easy in adults: males go dark brown to black with gold stripes, females stay yellow", "group": "1 male to 4 or more females", "breeding_tank": "55 gallons or more, lots of rock caves, hard alkaline water", "conditioning": "2 to 3 weeks of quality flake and pellets with spirulina and some protein", "trigger": "Stable water at 78 to 80°F, plenty of females and rock cover", "spawn": "Commonly 15 to 30 eggs", "eggs": "Held about 3 weeks (around 21 days) at 78 to 80°F", "fry": "Released free swimming, about 3/8 inch, striped yellow like the females", "first_foods": "Baby brine shrimp, crushed flake and fine fry pellets", "grow_out": "About 1 inch in 2 to 3 months", "parents": "No care after release; adults eat fry, so isolate the holding female"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Auratus are small, about 4.5 inches, but very aggressive. A 55 gallon tank is the minimum the site lists, and bigger is better. Stack rocks into many caves so females can break line of sight. Sand or fine gravel and strong filtration finish it.\n\nKeep the water at 76 to 82°F, pH 7.8 to 8.6 and GH 10 to 25, aiming for about 78 to 80°F for breeding. Change 30 to 50 percent of the water weekly.\n\nIf you can, use a dedicated species tank. A busy mixed mbuna tank adds stress, raises the odds of holding females being harassed and makes it easier for the male to cross with a similar looking female of another species."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a harem", "text": "Adult sexing is simple. Dominant males change from yellow to dark brown or black with yellow to gold stripes. Females and young fish stay yellow with black and white stripes. Note that a subordinate male may stay in female coloring, so do not assume every yellow fish is female.\n\nKeep only one male, with at least 4 females. Feed an omnivore flake or pellet with spirulina, plus occasional small portions of brine shrimp or mysis, twice daily for 2 to 3 weeks. Females that are well fed and not constantly chased fill out with eggs faster and hold more reliably.\n\nFemales can be ready to spawn at around 2 to 2.5 inches, so a group of young fish often starts breeding sooner than you expect."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "The male darkens even more, shakes and leads a female to a flat spot in his territory. She lays a few eggs at a time and picks them up in her mouth, then nips at his anal fin to fertilize them. Spawning can last an hour or more.\n\nAfterward the female will have a full throat, stop eating and hide in the rocks."}, {"heading": "Holding time", "text": "Females hold for about 3 weeks at 78 to 80°F. Auratus males often harass holding females, so most breeders move her after about a week to a 10 to 20 gallon tank with matched water and a cave. Net her gently and avoid chasing her around the tank, or she may spit the eggs.\n\nSome breeders strip well developed fry by hand. For beginners, letting her release naturally in her own tank is safer."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry are released free swimming at about 3/8 inch. Remove the female once she releases them and give her a week or so to recover before returning her to the harem.\n\nWeek 1: baby brine shrimp and finely crushed flake twice a day. Weeks 2 to 4: add fry pellets. Keep them at 78 to 80°F with a sponge filter and change 25 to 50 percent of the water two or three times a week."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry reach about 1 inch in 2 to 3 months. Even young auratus squabble, so give them space and rockwork as they grow. Young males start turning dark at around 2 to 3 inches, which is also when they start fighting in earnest.\n\nFeed the fry three or four small meals a day if you can, and keep up the frequent water changes. Sort out obvious males as they color, since a single growing male will start bullying his tankmates. Groups of young fish are easiest to rehome or sell at about 1.5 to 2 inches, before males fully color and turn on each other."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Keeping two males, or one male with only one or two females.\n- Leaving a holding female with a harassing male.\n- Mixing auratus with similar mbuna, which can lead to hybrids. Breed them in a species tank.\n- Assuming a yellow fish is a female; young males stay yellow for a while."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$auratus-cichlid$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 30 gallon tank works for one pair, and a 40 gallon breeder or larger gives the colony room to grow. Build stable rock piles with many small caves and crevices on a sand bottom. Add a few extra caves away from the main one, because older fry and young adults need places to retreat.\n\nKeep the water at 75 to 81°F, pH 7.8 to 8.6 and GH 10 to 25, as listed in the site's care data. For breeding, about 78 to 80°F and pH 8.5 works well. Tanganyikans like very clean, stable water, so change 20 to 30 percent weekly rather than doing large, sudden changes."}, {"heading": "Getting a pair", "text": "Brichardi are among the hardest cichlids to sex by eye. Males are often a little larger and may grow longer streamers on the tail and fins, but females can look the same. Venting is possible but takes practice on small fish.\n\nThe easy route is to raise a group of 6 juveniles together. When a pair forms, they claim a cave and chase the others. Remove the extra fish quickly, because a bonded pair can kill tankmates in a confined space.\n\nFeed small meals of frozen brine shrimp, mysis, daphnia and a good flake or small pellet twice a day."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "Spawning happens out of sight, usually on the ceiling or wall of a cave. You may notice the female spending all her time inside and the male patrolling the entrance more fiercely than usual. A clutch is commonly dozens of eggs, up to about 200 from a large female.\n\nDo not move rocks to look. The pair will usually handle everything."}, {"heading": "Eggs and the first days", "text": "The eggs hatch in about 2 to 3 days at 78 to 80°F. The wrigglers stay in the cave while they absorb their yolk, and you will first see tiny fry at the cave mouth roughly 7 to 10 days after spawning.\n\nFungus is rarely a problem with brichardi, since the female cleans the eggs. If eggs keep disappearing, it is usually a young pair or a stressed one, and they often get it right after a few tries."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Brichardi fry are best raised with their parents. Week 1: baby brine shrimp and microworms once or twice a day, squirted near the cave with a pipette. Week 2 onward: add finely crushed flake and powdered fry food. Keep the temperature steady and do small, regular water changes of around 20 percent twice a week, siphoning uneaten food.\n\nThe pair will spawn again every few weeks. Older juveniles usually stay and help defend the newest fry, which is the classic brichardi family behavior."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry reach about 1 inch in 2 to 3 months. The colony can stay together until the tank gets crowded or young males begin to fight with the parents, usually once the oldest young reach adult size. At that point, move some of the older juveniles to a grow out tank. If the parents start chasing a particular group of young, take them out quickly.\n\nJuveniles do well on baby brine shrimp, crushed flake and small frozen foods such as cyclops and daphnia. By 2 inches they look like small adults, with the white edged fins and lyre shaped tail. Keep a few pairs worth of young fish back if you want to start a second colony later."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Adding a pair to a small community tank, where they bully or kill tankmates.\n- Leaving extra adults in once a pair has formed.\n- Moving the rockwork to check for eggs.\n- Big swings in pH or temperature from large, unmatched water changes.\n- Expecting to see the spawn. Brichardi are secretive, so patience and close watching of the cave mouth are key."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$brichardi$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 20 gallon long tank is a good start, because multies use the bottom more than the height. Add at least 2 inches of fine sand, since they dig constantly and bury shells to make their homes. Then add 2 to 3 empty snail shells per fish, roughly 1.5 to 2 inches across. Shells sold for shell dwellers or cleaned escargot shells work well.\n\nThe site lists multies at 76 to 82°F, pH 7.8 to 8.6 and GH 10 to 20. Aim for about 78 to 80°F and pH 8.2 to 8.6. A sponge filter is ideal, and a weekly 20 to 25 percent water change keeps things stable."}, {"heading": "Starting a colony", "text": "Males are noticeably larger than females, often close to 2 inches against about 1.25 inches for a female. That is the main way to tell them apart, which only works once fish are grown. The usual approach is to buy a group of 6 to 10 juveniles and let them sort out territories, or start with one male and two or three females.\n\nMulties are carnivores. Feed small amounts of baby or adult brine shrimp, daphnia, cyclops and a quality fine flake or micro pellet once or twice a day. Their mouths are small, so food size matters."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "You will rarely see the actual spawning. The female lays her eggs inside a shell and the male fertilizes them, all out of sight. The signs are a female who spends most of her time in or right at the mouth of one shell and fiercely chases other fish away from it.\n\nClutches are small, commonly a few eggs up to about 20 or 30. Each female will use her own shell, and a male often controls a territory containing several females."}, {"heading": "Eggs and the first week", "text": "The eggs hatch in roughly 2 to 3 days at 78 to 80°F, and the fry stay inside the shell while they absorb their yolk. About 7 to 10 days after spawning, you will see tiny fry hovering at the shell entrance and darting back inside when anything comes near.\n\nThere is nothing for you to do except keep the water clean and avoid disturbing the shells. Do not lift shells to check; you may spill or stress the fry."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Multie fry are best raised in the colony. Week 1: squirt baby brine shrimp or microworms near the shells with a pipette once or twice a day. Week 2 onward: add very finely powdered flake. Keep the temperature steady and do small water changes of 15 to 20 percent twice a week, siphoning gently above the sand.\n\nThe fry will start digging and claiming their own little spots as they grow. Adults generally tolerate their own young, which is why colonies grow so well."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Multies grow slowly. Expect fry to reach about 1/2 inch in 2 months, with full adult size taking many months. A colony can stay together for a long time, but once the tank gets crowded or fights over shells become constant, move some of the young to their own tank with plenty of shells.\n\nKeep adding shells as the colony grows. A good rule is to always have more shells than fish."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Too few shells, which leads to fighting and lost fry.\n- Shallow sand, which stops them burying shells the way they like.\n- Feeding food that is too large for their small mouths.\n- Moving or rinsing shells while fry are inside.\n- Adding fish that compete for the bottom, such as other shell dwellers or bottom grazing cichlids."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$multifasciatus-shell-dweller$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 20 gallon long tank is enough for a pair or a male with two females. Use at least 2 inches of fine sand, since ocellatus dig constantly and bury their shells until only the opening shows. Add at least 2 empty snail shells per fish, about 1.5 to 2 inches across.\n\nThe site lists ocellatus at 75 to 80°F, pH 7.8 to 8.6 and GH 10 to 20. Breeding goes well at about 78°F and pH 8.2 to 8.6. A sponge filter and weekly 20 to 25 percent water changes keep the tank stable. Keep tankmates out, as these little fish will fight much larger ones over a shell."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a pair", "text": "Adult males are clearly larger than females, roughly 2 inches against about 1.5 inches. Both sexes have the dark spot on the gill cover that gives the fish its name. Some strains, such as gold ocellatus, have bright color in both sexes, so size is the best guide.\n\nA pair works well, and a male with two females also works if each female can claim her own shells. Feed small portions of brine shrimp, daphnia, cyclops and fine flake or micro pellets once or twice a day."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "The female picks a shell, buries it and starts guarding it. The male visits and courts her, and spawning happens inside the shell where you cannot see it. The female then spends most of her time in the shell or right at the opening and becomes very defensive.\n\nClutches are commonly around 20 to 50 eggs. The male is not much help with care, and he may move on to court other females."}, {"heading": "Eggs and the first week", "text": "The eggs hatch in roughly 2 to 3 days at about 78°F, and the fry stay inside the shell while they absorb their yolk. About 7 to 10 days after spawning, you will see tiny fry hovering around the shell entrance with mom keeping watch.\n\nLeave the shell alone. Lifting it to check will scatter the fry or stress the female."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Week 1 of free swimming: squirt baby brine shrimp or microworms near the shell once or twice a day. Week 2 onward: add very fine powdered flake. Keep the temperature steady and change 15 to 20 percent of the water twice a week, siphoning gently.\n\nAfter a few weeks, the female will usually want to spawn again and may start chasing the older fry away. That is the time to move them to their own grow out tank, along with a few shells."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Ocellatus grow slowly. Fry reach about 1/2 inch in 2 months, and full size can take most of a year. Grow them out in a tank with sand and plenty of small shells so each juvenile can find a spot. Feed small amounts several times a day and keep up water changes.\n\nAs they mature, young males start fighting over shells. Split them up or move them on before injuries happen.\n\nYoung ocellatus are easiest to sell or trade at around 1 inch, once they are big enough to eat small pellets and handle a move. Keep one or two of your best young females and an unrelated male if you want to start a fresh pair down the line. Label gold and regular forms separately so buyers know what they are getting."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Too few shells, which leads to constant fighting.\n- Keeping them with other fish, which they will attack.\n- Lifting shells to look for eggs or fry.\n- Leaving older fry in with a female ready to spawn again.\n- Using coarse gravel, which stops them burying their shells."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$ocellatus-shell-dweller$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Maternal mouthbrooder", "sexing": "Tricky in aquarium strains; males have more egg spots and longer fins. Venting is most reliable", "group": "1 male to 3 or 4 females", "breeding_tank": "55 gallons or more, rock caves, hard alkaline water", "conditioning": "2 weeks of spirulina flake and pellets with small amounts of protein", "trigger": "Stable water at 78 to 80°F with enough females and rock cover", "spawn": "Commonly 10 to 40 eggs, depending on female size", "eggs": "Held about 3 to 4 weeks (often 21 to 28 days) at 78 to 80°F", "fry": "Released free swimming, about 3/8 inch", "first_foods": "Baby brine shrimp, crushed spirulina flake and fry pellets", "grow_out": "About 1 inch in 2 to 3 months", "parents": "No care after release; adults eat fry, so isolate the holding female"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 55 gallon tank is the site's minimum for red zebras, and it suits one male and three or four females. Stack rocks into lots of caves and crevices so females can break line of sight with the male. Sand or fine gravel is best, and strong filtration helps in a heavily stocked tank.\n\nKeep the water at 76 to 82°F, pH 7.8 to 8.6 and GH 10 to 25. For breeding, aim for about 78 to 80°F and pH 8.0 to 8.4. Change 30 to 50 percent of the water each week."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a harem", "text": "Sexing red zebras is tricky because aquarium strains vary. Females are usually orange, and males can be orange or pale blue to whitish depending on the strain. Males usually grow larger with longer pointed fins and more, brighter egg spots on the anal fin. Color alone is not reliable, so venting is the best check.\n\nKeep one male with three or four females. Feed a spirulina based flake or pellet twice a day for about 2 weeks, with protein foods as an occasional treat. Breed them in a species tank, since red zebras hybridize easily with other Maylandia."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "The male intensifies his color, shakes and leads a female to a flat spot or cave. She lays a few eggs at a time, picks them up and mouths at his egg spots to fertilize them. This can go on for an hour or more.\n\nAfterward she hides with a full throat and stops eating. Clutches are commonly around 10 to 40 eggs, with larger females holding more."}, {"heading": "Holding time", "text": "Females hold for about 3 to 4 weeks, often 21 to 28 days at 78 to 80°F. If the male harasses her, move her after about a week to a 10 to 20 gallon tank with matched water and a cave. Net her gently and avoid chasing, or she may spit the eggs.\n\nSome breeders strip the fry when they are well developed, but most beginners do best letting her release naturally. A well conditioned female holds better, so do not skip the conditioning."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "The fry are released free swimming, about 3/8 inch long. Week 1: baby brine shrimp and finely crushed spirulina flake twice a day. Weeks 2 to 4: add fry pellets. Keep the tank at 78 to 80°F, use a sponge filter and change 25 to 50 percent of the water two or three times a week.\n\nRemove the female after release and give her a week or two of good feeding before returning her to the harem."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry reach about 1 inch in 2 to 3 months and around 2 inches in 5 to 6 months. Give them rockwork and space, and keep up the water changes. Young males start to show more egg spots and longer fins at about 2 inches, which is a good time to sort them.\n\nFeed spirulina based foods as the main diet, since rich foods every day can cause bloat in mbuna.\n\nThree or four small meals a day grow fry faster than one big one, and frequent water changes matter even more than food. Sort the fry by size if a few start to outgrow the rest, because bigger siblings will push smaller ones off the food. Groups sell and rehome best at around 1.5 to 2 inches, before the males turn on each other."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Assuming color tells you the sex and ending up with the wrong ratio.\n- Mixing red zebras with other Maylandia and getting hybrids.\n- Moving the holding female too early, causing her to spit.\n- Overfeeding protein, which can lead to bloat."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$red-zebra-cichlid$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "The site lists a 40 gallon minimum for yellow labs, and a group of one male and three or four females fits well in a 40 breeder or larger. Stack rocks into caves along the back, add sand or fine gravel and leave open water in front. Labs are calmer than most mbuna, but males still claim a territory.\n\nKeep the water at 75 to 82°F, pH 7.6 to 8.6 and GH 10 to 25. For breeding, aim for about 78 to 80°F and pH 8.0 to 8.4. Change 30 to 50 percent of the water every week."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a harem", "text": "Sexing yellow labs by looks is unreliable. Males usually grow larger and have more intense black on the dorsal, anal and pelvic fins, sometimes with a dusky throat. But many females also have black in their fins, so do not rely on it. Venting is the most reliable method. The other route is to grow out 6 to 8 juveniles and remove extra males once you see who is dominant.\n\nFeed a spirulina based flake or pellet twice a day with small portions of brine shrimp, mysis or daphnia a few times a week for about 2 weeks. Labs eat more protein than most mbuna, but rich foods every day can still cause bloat."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "The male brightens, shakes his body and leads a female toward a flat rock or cave. She lays a few eggs at a time, picks them up in her mouth and mouths at his anal fin to fertilize them. Spawning can last an hour or more.\n\nAfterward she hides with a puffy throat and stops eating. Broods are commonly about 10 to 25 eggs, with young females holding fewer."}, {"heading": "Holding time", "text": "Females hold for about 3 to 4 weeks at 78 to 80°F. Labs are mild enough that some breeders leave holding females in the main tank, but moving her after about a week to a 10 to 20 gallon tank with matched water gives the best fry survival.\n\nNet her gently without a long chase. Moving her in the first few days often makes her spit. Some breeders strip well developed fry, but natural release is easier on beginners and the female."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "The fry come out free swimming, about 3/8 inch long and already yellow. Week 1: baby brine shrimp and finely crushed flake twice a day. Weeks 2 to 4: add fry pellets and continue the brine shrimp. Keep them at 78 to 80°F with a sponge filter and change 25 to 50 percent of the water two or three times a week.\n\nRemove the female after release and give her a week or two of good feeding before returning her to the group."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry reach about 1 inch in 2 to 3 months and roughly 2 inches in 5 to 6 months. They are peaceful with each other, so grow out is straightforward. Feed three or four small meals a day if you can and keep up frequent water changes, which do more for growth than extra food.\n\nLook for good color: bright, even yellow and clean black fin edges. Keep your best fish for future breeding, and avoid breeding fish with faded or patchy color."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Assuming black fins always mean male.\n- Keeping labs with similar yellow mbuna, which can lead to hybrids.\n- Moving the holding female too early.\n- Feeding heavy protein every day.\n- Overcrowding the grow out tank, which slows growth."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$electric-yellow-lab$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "The site lists a 40 gallon minimum for a group of Boeseman's rainbowfish. For a temporary breeding setup, a 30 gallon long or larger works for a male and two or three females. Add two or three spawning mops made of dark acrylic yarn, or dense fine leaved plants such as java moss. A sponge filter is ideal, because fry and eggs get pulled into stronger filters.\n\nThe site's care range is 75 to 82°F, pH 7 to 8 and GH 8 to 20. For breeding, aim for about 79 to 82°F and pH 7.2 to 7.8."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning", "text": "Males are deeper bodied, with brighter blue fronts and orange to red rear halves, and longer dorsal and anal fins. Females are slimmer, with duller coloring. Males also show a bright color stripe on the forehead when displaying.\n\nFeed live or frozen foods such as brine shrimp, bloodworms and daphnia, plus a good flake and spirulina, two or three times a day for about 2 weeks. A well fed group spawns more often and lays more eggs."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "Rainbowfish usually spawn in the morning, especially when the tank gets morning light. The male displays with his fins spread and a bright forehead stripe, then swims beside a female into the mop. She releases a few eggs at a time, each with a sticky thread that catches on the yarn or leaves.\n\nThey spawn daily for weeks, laying a few to a few dozen eggs each day."}, {"heading": "Eggs and hatching", "text": "Check the mop every day or two and move it, or pick the eggs off, into a hatching tank or container with the same water. The eggs are clear and about 1 mm across.\n\nEggs hatch in about 7 to 12 days at 79 to 82°F. Because they are laid over several days, you will get a steady trickle of fry rather than one big hatch. Remove any eggs that turn white, since fungus can spread. A small dose of methylene blue in the hatching container helps, but remove it before the fry hatch."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Rainbowfish fry are tiny and stay near the surface. Week 1: infusoria, green water or liquid fry food, several times a day. Week 2: add vinegar eels and very fine powdered food. From about week 2 to 3, they can take baby brine shrimp. Week 4 onward: add crushed flake.\n\nKeep the fry at 78 to 80°F with a gentle sponge filter, and change 10 to 20 percent of the water every few days with matched water. Keep the water clean but avoid big changes, which stress the fry. Many losses in the first two weeks come from starvation, so feed small amounts often."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Boeseman's rainbowfish grow slowly. Expect about 1 inch in 3 to 4 months. Young fish are silvery and plain, and the classic blue and orange colors can take a year or more to fully develop.\n\nFeed three or four times a day as they grow and keep up regular water changes. Separate fry by size if bigger siblings start bullying smaller ones.\n\nMove the growing fry into a larger tank once they reach about 1/2 inch. More space and clean water are the two things that speed up rainbowfish growth the most. Keep your best colored fish back for future breeding."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Leaving eggs in with the adults, who will eat them and the fry.\n- Feeding baby brine shrimp too early, before fry can eat it.\n- Mixing different Melanotaenia species in the breeding tank, which can cause hybrids.\n- Expecting full color in fry; it takes many months."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$boesemani-rainbowfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting Up the Breeding Tank", "text": "A 5 to 10 gallon tank is ideal. Use soft, acidic water: around pH 5.5 to 6.5 and GH 1 to 6, which sits inside the care range of pH 5.5 to 7. Keep the temperature at 77 to 80°F.\n\nFill a good part of the bottom with java moss or another fine moss, and add leaf litter and floating plants to dim the light. A sponge filter on a low setting keeps the water clean without sucking up fry. Mature tanks with established biofilm give much better results than new ones. If you can, let the tank run with moss and leaves for a month or more before adding the fish."}, {"heading": "Sexing and Conditioning", "text": "Males are slimmer and show more intense red color, especially when displaying. Females are rounder and paler. In practice, keeping a group of 6 to 10 gives you both sexes.\n\nCondition the group for 1 to 2 weeks with tiny live and frozen foods such as daphnia, cyclops and baby brine shrimp. Small, frequent meals work better than big ones for fish this size."}, {"heading": "Spawning", "text": "Chili rasboras spawn in moss and fine plants. A male shows his color and leads a female into the moss, where they release a few eggs. They tend to spawn often but in very small numbers, so you will not see a big event.\n\nTwo approaches work. One is to move a group into the breeding tank for about a week, then remove the adults. The other is to keep a heavily planted, species-only tank and let fry appear among the moss. This second method gives fewer fry but is less work."}, {"heading": "Eggs and Hatching", "text": "The eggs are tiny and very hard to find. They commonly hatch in about 1 to 2 days at 77 to 80°F, and the fry become free swimming a few days later. Keep the water stable and avoid disturbing the moss while you wait."}, {"heading": "Raising the Fry", "text": "Chili rasbora fry are some of the smallest fry in the hobby.\n\n- Weeks 1 to 2: infusoria, paramecium and the biofilm on moss and leaves\n- Weeks 2 to 3: vinegar eels and microworms\n- From about week 3: newly hatched baby brine shrimp\n\nFeed a little several times a day and keep a strong infusoria culture going so you never run short. Snails and a small amount of moss help clean up leftover food. Change about 10 percent of the water twice a week using soft water of the same temperature. Clean water matters, but large changes can do more harm than good."}, {"heading": "Growing Out", "text": "Growth is slow. Juveniles reach near adult size in about 3 months, and red color develops gradually. Keep them in soft water and feed small live foods to bring out the best color.\n\nOnce they are taking baby brine shrimp, move the young fish to a larger planted tank and increase water changes to about 20 percent a week, still using soft water. They can be sexed once color comes in. Chili rasboras are best kept and sold in groups, so wait until most of the batch is well colored before passing them on. Keep a group of your best fish to start the next generation."}, {"heading": "Common Mistakes", "text": "- Hard or alkaline water, which reduces spawning and egg survival\n- Leaving adults in with eggs and fry\n- A brand new tank without biofilm or infusoria\n- Big water changes with mismatched water\n- Giving up too soon; small spawns add up over weeks"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$chili-rasbora$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "How Monte Carlo Propagates", "text": "Monte Carlo grows as creeping stems with small, round leaves. The stems spread across the substrate and send roots down at the leaf nodes. Each rooted section can live on its own, so any piece with a few leaves and roots can be cut off and planted. Monte Carlo can also be grown out of water in a humid setup, and many nursery pots arrive in this emersed form, which is why new plants sometimes look a little different once they adjust underwater.\n\nIn good conditions the plant spreads on its own into a thick mat. You speed things up by splitting it into many small clumps and spacing them out."}, {"heading": "What You Need", "text": "- A healthy portion or pot of Monte Carlo\n- Fine substrate, ideally an aquarium soil\n- Moderate to high light\n- CO2 injection for the best results\n- Fine scissors and planting tweezers\n\nMonte Carlo is a little more forgiving than some carpet plants and can grow without CO2, but growth is slow and the carpet is thinner. With CO2 and good light it spreads quickly. If you add CO2, raise it slowly, run it only while the lights are on, and watch fish and shrimp for gasping."}, {"heading": "Step by Step", "text": "Remove any rock wool or pot material from new plants. Gently pull or cut the mat into small clumps, each about the size of a thumbnail with a few stems and roots.\n\nPush each clump into the substrate with tweezers, leaving the leaves just above the surface. Space clumps about 1 to 2 inches apart in a grid. Closer spacing fills in sooner. Planting at an angle, with the stems leaning slightly, helps hold the clumps down until roots take hold.\n\nAs the clumps send runners outward, you can also trim edge pieces from the established carpet and replant them into bare areas."}, {"heading": "The Dry Start Method", "text": "Some hobbyists start Monte Carlo out of water. Plant the clumps in moist substrate, keep the tank covered with plastic wrap to hold humidity, mist daily and leave the lights on a normal schedule. After about 4 to 6 weeks the carpet has rooted well, and you can slowly flood the tank. This avoids floating pieces and gives a strong root system before fish go in.\n\nWatch for mold during a dry start. Small patches can usually be removed and the area aired out. When you flood the tank, pour water slowly over a plate or bag so the substrate is not disturbed, and expect a short adjustment period as the leaves switch to underwater growth."}, {"heading": "Caring for New Plants", "text": "Expect some melting of leaves in the first week or two as the plant adjusts. New runners usually appear within 2 to 3 weeks. Once the carpet fills in, trim it regularly with sharp scissors to keep it low. Thick, untrimmed carpets can lift off the substrate as the lower layers die.\n\nFeed the carpet with a regular liquid fertilizer, and use root tabs if your substrate is plain gravel rather than soil. Pale or yellow new leaves often mean a nutrient shortage. Keep the area clean during water changes by hovering the siphon above the carpet rather than digging into it. As the carpet thickens, trim it like a lawn, cutting off the top layer every couple of weeks, and use the trimmings to start new patches elsewhere."}, {"heading": "Common Mistakes", "text": "- Planting large clumps that rot in the middle\n- Low light, which makes the plant grow upward instead of across\n- Coarse gravel that the fine roots cannot grip\n- Letting the carpet grow too thick so it lifts\n- Digging fish that uproot new clumps\n- Adding fish that pick at plants before the carpet has rooted"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$monte-carlo$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 5 to 10 gallon tank is ideal. Cover the bottom with plastic mesh, marbles or a thick mat of java moss so eggs fall out of reach of the parents. Use a small sponge filter and keep the light low, or cover the sides with paper.\n\nThe site lists a range of pH 6 to 7.5 and GH 3 to 15 for keeping. For spawning, aim for the soft, acidic end: pH 6 to 6.5 and GH under 6, at 77 to 80°F. Rain water or RO water mixed with tap water and filtered over peat or leaves are common ways to get there."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a pair", "text": "Females are rounder and deeper bodied, especially when full of eggs, while males are slimmer. Separate the sexes for one to two weeks if you can and feed generously with live or frozen brine shrimp, bloodworms and daphnia, plus a good flake.\n\nPick the roundest female and the most active, best colored male. Some breeders use two males with one female, which can improve fertility. Group spawning a few males and females together in a larger tank also works and needs less fuss over picking pairs, though egg eating is higher.\n\nStart preparing fry food now. Set up an infusoria culture and a vinegar eel or microworm culture a week or two ahead, because the fry will need them within days of hatching."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "Move the pair into the breeding tank in the evening. Spawning commonly happens the next morning as light reaches the tank. The male chases the female through the moss, they quiver side by side, and a few eggs are released and fertilized each time. A full spawn is commonly 100 to 300 eggs over a couple of hours.\n\nIf nothing happens after two or three days, return the fish to conditioning and try again. A small, cooler water change of soft water can help start things."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "Remove the adults as soon as spawning ends, as they will eat the eggs. Keep the tank dim, since the eggs and newly hatched fry are light sensitive. Eggs hatch in about 24 to 36 hours at 77 to 80°F.\n\nSome eggs will go white and fuzzy; these are infertile. Remove what you can with a pipette. A little methylene blue or a few dried leaves can help keep fungus down in soft water."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "The fry hang on the glass and plants after hatching and become free swimming about 3 to 5 days later. At that point they need very small food.\n\n- Days 1 to 7 of free swimming: infusoria or paramecium, several times a day\n- Week 2: vinegar eels or microworms, adding newly hatched brine shrimp once they can take it\n- Weeks 3 to 6: brine shrimp and finely crushed flakes\n\nStart small water changes of about 10% every couple of days in the second week, using matched soft water. Keep the temperature steady around 77 to 79°F and gradually raise the light."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Black neons show their black and white stripes at a few weeks old and reach about three quarters of an inch in about 8 to 10 weeks. Move them into a larger tank with more frequent water changes as they grow, and keep feeding several small meals a day."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Leaving adults in with the eggs\n- Bright light on the eggs and young fry\n- Hard, alkaline water, which lowers fertility\n- Offering baby brine shrimp before the fry can eat it\n- Big water changes that swing temperature or hardness"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$black-neon-tetra$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Diamonds are a little larger than many tetras, so a 10 to 20 gallon tank works well. Use a sponge filter, a mat of java moss or a spawning mop, and mesh or marbles on the bottom. Keep the light dim.\n\nThe site lists 72 to 82°F, pH 6 to 7.5 and GH 4 to 15 for keeping. For spawning, aim for soft, acidic water: pH around 6 to 6.5 with low hardness, at 77 to 80°F."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a pair", "text": "Adult males develop long, trailing dorsal and anal fins and a stronger violet sparkle. Females are rounder with shorter fins. Note that the full sparkle takes many months to develop, so young fish can be hard to sex.\n\nCondition for one to two weeks with bloodworms, brine shrimp, daphnia and a good flake. Separating the sexes during this time often improves results.\n\nChoose a female that looks clearly full of eggs and a male in his best color. Prepare an infusoria culture and a vinegar eel or microworm culture at least a week ahead, as you will need them within a few days of the eggs hatching. Having baby brine shrimp hatching on schedule for week two also saves a lot of stress."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "Add the pair in the evening. Spawning commonly starts with the first light next morning. The male displays with spread fins and leads the female into the plants, where they quiver together and scatter eggs. A large, well fed female can lay a few hundred eggs over a couple of hours.\n\nIf they do not spawn within a few days, try a small water change with slightly cooler soft water. Some pairs need a second attempt after another week of conditioning, so do not give up after one try.\n\nA pair can be spawned again every couple of weeks if they are well fed between spawns, so a missed or poor first spawn is not a big loss."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "Remove the adults as soon as spawning is over. Keep the tank dark or very dim. Eggs hatch in about 1 to 2 days at 77 to 80°F.\n\nPull out white, fungused eggs where you can. A few dried leaves or a mild dose of methylene blue are common ways to limit fungus."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry become free swimming about 3 to 5 days after hatching.\n\n- First week free swimming: infusoria or paramecium several times a day\n- Week 2: vinegar eels or microworms, then newly hatched brine shrimp\n- Weeks 3 to 8: brine shrimp and finely crushed flake\n\nFrom week two, change about 10% of the water every couple of days with matched water. Keep the temperature steady near 78°F."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Diamond tetra fry grow at a moderate pace, reaching about 1 inch in roughly 3 months. The famous sparkle develops slowly and can take most of a year to show fully. Give the growing fish more space and keep feeding varied foods.\n\nMove the young fish into a larger, planted tank once they are eating brine shrimp well. Weekly water changes of 20 to 30% and several small meals a day give the most even growth. Sort out any runts so they are not outcompeted at feeding time."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Choosing young fish before they can be sexed reliably\n- Bright light on eggs\n- Hard, alkaline water\n- Leaving parents in the tank\n- Feeding food that is too large in the first week"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$diamond-tetra$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 5 to 10 gallon tank is enough. The best results come from a mature tank that has been running for weeks with plenty of biofilm and microscopic life. Fill it with dense java moss or other fine leaved plants, use a gentle sponge filter, and keep the light low.\n\nThe site lists 73 to 82°F, pH 6 to 7 and GH 2 to 10 for embers. For breeding, stay soft and acidic: around pH 6 to 6.5 and GH under 5, at 77 to 80°F. A few dried leaves help soften and tint the water and grow infusoria at the same time."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a pair", "text": "Males are slimmer with a deeper orange red color, especially when displaying. Females are rounder through the belly and a little paler. Condition for one to two weeks with live or frozen baby brine shrimp, daphnia, cyclops and a fine flake.\n\nEmbers are tiny, so their foods should be tiny too. Pick one plump female with one or two of the brightest males, or move a small group of four to six fish into the breeding tank. Start an infusoria culture and a vinegar eel culture before you set them up."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "Spawning usually happens in the morning. The male flashes his colors and drives the female into the moss, where they quiver together and release a few eggs at a time. Embers lay far fewer eggs than larger tetras; a few dozen per spawn is typical, and the eggs are almost invisible.\n\nThere are two common methods. One is to leave the pair in for two or three days and then remove them. The other is a colony approach: keep a small group in a heavily mossed species tank and let a few fry survive among the moss without separating anything. The colony method gives fewer fry per spawn but needs little effort."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "Once the adults are out, keep the tank dim. Eggs hatch in about 24 to 36 hours at 77 to 80°F. Because the eggs are so small and hidden in moss, you may not see them at all until fry appear.\n\nFungus is less of a problem in a mature, leafy tank, but keep the water clean and avoid overfeeding the adults while they are in the tank."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "The fry become free swimming about 3 to 4 days after hatching and are tiny, close to the size of a dust speck. This is the stage where most attempts fail.\n\n- Week 1: infusoria or paramecium several times a day, plus whatever grows on the moss\n- Week 2: vinegar eels added, along with very fine powdered fry food\n- Weeks 2 to 3 on: newly hatched brine shrimp once their bellies can take it\n\nDo not change much water in the first week; top up with matched soft water. From week two, small changes of about 10% every few days keep things clean. Keep the temperature steady at 77 to 79°F."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Ember fry grow slowly at first and then pick up speed once they eat baby brine shrimp. Expect around 2 to 3 months to reach about half an inch and start showing orange color. They can join the adults in a planted tank once they are too big to be eaten, usually at about a third of an inch."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Starting in a brand new, sterile tank with no microscopic food\n- Offering baby brine shrimp too early; the fry are too small\n- Large water changes that shock tiny fry\n- Hard water, which cuts fertility\n- Expecting large spawns like bigger tetras"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$ember-tetra$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use a 5 to 10 gallon tank with a sponge filter, mesh or marbles on the bottom, and a mat of java moss or a spawning mop. Keep the room dim and cover the sides.\n\nThe site lists 74 to 82°F, pH 6 to 7.5 and GH 2 to 12 for glowlights. For spawning, aim for soft, slightly acidic water around pH 6 to 6.5 and GH under 6, at 78 to 80°F. Filtering over peat or mixing RO water with tap water are common ways to get there."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a pair", "text": "Females are larger and noticeably rounder, especially from the side, while males are slimmer. Condition for one to two weeks on brine shrimp, bloodworms, daphnia and flake, ideally with the sexes kept apart.\n\nChoose a female that looks full of eggs and an active, well colored male. Start an infusoria culture and a microworm or vinegar eel culture before you spawn them.\n\nA female that has been well fed for two weeks is usually obvious: she looks swollen behind the pelvic fins when viewed from above. If she still looks slim, give it another week. Spawning an underconditioned female tends to give small spawns with many infertile eggs, which only invites fungus into the tank."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "Move the pair in the evening. In the morning the male chases the female and the two often swim side by side and roll over together as they release eggs, a behavior many breeders describe as a barrel roll. Eggs drop into the moss a few at a time. A full spawn is commonly 100 to 200 eggs.\n\nIf nothing happens in two or three days, try a small water change with slightly cooler soft water. If that fails, return them to conditioning for another week."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "Remove the adults right after spawning. Keep the tank dim, as tetra eggs are light sensitive. Eggs hatch in about 24 hours at 78 to 80°F.\n\nInfertile eggs turn white and grow fungus. Remove them where you can and consider a few dried leaves or a light dose of methylene blue to keep fungus down."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "The fry hang on the glass and moss at first, then become free swimming about 3 to 5 days after hatching.\n\n- First week free swimming: infusoria or paramecium several times a day\n- Week 2: vinegar eels or microworms, then newly hatched brine shrimp\n- Weeks 3 to 8: baby brine shrimp and finely crushed flake\n\nStart 10% water changes every couple of days from week two, matching temperature and hardness. Slowly increase light as the fry grow. Keep the temperature steady around 78°F, since swings stress tiny fry.\n\nA common trick is to add a few small snails or a little established moss from another tank before the fry hatch. They help keep uneaten food from fouling the water while the fry are too small to clean up after."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Glowlight fry grow at a steady pace and reach about three quarters of an inch in 2 to 3 months. The glowing stripe appears after a few weeks. Move them to a larger tank as they grow, feed several small meals a day and keep up weekly water changes of 20 to 30%."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Bright light on the eggs and young fry\n- Leaving the parents in the tank\n- Water that is too hard\n- Feeding food that is too big in the first week\n- Overfeeding a bare breeding tank\n- Moving fry to new water without matching temperature"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$glowlight-tetra$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 5 to 10 gallon tank is plenty. Plant it heavily with fine leaved plants or moss, add a gentle sponge filter, and keep the light low. Floating plants help keep the surface dim.\n\nThe site lists a wide range of 72 to 80°F, pH 6 to 7.5 and GH 1 to 12. For breeding, aim for the soft, acidic end: around pH 6 to 6.5 and GH under 5, at 77 to 79°F."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a pair", "text": "Males are slimmer, show a stronger red in the fins and stripe, and usually have white or bluish tips on the pelvic fins. Females are rounder and paler. Like other pencilfish, both sexes switch to a pattern of dark blotches at night, which is normal.\n\nCondition for one to two weeks with small live and frozen foods such as daphnia, cyclops and baby brine shrimp. Pencilfish have small mouths, so keep foods fine. Start infusoria and vinegar eel cultures in advance."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "Pencilfish do not spawn in a single burst. The male displays with spread fins, leads the female into the plants and the pair release a few eggs at a time. This continues on and off for days. The total from one pair is commonly a few dozen eggs over a spawning period.\n\nBecause the adults eat eggs, many breeders use one of two methods. One is to move the clumps of moss or plants into a separate hatching tank every day or two and replace them. The other is to leave a pair in a very heavily planted tank for about a week and then remove the adults."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "The eggs are small, clear and hard to see. They hatch in roughly 1 to 3 days at 77 to 79°F, depending on temperature. Keep the hatching tank dim.\n\nFungus is usually limited in a planted, soft water setup, but remove any white eggs you can see and avoid overfeeding nearby."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "The fry become free swimming about 3 to 5 days after hatching. They are very small and need microscopic food.\n\n- Week 1: infusoria or paramecium several times a day\n- Week 2: vinegar eels and very fine powdered food\n- Week 3 on: newly hatched brine shrimp, then crushed flake\n\nKeep water changes small, about 10% every few days with matched soft water, and avoid temperature swings. If you move plants with eggs every few days, you will end up with fry of different ages, so keep batches in separate containers if you can, as larger fry outcompete smaller ones."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Pencilfish fry grow slowly at first. Expect roughly 3 months to reach about 1 inch, and a little longer for the full adult color. Feed several small meals a day and keep up regular water changes in a planted grow out tank.\n\nGolden pencilfish stay peaceful as they grow, so a planted community style grow out tank with only small, gentle tank mates works well once the young are about half an inch. Males start to show the red fins and white pelvic tips as they mature, which makes it easy to pick out future breeding pairs. Weekly water changes of 20 to 30% and fine, varied foods give the best color."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Waiting for one big spawn, when pencilfish lay a few eggs a day\n- Leaving eggs with the adults for weeks\n- Offering food too large for the fry's tiny mouths\n- Hard or alkaline water\n- Mixing fry of very different sizes"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$beckford-pencilfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 10 gallon tank works well. Add a sponge filter, mesh or marbles on the bottom, and a mat of java moss or a spawning mop. Keep the light dim.\n\nThe site lists 72 to 81°F, pH 6 to 7.5 and GH 3 to 15 for keeping lemons. For spawning, aim for soft, slightly acidic water: around pH 6 to 6.5 and GH under 6, at 77 to 80°F."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a pair", "text": "Mature males show a broader black edge along the bright yellow anal fin, and the top of the eye turns a vivid red when they are in condition. Females are rounder and slightly paler.\n\nCondition for one to two weeks with bloodworms, brine shrimp, daphnia and flake. Separating the sexes helps. Choose a plump female and the most colorful male, or put a small group of two males and two females into a larger breeding tank. Get infusoria and microworm or vinegar eel cultures going a week ahead of time."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "Move the fish in the evening. Spawning usually starts the next morning. Males display with spread yellow fins, chase the female into the plants and the pair quiver side by side to release eggs. A full spawn is commonly 100 to 300 eggs.\n\nIf they do not spawn in a few days, try a small, slightly cooler soft water change, then go back to conditioning if needed.\n\nWatch the male's color. A ready male turns a much stronger yellow and the black on his fins becomes very sharp. A pale, shy male in the breeding tank usually means the fish are not yet settled. Give them a day or two before deciding the attempt has failed."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "Remove the adults straight after spawning. Keep the tank dim. Eggs hatch in about 1 to 2 days at 77 to 80°F.\n\nRemove white, fungused eggs where you can. A few dried leaves or a light dose of methylene blue can help limit fungus.\n\nThe eggs are small and slightly sticky, so most will be caught in the moss or under the mesh. Leave them where they fell rather than trying to move them."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry become free swimming about 3 to 5 days after hatching.\n\n- First week free swimming: infusoria or paramecium several times a day\n- Week 2: vinegar eels or microworms, then newly hatched brine shrimp\n- Weeks 3 to 8: brine shrimp and finely crushed flake\n\nStart 10% water changes every couple of days from week two, matching temperature and hardness. Gradually increase the light.\n\nA large spawn can produce more fry than one small tank can support. Once they are on baby brine shrimp, thin them out into a second tank rather than letting the water quality slide."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Lemon tetra fry reach around 1 inch in about 3 months. The yellow color builds slowly and the full adult color takes several more months. Grow them out in a larger tank with weekly 20 to 30% water changes and varied foods. Lemons that get a good mix of foods tend to color up better as adults.\n\nOnce the young fish are about half an inch they can move into a planted tank with other small, peaceful fish. Keep back a few of the best colored males and roundest females as your next breeding group. Avoid pairing siblings for generation after generation; bring in unrelated stock from time to time."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Leaving the parents with the eggs\n- Bright light on the eggs\n- Hard water lowering fertility\n- Feeding food too large in the first week\n- Crowding a large spawn into a small tank\n- Expecting full adult color from young fish"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$lemon-tetra$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 10 gallon tank with a sponge filter, mesh or marbles on the bottom and a thick mat of java moss or a spawning mop is ideal. Keep the light dim and cover the sides.\n\nThe site lists a cooler, softer range for red phantoms: 71 to 79°F, pH 5.5 to 7 and GH 1 to 12. For spawning, keep the water soft and acidic, around pH 6 and GH under 4, at about 75 to 78°F. Peat filtration, dried leaves and RO water are common ways to get there."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a pair", "text": "Sexing is fairly easy in adults. Males have a taller, more pointed, mostly red dorsal fin and are slimmer. Females have a smaller dorsal fin marked with black and white, and a rounder belly.\n\nCondition for one to two weeks with bloodworms, brine shrimp, daphnia and a quality flake. Wild caught or recently imported fish may need longer to settle and recover before they spawn well. Pick the roundest female and a male in strong color.\n\nSet up infusoria and microworm or vinegar eel cultures at least a week ahead. The fry need tiny food within days of hatching, and it is hard to start a culture fast enough once they are already swimming."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "Move the pair into the breeding tank in the evening. Spawning commonly starts the next morning. The male displays with his red fins spread, leads the female into the moss, and the pair quiver side by side to release a few eggs at a time. A well conditioned female commonly lays 100 to 300 eggs.\n\nIf they do not spawn in a few days, a small water change with slightly cooler, very soft water may help. Some pairs need a second or third try.\n\nYou can also try a small group spawn with two males and one or two females in a slightly larger tank. Competition between males often triggers spawning in fish that ignore each other as a single pair. Expect more egg eating in a group spawn, so remove the adults promptly."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "Remove the adults as soon as spawning ends. Keep the tank dark or very dim, as the eggs are light sensitive. Eggs hatch in about 1 to 2 days at 75 to 78°F.\n\nIn very soft water, fungus can spread quickly from dead eggs. Remove white eggs where you can and consider a few dried leaves or a light dose of methylene blue."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry become free swimming about 3 to 5 days after hatching.\n\n- First week free swimming: infusoria or paramecium several times a day\n- Week 2: vinegar eels or microworms, then newly hatched brine shrimp\n- Weeks 3 to 8: baby brine shrimp and finely crushed flake\n\nStart 10% water changes every couple of days from week two, matching the soft water closely. Keep the temperature steady and slowly increase the light."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Red phantom fry grow steadily and reach about three quarters of an inch in around 3 months. Red color builds gradually as they mature. Grow them on in a larger tank with weekly 20 to 30% water changes and varied foods. Slowly acclimate young fish to slightly harder water if they are going to buyers with different tap water.\n\nOnce the young fish are about half an inch they can share a planted tank with other small, peaceful fish. Keep a group of the best colored young as future breeders, and bring in unrelated fish now and then to avoid inbreeding over many generations."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Water that is too hard or alkaline\n- Running the breeding tank too warm\n- Bright light on eggs\n- Leaving the adults in after spawning\n- Feeding food that is too large in the first week"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$red-phantom-tetra$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Egg-depositor, eggs stuck to a surface above the waterline (male splashes eggs to keep them wet)", "sexing": "Males larger with longer fins and red to orange markings; females smaller with shorter fins", "group": "One pair", "breeding_tank": "15 to 20 gallon, lowered water level, tight glass lid, overhanging leaf or slate", "conditioning": "1 to 2 weeks on mosquito larvae, fruit flies, bloodworms and brine shrimp", "trigger": "Soft, slightly acidic water and a gap of a few inches below the lid", "spawn": "Commonly several dozen to over 100 eggs from repeated leaps", "eggs": "Hatch in about 2 to 3 days at 77 to 80°F; fry drop into the water", "fry": "Free swimming within a few days of hatching; small", "first_foods": "Infusoria or vinegar eels, then baby brine shrimp", "grow_out": "About 3 months to roughly 1 inch", "parents": "Male tends eggs; remove adults once eggs hatch, as they may eat fry"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use a 15 to 20 gallon tank with a sponge filter and a tight fitting glass lid. Splash tetras are excellent jumpers, so the lid is essential. Lower the water level to leave a gap of about 2 to 4 inches between the surface and the lid.\n\nGive the pair a spawning surface above the water. Many breeders let them use the underside of the glass lid, or place a broad plant leaf or a sloping piece of slate that overhangs the surface. Floating plants help the fish feel secure.\n\nThe site lists 73 to 82°F, pH 6 to 7.5 and GH 1 to 12. For spawning, soft and slightly acidic water around pH 6 to 6.5 at 77 to 80°F works well."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a pair", "text": "Males are larger and more colorful, with longer, flowing fins edged in red or orange. Females are smaller with shorter fins and less color.\n\nCondition for one to two weeks. Splash tetras feed heavily at the surface, so mosquito larvae, fruit flies, bloodworms and brine shrimp are ideal, along with flake. Pick a plump female and a male in full color.\n\nKeep the pair in the breeding tank for a few days before expecting a spawn. A settled male starts patrolling a spot just under the lid or leaf, which is usually where the eggs will be laid. If he picks a spot you cannot see well, that is fine; the splashing tells you where the eggs are."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "The male courts the female near the surface and leads her to the spot he has picked. The pair then leap out of the water together, cling to the surface for a moment, and lay a few eggs before dropping back. This repeats many times, and the total commonly reaches several dozen to over 100 eggs.\n\nAfterward the male stays close to the egg site and regularly flicks water onto the eggs with his tail to keep them from drying out. Watching this is the best way to confirm a spawn."}, {"heading": "Eggs and hatching", "text": "Leave the male to do his job. The eggs hatch in about 2 to 3 days at 77 to 80°F, and the fry drop into the water below. Keep the room humid and the lid closed so the eggs do not dry out.\n\nOnce the eggs have hatched, remove both adults, as they may eat the fry. If the male is not tending the eggs, some breeders keep them moist with a gentle mist of tank water instead."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "The fry become free swimming within a few days of hatching and stay near the surface.\n\n- First week: infusoria or vinegar eels several times a day\n- Week 2: newly hatched brine shrimp and very fine powdered food\n- Weeks 3 to 8: baby brine shrimp and crushed flake\n\nChange about 10% of the water every couple of days from week two, matching temperature and softness. Keep the lid on even for fry, as young splash tetras jump too.\n\nThe fry do best with a little floating plant cover and gentle filtration. Feed small amounts often, and siphon the bottom every day or two so leftover food does not foul the water."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry grow at a moderate pace, reaching about 1 inch in roughly 3 months. Males start to show longer fins as they mature. Grow them on in a covered tank with regular water changes and small surface foods."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- No lid, or a lid with gaps; splash tetras will jump out\n- Water level too high, leaving no space for egg laying\n- Removing the male before the eggs hatch\n- Leaving adults in with newly hatched fry\n- Letting the room dry out so eggs shrivel"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$splash-tetra$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Substrate spawner (eggs laid on slate, leaves or glass)", "sexing": "Unreliable by shape; best told by the breeding tube at spawning (male narrow, female blunt)", "group": "One pair; raise 6 juveniles to let a pair form", "breeding_tank": "30 gallon or larger tall tank for one pair, sponge filter, slanted slate or broad leaf", "conditioning": "2 to 3 weeks on bloodworms, brine shrimp, blackworms and quality pellets", "trigger": "A bonded pair, warm stable water and frequent water changes", "spawn": "Commonly 200 to 1000 eggs", "eggs": "Hatch in about 2 to 3 days at 80 to 82°F", "fry": "Wigglers for about 4 to 5 days, then free swimming", "first_foods": "Newly hatched brine shrimp", "grow_out": "About 6 to 8 weeks to quarter size, 3 months or more to sell", "parents": "Parents fan and guard eggs; young pairs often eat eggs and improve with time"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A pair needs a tall tank of of at least 30 gallons. Use a sponge filter or a well covered intake and a bare bottom for easy cleaning. Add a spawning site: a slanted slate, a length of PVC pipe, a broad plant leaf or a cone leaning against the glass.\n\nThe site lists 76 to 84°F, pH 6 to 7.5 and GH 3 to 15. Domestic angels spawn readily in that range. Most breeders keep spawning pairs at about 80 to 82°F with stable water and regular changes."}, {"heading": "Getting and conditioning a pair", "text": "Angelfish are hard to sex until they spawn. The most reliable way to get a pair is to raise about six young fish together and watch for two that claim a corner and chase the others away. Once they spawn, you can see the breeding tubes: the female's is broad and blunt, the male's is narrow and pointed.\n\nCondition for two to three weeks with bloodworms, brine shrimp, blackworms and quality pellets. Do frequent water changes during this time. Angels usually reach breeding age at around 8 to 12 months."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "A pair ready to spawn will choose a site and clean it carefully with their mouths for a day or more. The breeding tubes then drop. The female makes passes along the surface laying rows of eggs, and the male follows to fertilize them. Spawns are commonly 200 to 1000 eggs, with older, larger females at the upper end.\n\nHealthy pairs often spawn every 1 to 2 weeks if they are well fed and their eggs are removed.\n\nIf you choose to let the parents raise the eggs, keep the tank quiet, avoid moving decor and keep other fish out. Parents that get through to free swimming fry can be great to watch, as they herd the cloud of babies around the tank."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "You have two choices. Leave the eggs with the parents, who will fan and guard them, or remove the slate to a separate hatching tank. Many breeders remove the eggs, especially with young pairs that eat their first few spawns.\n\nIn a hatching container, place an air stone near the eggs so bubbles gently move water across them, and add a little methylene blue to help prevent fungus. Eggs hatch in about 2 to 3 days at 80 to 82°F. Infertile eggs turn white; remove them if you can."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "After hatching, the fry are wigglers that hang from the slate or the bottom for about 4 to 5 days while they absorb their yolk. Once they swim freely, start feeding.\n\n- Weeks 1 to 2: newly hatched brine shrimp two or three times a day\n- Weeks 3 to 4: brine shrimp plus finely crushed flake or small pellets\n- Week 5 on: frozen foods and small pellets\n\nChange 10 to 20% of the water every day or two once they are eating, siphoning uneaten food. Keep the temperature around 80°F.\n\nA bare bottom makes it much easier to see and remove leftover brine shrimp. If you hatch eggs artificially, wait until the fry are swimming before you remove the methylene blue with water changes."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Angel fry grow quickly with good feeding. Fins and body shape develop within a few weeks, and they reach about quarter size in roughly 6 to 8 weeks. Most breeders sell them at quarter to half dollar body size, which takes around 2 to 3 months or more. Thin out the fry into more tanks as they grow; crowding stunts them."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Buying two adults and assuming they are a pair\n- Giving up when a young pair eats its first spawns\n- No fungus control on artificially hatched eggs\n- Feeding fry before they are free swimming\n- Crowding fry in too small a tank"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$angelfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 5 to 10 gallon tank with a tight lid is ideal, because clown killies jump. Add plenty of floating plants with trailing roots, a spawning mop near the surface, and a gentle sponge filter. These fish live at the surface and prefer very little flow.\n\nThe site lists 73 to 79°F, pH 6 to 7 and GH 2 to 8. For breeding, soft, slightly acidic water around pH 6 to 6.5 at 75 to 77°F works well."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a group", "text": "Males are easy to spot by their bright tail fin, streaked with red and blue in a rocket shape, and colorful dorsal and anal fins. Females have the same dark vertical bands but plain, clear fins.\n\nCondition for one to two weeks with small live and frozen foods: baby brine shrimp, daphnia, microworms and wingless fruit flies. Use one male with two or three females, or a small colony in a heavily planted tank.\n\nA colony tank is the low effort option: a small group in a mature, heavily planted 10 gallon with lots of floating plants will usually produce a trickle of fry that hide at the surface among the roots. Separating eggs gives far more fry, but needs more containers and more food cultures. Either way, start infusoria and vinegar eel cultures before you expect the first hatch."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "The male displays with spread fins next to a female near the surface. The pair settle together into floating plant roots or the mop, and she lays a single egg or a few eggs at a time. This goes on daily rather than in one burst.\n\nEggs are tiny and clear. You can find them by gently checking the mop or plant roots with a flashlight. Each female adds only a few eggs per day, so numbers build up slowly.\n\nIf you pick eggs, a simple routine works well: check the mop every two or three days, move eggs into a small labeled container with the date, and keep each batch separate. That way you know roughly when each batch should hatch and can match the fry to the right food."}, {"heading": "Incubating the eggs", "text": "You can leave eggs in the tank and let some fry survive among the plants, or pick eggs from the mop every few days and move them to a small container with tank water. Commonly reported hatch time is about 8 to 14 days at 75 to 77°F, so be patient.\n\nA small dose of methylene blue or a few dried leaves can limit fungus. Remove white or fuzzy eggs."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Clown killifish fry are very small and stay at the surface. They swim right away and need tiny foods.\n\n- Week 1: infusoria or paramecium several times a day\n- Week 2: vinegar eels and finely powdered food\n- Week 3 on: microworms, then newly hatched brine shrimp\n\nKeep fry in shallow water with floating plants. Change about 10% of the water every couple of days, matching temperature and softness. Because eggs hatch over many days, fry will be different sizes, so separate bigger ones from smaller ones."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Growth is slow. Expect about 3 months for fry to reach adult size, around 1.25 inches. Males start showing tail color a few weeks before then. Keep grow out tanks covered and well planted.\n\nYoung clown killies are peaceful and do well together in groups. Once they are about half grown they can rejoin the adults in a planted tank. Keep feeding several small meals a day of live or frozen foods, as well fed fry reach breeding size sooner and color up better."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Strong filter flow that pushes these surface fish around\n- No lid\n- Feeding baby brine shrimp too soon\n- Expecting one large spawn\n- Discarding eggs too early when they can take up to two weeks to hatch"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$clown-killifish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 10 to 20 gallon tank with a tight lid is enough for a male and two or three females. Use a sponge filter and provide a container of boiled peat or fine sand on the bottom for spawning. Plants or floating cover give the females somewhere to rest.\n\nTurquoise killies are adaptable and commonly kept at about 72 to 79°F in neutral to slightly alkaline water. They do not need very soft water."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing a group", "text": "Males are vivid, with turquoise or blue bodies and either red or yellow tails depending on the strain. Females are smaller and plain brown.\n\nCondition with bloodworms, blackworms and brine shrimp several times a day. Like other annual killies, they rarely accept dry food. Use one male with two or three females so no single female is chased too much.\n\nTurquoise killies are often kept as two strains with red or yellow tails. Keep strains apart if you want to maintain them true, and label your peat bags by strain. Females can be worn down by constant spawning, so rest them in a separate tank for a week or two between rounds with plenty of food."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "The male displays to a female with spread fins and leads her to the substrate. They press together and dive into it, laying an egg or a few at a time. This continues daily. Over a few weeks you can collect dozens of eggs.\n\nThis species is also widely kept in research labs, which is why it is so well known for its fast life cycle. In a home aquarium the basics are the same as for other annual Nothobranchius: a substrate to spawn in, meaty food and a regular routine for collecting and storing eggs."}, {"heading": "Storing the eggs", "text": "Every one to two weeks, collect the peat or sand and squeeze out most of the water. Store it damp in a labeled, dated bag or container at room temperature.\n\nIncubation varies a lot. Some eggs develop in a few weeks, while others pause in a resting stage for months. Breeders often report around 6 weeks to 3 months. Check the eggs regularly; when they are ready you will see the dark eyes of the developing fry."}, {"heading": "Hatching and raising the fry", "text": "To hatch, place the substrate in a container and add an inch or two of cool water. Fry usually hatch within hours.\n\n- Days 1 to 7: newly hatched brine shrimp several times a day\n- Week 2: baby brine shrimp plus chopped blackworms or grindal worms\n- Week 3 on: bloodworms and other meaty foods\n\nMove the fry out of the substrate after a day or so. Keep water shallow at first and change 20% every few days. Re-dry the substrate; more eggs may hatch on a later wetting."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Turquoise killifish grow incredibly fast. With heavy feeding, they can mature in as little as 3 to 5 weeks. They are also short lived, often only a few months to about a year depending on strain and care. Keep a steady supply of eggs in storage so your line continues.\n\nFeed growing fry several small meals a day and keep up frequent water changes, since heavy feeding fouls water fast. Separate males if they fight as they mature. Many breeders start a new round of spawning as soon as the young fish mature, while also keeping older bags of eggs in storage as a backup."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Storing substrate too wet or letting it dry out completely\n- Wetting eggs before they are developed\n- Underfeeding the fast growing fry\n- Losing the line because adults aged out and no eggs were saved\n- No lid; they jump"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$turquoise-killifish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 20 gallon tank is ideal for a pair or a male with two females. Use soft, acidic water: pH 5.5 to 6.5 and GH 1 to 10, with the lower end of both helping egg survival. Keep the temperature at 80 to 82°F for spawning, within the usual 78 to 84°F range.\n\nProvide a small cave for each female, such as a coconut shell or clay cave with an entrance just big enough for her. Add leaf litter, a sand bottom and some plants. A sponge filter is best because it will not suck up fry."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a pair", "text": "Males are clearly larger, up to about 3 inches, with long, pointed dorsal fin rays and a spade or lyre shaped tail with color. Females stay smaller, around 2 inches, with rounder fins, and turn bright yellow with black markings when ready to breed.\n\nStart with a pair, or one male with two or three females if the tank allows separate territories. Condition for two to three weeks with live and frozen foods: baby and adult brine shrimp, daphnia, bloodworms and mosquito larvae. Keep up regular water changes with soft water."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "The female turns bright yellow and starts guarding her cave, chasing other fish away. The male displays with spread fins. She then lays eggs on the cave ceiling, commonly 50 to 150, and the male fertilizes them.\n\nAfter spawning she usually drives the male away from the cave. In a small tank this can get rough, so watch that he has places to hide. You may only see the female hovering at the cave entrance; that is a good sign."}, {"heading": "Eggs and hatching", "text": "The eggs are small and reddish. At 80 to 82°F they hatch in about 2 to 3 days, and the wrigglers stay in the cave for several more days while they use up their yolk.\n\nThe female fans and cleans the eggs and removes any that fungus. Very hard or alkaline water is a common reason eggs fail, so keep the water soft. Do not shine lights into the cave or move it; disturbing her is the main reason young females eat their eggs."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry become free swimming about 7 to 9 days after spawning and follow the female around the tank. Week one: feed newly hatched baby brine shrimp, microworms and vinegar eels two or three times a day. Weeks two and three: keep up baby brine shrimp and add finely crushed flake. From week four, add chopped frozen foods.\n\nChange 10 to 20 percent of the water every two or three days with matched soft water, and keep the temperature steady at 78 to 82°F. Fry are sensitive to sudden changes."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry reach about three quarters of an inch in six to eight weeks and can be sexed at three to four months. The female will tolerate them for several weeks, but move them to a grow out tank by around a month so she can rest and spawn again.\n\nWater temperature is widely reported to affect the sex ratio in Apistogramma, with very warm water producing mostly males. Many breeders raise fry around 79 to 81°F for a more even mix."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Hard or alkaline water, which leads to eggs failing to hatch\n- Too many females with too few caves\n- Disturbing the cave in the first days\n- Feeding fry only powdered flake\n- Large water changes with water of a different pH\n- Giving up after the first clutch is eaten; young pairs often improve"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$apistogramma-agassizii$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Give a pair at least 75 gallons, matching the site's minimum, and more if possible. Keep the water at 70 to 77°F, pH 6.5 to 8 and GH 5 to 20. For spawning, many breeders run the tank at the upper end, around 76 to 77°F.\n\nAdd several flat stones or slates on sand, plus caves and tall decor to break lines of sight so the female has places to escape. Strong filtration is a must, because these are messy, heavy eaters."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a pair", "text": "Adult males are noticeably larger and develop a pronounced nuchal hump. Fin edging, orange in some lines and whitish in others, is usually brighter in males, but it is not a reliable test on its own. Venting at spawning is the only certain check.\n\nThe safest way to get a pair is to grow up five or six juveniles and let two pair off, then rehome the rest. If you introduce an adult pair, use a tank divider until the female shows interest. Condition for three to four weeks with quality pellets and frozen krill, mysis and earthworms."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "A ready pair darkens, cleans a flat stone and digs pits in the sand. You will see lip locking, tail slapping and shaking. The female lays rows of eggs, commonly 300 to 600, and the male fertilizes them.\n\nIf the male keeps chasing the female hard before she is ready, put in a divider at once. Some breeders use a divider with a gap only the smaller female can pass, so she can escape him.\n\nAfter spawning both parents guard the area fiercely and will attack a hand in the tank, so do maintenance carefully. Remove any other tankmates before the fry are free swimming, or they will be harassed."}, {"heading": "Eggs and hatching", "text": "Both parents fan the eggs and remove any that turn white. At around 77°F the eggs hatch in about 3 to 4 days, and the parents move the wrigglers into pits in the sand. Cooler water slows hatching.\n\nIf a pair keeps eating eggs, give them several chances first. As a backup, a stone of eggs can be hatched in a separate tank with matched water, gentle air near the eggs and a little methylene blue to limit fungus."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry are free swimming about 4 to 6 days after hatching. Week one: feed newly hatched baby brine shrimp three or four times a day. Weeks two and three: add finely crushed flake and micro pellets. From week four, add chopped frozen foods.\n\nWith hundreds of fry eating heavily, change 30 percent of the water two or three times a week. Keep temperature steady at 75 to 77°F."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry reach about an inch in six to eight weeks and around 2 inches by three months. Move them to grow out tanks by about three to four weeks, before the parents turn on them or start a new spawn.\n\nAs they grow, aggression rises, so sort by size and give plenty of space. Plan ahead for numbers: a single spawn can fill several tanks, and you need homes for them.\n\nFeed juveniles three or four times a day and keep up big water changes. Green terrors grow fast on a good diet but stunt in dirty water."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Putting two adults together with no divider\n- A tank too small for a pair\n- No hiding places for the female\n- Weak filtration for heavy feeders\n- Leaving fry with the parents too long\n- Raising more fry than you can house or rehome"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$green-terror$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 20 gallon tank works for a pair, with more room needed for a harem. Keep the water at 75 to 84°F, with about 80°F for spawning, pH 5.5 to 7 and GH 1 to 10. Softer, more acidic water within that range gives better egg survival.\n\nGive each female a cave, such as a coconut shell or clay cave, spaced apart with plants or wood in between. Add leaf litter and a sand bottom. Use a sponge filter so tiny fry are safe.\n\nDried leaves such as Indian almond leaves release tannins that soften and slightly acidify the water and give fry microscopic food to graze. Keep lighting moderate with some floating plants; a calm female is a better mother."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a group", "text": "Males grow to about 3 inches with long dorsal fin rays and red markings on the tail. Females are smaller and turn bright yellow with black markings when breeding.\n\nThis species is commonly kept as a harem of one male with two or three females. Condition for two to three weeks with live and frozen foods such as brine shrimp, daphnia, bloodworms and mosquito larvae. Keep the water soft and stable.\n\nSmall schooling dither fish such as pencilfish can help a shy pair feel safe, but keep them few, because they will eat free swimming fry. A dedicated tank for the group is easiest."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "The female colors up and claims a cave, chasing others away. The male displays to her, and she lays eggs on the cave ceiling, commonly 50 to 150. After the spawn she guards the cave and may chase the male away.\n\nIn a harem, each female may spawn at a different time, so expect several broods in the same tank once things settle."}, {"heading": "Eggs and hatching", "text": "At about 80°F the eggs hatch in 2 to 3 days. The wrigglers stay in the cave for several more days. The female fans the eggs and removes any that go white with fungus.\n\nKeep the cave dark and undisturbed. Hard water and big swings in pH are common reasons eggs fail. If a young female eats her first clutch, leave her be; many do better the next time."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry become free swimming about 7 to 9 days after spawning and follow their mother in a tight group. Week one: feed newly hatched baby brine shrimp, microworms and vinegar eels two or three times a day. Weeks two and three: keep up baby brine shrimp and add crushed flake. From week four, add chopped frozen foods.\n\nChange 10 to 20 percent of the water every two or three days with matched soft water, and keep the temperature at 78 to 82°F.\n\nSiphon leftover food daily. Dirty, warm water is the most common reason a healthy brood dwindles in its first two weeks."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry reach about three quarters of an inch in six to eight weeks. Move them to a grow out tank by about four weeks, especially in a harem where other females may attack a neighbor's fry.\n\nAs in other Apistogramma, very warm water is widely reported to produce mostly males, so raising fry around 79 to 81°F gives a better mix of sexes."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Too few caves for the number of females\n- Hard water and unstable pH\n- Disturbing the cave in the first days\n- Fry left in a busy harem tank\n- Feeding fry dry food only\n- Expecting a pair to spawn in hard tap water without softening"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$macmasters-dwarf-cichlid$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Cave spawner (male guards eggs)", "sexing": "Males slimmer and often brighter; females rounder and plumper when full of eggs", "group": "A group of 6 or more with several caves", "breeding_tank": "15 to 20 gallons, small caves or shells, hard alkaline water", "conditioning": "Live and frozen foods (brine shrimp, daphnia, bloodworms) for 3 weeks", "trigger": "Good conditioning, warm stable water around 78 to 82°F", "spawn": "Breeders report about 100 to 200 eggs on the cave ceiling", "eggs": "Breeders report hatching in roughly 4 to 5 days at around 80°F", "fry": "Very small; need tiny live foods from the start", "first_foods": "Infusoria and rotifers, then baby brine shrimp after about a week", "grow_out": "About half an inch in 2 to 3 months; growth is slow", "parents": "Male guards eggs; remove fry or eggs after hatching as adults eat fry"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 15 to 20 gallon tank suits a breeding group. Keep the water at 72 to 82°F, with spawning usually at about 78 to 82°F, pH 7 to 8.5 and GH 10 to 20. These fish come from coastal and brackish areas as well as freshwater; many breeders keep them in hard freshwater, and some add a small amount of marine salt for the adults.\n\nProvide several small caves, such as clay pots on their sides, short tubes or snail shells. Each male claims one. Use fine sand and a sponge filter to keep flow gentle.\n\nKeep tankmates out of the breeding tank. Bumblebee gobies are slow, shy feeders, and other fish will take the food before the gobies, leaving females poorly conditioned."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a group", "text": "Sexing is not always easy. Males are usually slimmer and may show brighter yellow and darker bands when breeding. Females are rounder, especially when carrying eggs. Buying a group of six or more is the easiest way to get both sexes.\n\nBumblebee gobies are picky eaters and often refuse flake. Feed live or frozen foods every day: baby brine shrimp, daphnia, cyclops and chopped bloodworms. Condition for about three weeks before expecting spawns."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "A male guards his cave and displays to females with flared fins and stronger colors. A ripe female enters the cave and lays eggs on the ceiling. Breeders report around 100 to 200 eggs per spawn.\n\nThe male then stays in or near the cave, guarding and fanning. He becomes aggressive toward other fish that come close. You may only see his head at the entrance for several days."}, {"heading": "Eggs and hatching", "text": "Breeders report the eggs hatching in roughly 4 to 5 days at around 80°F. The eggs are small and attached to the cave surface. The male fans them and removes damaged eggs.\n\nAdults will eat the fry once they hatch, so many breeders move the cave with eggs to a separate small tank shortly before hatching. Match the temperature and water chemistry, add gentle aeration near the eggs, and keep the tank dim."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "The fry are very small and many breeders find this the hardest stage. Week one: feed infusoria and rotifers several times a day. Some breeders report better survival with a small amount of salt in the fry tank, but keep any change gradual. Week two: start adding newly hatched baby brine shrimp. Weeks three and four: feed baby brine shrimp and microworms.\n\nKeep the fry at 78 to 82°F and change small amounts of water, 10 percent every day or two, with matched water. Keep cultures running before the spawn happens.\n\nStart an infusoria culture and a rotifer or green water culture a week or two before you expect a spawn. Without food ready on day one, most broods are lost."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Bumblebee goby fry grow slowly, reaching about half an inch in two to three months. They develop their banding as they grow. Feed small live and frozen foods several times a day and keep the water clean.\n\nAs juveniles get bigger, give them small caves of their own, because young males start defending territories early. Do not expect them to take flake; most keep preferring live and frozen foods.\n\nA small amount of fine algae and biofilm in the grow out tank helps, because fry graze between meals."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Soft, acidic water\n- Expecting them to eat flake\n- Leaving fry in with adults\n- No microscopic foods ready for the fry\n- Too few caves for the males\n- Big, sudden changes in salinity or hardness"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$bumblebee-goby$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use a 55 gallon tank or larger and fill much of it with rockwork that forms caves and crevices. Keep the water at 75 to 82°F, with breeding usually around 78 to 80°F, pH 7.6 to 8.6 and GH 10 to 25. Lake Malawi water is hard and stable, so avoid swings.\n\nDemasoni are very territorial. A crowded tank with lots of rock, and a colony of 12 or more fish, spreads aggression so no single fish is targeted. Strong filtration and weekly water changes are needed for the high stocking."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a colony", "text": "Sexing demasoni is difficult. Both sexes are the same blue and black barred pattern. Males are often larger and may have more egg spots, but this is not reliable. Venting is the most accurate method.\n\nMost breeders buy a group of 12 or more juveniles and let them sort out. Aim for more females than males over time. Feed spirulina based flake and pellets and other vegetable rich foods. Too much rich, meaty food can cause bloat in mbuna, so keep protein foods as an occasional treat."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "A male claims a cave or flat rock and displays to females with shaking and quivering. A ripe female follows him, lays a few eggs, and picks them up in her mouth, then nips at the male's anal fin egg spots, which fertilizes the eggs in her mouth. This repeats until she has her clutch, commonly about 10 to 20 eggs.\n\nAfterwards she has a swollen throat and stops eating. She will hide among the rocks."}, {"heading": "Holding the eggs and fry", "text": "The female holds the eggs and growing fry for about three weeks at 78 to 80°F. She eats little or nothing during this time.\n\nYou can leave her in the colony with plenty of rocks, but many fry are eaten after release. Most breeders move a holding female to a separate 20 gallon tank after about a week, catching her gently. Some strip the fry at about two weeks, but full term release is easier on the fry. Return her to the colony once she has recovered, but expect some aggression, so add her back after rearranging rocks.\n\nNever squeeze a female or force her mouth open hard. If you strip fry, hold her gently in a wet net underwater and let them fall out, and only after about two weeks."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "When released, fry are about a third of an inch and free swimming. Week one: feed crushed spirulina flake and baby brine shrimp three times a day. Weeks two and three: add crushed pellets. From week four, feed small pellets and flake.\n\nKeep fry at 78°F with 20 to 30 percent water changes twice a week. Fry are peaceful at first, but even young demasoni start to squabble within a few weeks."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry reach about an inch in eight to ten weeks. Raise them in a tank with plenty of small rocks and caves. As they grow, aggression builds, so a large group grows up better than a small one.\n\nKeep the diet mostly vegetable based and avoid overfeeding. Clean water and a good diet give strong, well colored fish.\n\nMany breeders move young demasoni into the main colony once they are about an inch and a half, so they join a large group rather than being picked on as newcomers."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Keeping only a few demasoni, which leads to one being bullied to death\n- Too little rockwork\n- Rich, meaty diets that cause bloat\n- Netting a holding female roughly\n- Mixing with similar looking mbuna that may hybridize"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$demasoni-cichlid$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 20 gallon long tank or larger works for a pair. Keep the water at 75 to 80°F, with spawning usually around 77 to 80°F, pH about 7.8 to 8.6 and hard water.\n\nBuild rock piles with lots of narrow crevices and caves. Julies spawn deep in the rocks and fry hide there for weeks. Use sand or fine gravel and a sponge filter to keep the tiny fry safe. Lake Tanganyika water is very stable, so avoid swings.\n\nKeep tankmates few. A pair defends its rock pile hard, and other fish may eat fry that wander out. Some breeders use a few small Tanganyikan dither fish, but a species tank is simplest."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a pair", "text": "Sexing masked julies is hard. Females are usually a bit larger and heavier, but this is not reliable. Venting at spawning is the surest check.\n\nThe best way to get a pair is to raise five or six juveniles together and let two pair off. Once a pair forms, move the others out. Condition for three weeks with quality flake and frozen brine shrimp, mysis and daphnia.\n\nIf you buy an adult pair, watch them closely for the first week. Mismatched julies can fight hard, and one may need to be removed."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "The pair claims a crevice deep in the rocks. They chase other fish away and stay close to the cave. The female lays eggs on the cave wall or ceiling, commonly 20 to 50, and the male fertilizes them.\n\nYou may see very little of the spawning itself, as it happens deep in the rocks. Increased guarding and both fish staying near one spot are good signs."}, {"heading": "Eggs and hatching", "text": "At 77 to 80°F, eggs hatch in about 3 days. The parents fan and clean them. The wrigglers stay in the cave while they use up their yolk.\n\nKeep the rocks undisturbed. Settled pairs rarely eat eggs, so artificial hatching is usually not needed.\n\nAvoid moving rocks or doing heavy cleaning near the cave during the first two weeks."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry become free swimming roughly 7 to 10 days after spawning. They stay in and around the rocks, grazing and picking at food. Week one: feed newly hatched baby brine shrimp and microworms, letting some reach the rocks. Weeks two and three: add crushed flake. From week four, add chopped frozen foods.\n\nKeep fry at 77 to 80°F and change 15 to 20 percent of the water twice a week, matching temperature and hardness.\n\nSiphon leftover food every day, as it collects in the rocks. Clean, stable, hard water is what keeps the fry growing."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Masked julie fry grow slowly, reaching about half an inch in six to eight weeks. The parents usually tolerate older broods, so you may see several sizes of fry living in the rocks.\n\nIf the parents start chasing older fry, move them to a grow out tank with plenty of small rocks. Feed several small meals a day.\n\nYoung julies take several months to reach adult size. Raise a group of five or six together if you want a future breeding pair."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Forcing two adults together instead of letting a pair form\n- Too few rocks and crevices\n- Soft or acidic water\n- Large sudden water changes\n- Leaving older fry when the parents begin chasing them\n- Keeping other Julidochromis that may hybridize"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$masked-julie$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 30 gallon tank is a sensible minimum for one pair, and larger is better if you want fry to stay with the parents. Use a sand bottom and build tall stacks of rock with plenty of narrow gaps and caves, so the pair can claim one and the fry have places to hide.\n\nKeep water hard and alkaline, in line with the care numbers for this fish: pH 7.8 to 8.6, GH 10 to 20, and 75 to 81°F. Around 78 to 80°F suits spawning. A sponge filter or a gently flowing canister with a sponge pre-filter keeps fry safe from the intake."}, {"heading": "Getting a compatible pair", "text": "Julies are hard to sex by looks. Females are usually the larger fish, but size alone is not reliable, and venting (checking the genital papilla) takes practice. The easiest route is to raise 6 juveniles together and let a pair form on its own.\n\nOnce two fish start sharing one cave and chasing others away, move the rest out. Pairs can turn on each other, so watch for one fish being driven into a corner. If that happens, separate them with a divider for a while before trying again.\n\nCondition with good pellets plus frozen mysis and brine shrimp for 2 to 3 weeks."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "You often won't see the spawn itself. The pair spend more time in one cave, dig sand out of it, and become more defensive. The female lays her eggs on the roof or wall of the cave, and the male fertilizes them.\n\nBreeders commonly report 20 to 100 eggs, with large, mature females sometimes laying more. The pair take turns guarding the entrance. Avoid moving rocks or shining a flashlight into the cave, which can stress them into eating the spawn.\n\nIf the pair spawn again while older fry are still in the tank, you may see two or three sizes of fry around the same rock pile. That is normal for Julies and a good sign the pair is settled."}, {"heading": "Eggs and early fry", "text": "At 78 to 80°F the eggs commonly hatch in about 3 days. The wrigglers stay stuck to the cave or on the floor of it while they use up their yolk.\n\nRoughly a week after hatching, about 10 days after the spawn, the fry start swimming. They do not roam the tank like many fish; they hover close to the rockwork near their cave. Parents handle egg care and fungus control themselves, so leave them alone."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "- Week 1 of swimming: baby brine shrimp once or twice a day, plus finely powdered flake.\n- Weeks 2 to 4: keep up baby brine shrimp and add crushed flake and small pellets.\n- Water changes: 15 to 20% weekly with matched temperature and hardness.\n- Temperature: hold steady at 78 to 80°F.\n\nFry can stay with the parents, which is the natural way for this species. If you want to raise every fry, move some to a grow-out tank once they reach about half an inch."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Julies grow slowly. Expect fry to reach roughly an inch at 2 to 3 months and close to adult size in about a year. Feed small meals several times a day and keep the water clean, since stunting is common in crowded grow-out tanks. Rockwork in the grow-out tank cuts down on squabbles.\n\nOnce fry are around an inch, they can be moved to a separate tank or sold on as juveniles. Leaving a large group of older fry with the pair works for a while, but parents may start to chase them once a new spawn arrives."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Buying two adults and assuming they will pair. Raise a group instead.\n- Too few caves, which leads to fighting.\n- Soft or acidic water; keep it hard and alkaline.\n- Shifting rocks to peek at the eggs.\n- Tankmates that steal fry. A species tank gives the best results."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$regani-julie$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 10 gallon tank is plenty, in line with the care numbers for this fish. Use a gentle sponge filter, a dark bottom and a big clump of java moss or two yarn mops. Floating plants help the fish feel secure.\n\nKeep water soft to medium hard: pH 6 to 7.5, GH 3 to 12, and 72 to 82°F, with 75 to 80°F a good spawning range.\n\nSome breeders keep a breeding colony in a well planted tank and let a few fry survive naturally in the moss. That gives fewer fry than pulling eggs, but it's a low effort way to keep a line going. For a breeding log, collecting eggs gives you more control."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing fish", "text": "Males have bigger dorsal and anal fins with bolder dark spots and more yellow color. Females are paler, with smaller and less heavily marked fins. Use 1 male to 2 or 3 females to spread out the male's attention.\n\nFeed small live and frozen foods such as baby brine shrimp, daphnia and cyclops for 1 to 2 weeks, plus fine flake. Spotted blue-eyes are short lived, often only about 2 to 3 years, so breed them while they're young and healthy."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "Males flare their fins and dance around a female. The pair dive into the moss or mop together and release a few eggs, which stick by fine threads. This repeats most mornings.\n\nYou won't get a big clutch. Most females lay a few eggs a day, sometimes up to 10 to 20 over a good day, so collect over a week or two for a usable batch.\n\nFemales that have spawned recently may look a little thin. Keep feeding them well, and give them breaks by adding more females or rotating them out if the male chases one too hard."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "Pick eggs off the mop with your fingers or a soft brush and put them in a small container with tank water. A drop of methylene blue or a few almond leaves helps prevent fungus, though many breeders skip it. Remove any eggs that turn white.\n\nHatch time is long for such a small fish: commonly about 10 to 20 days at 75 to 80°F. Warmer water hatches them faster."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "- Days 1 to 3: infusoria, vinegar eels or a fine fry powder several times a day.\n- Day 4 to week 3: newly hatched baby brine shrimp and microworms.\n- Week 4 on: add crushed flake.\n- Water changes: 10 to 20% every few days, matched carefully.\n- Temperature: 75 to 80°F.\n\nFry live near the surface, so keep the water level low at first and feed in small amounts often.\n\nKeep each week's batch of fry in its own container if you can. A clean, small container or a 2 to 5 gallon tank with a sponge filter is enough for the first few weeks."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry reach about half an inch at 1 to 2 months and can spawn by 3 to 4 months. Keep batches separated by age so larger fry don't outcompete smaller ones for food.\n\nGrow-out tanks should be well planted and stable. Feed small meals of baby brine shrimp and fine flake several times a day. Males usually show their fin spots and color by about 3 months."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Leaving eggs with the adults.\n- Giving up because eggs haven't hatched after a week; they often take longer.\n- Feeding too much at once and fouling the fry tank.\n- Waiting too long to breed short lived adults."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$gertrudes-blue-eye$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 20 gallon tank works well, in line with the care numbers for this fish. Use a gentle sponge filter, little or no current, and plenty of fine leaved plants, java moss or yarn mops.\n\nKeep water soft to medium: pH 6 to 7.5, GH 3 to 12, and 75 to 82°F, with about 78 to 82°F good for spawning. Threadfins are shy, so avoid boisterous tankmates.\n\nMany breeders leave the adults in a planted species tank and simply pull mops or moss every few days. This reduces stress on the adults, who can be skittish and don't move well between tanks."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and choosing fish", "text": "Males are easy to tell: they grow long, trailing dorsal and anal fins. Females are plain silver with short fins. Use 1 male to 2 or 3 females, or keep a small school with more females than males.\n\nThreadfins have tiny mouths. Feed baby brine shrimp, daphnia, cyclops and crushed flake for about 2 weeks.\n\nFeed small meals two or three times a day rather than one larger feed. Threadfins are slow, gentle eaters, and faster tankmates will take most of the food if you don't watch."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "Males flare their fins and display in front of females, often in a jerky dance. The pair dart into the mop or moss together and leave a few eggs that stick with fine threads.\n\nThey spawn a little each day, so check the mops every day or two and move eggs to a hatching container, or swap the mop out.\n\nSpawning is spread over many days, so plan to collect eggs over a week or two. A typical daily haul is small, from a few to a dozen or so eggs, which adds up over time."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "Hatch the eggs in a small tank or container with water from the breeding tank and gentle aeration. At 78 to 82°F, hatching commonly takes about 7 to 12 days.\n\nRemove white, fungused eggs daily. A few almond leaves or a drop of methylene blue can help with fungus.\n\nSince eggs are collected over several days, label each container with the date. Fry that hatch a week apart are very different in size, and keeping batches apart stops the bigger fry taking all the food."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "- Weeks 1 to 2: infusoria or paramecium cultures several times a day. Many fry are too small for baby brine shrimp at first.\n- Weeks 2 to 3: add microworms and newly hatched baby brine shrimp as soon as fry can take them.\n- Weeks 4 on: baby brine shrimp plus finely crushed flake.\n- Water changes: 10% every few days, matched carefully.\n- Temperature: 78 to 82°F.\n\nStart your infusoria cultures before the eggs hatch, since you'll need a steady supply."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Threadfins grow slowly. Expect about half an inch at 2 months and adult size in several months. Males start to show their trailing fins as they mature. Avoid fin nippers in the grow-out tank.\n\nOnce fry are taking baby brine shrimp well, survival improves a lot. Grow them in a tank with gentle flow and plenty of fine plants. Keep feeding small meals often, as their small mouths limit how much they can take at once."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- No infusoria ready when fry hatch.\n- Feeding only baby brine shrimp from day one, which many fry can't eat yet.\n- Strong filter flow that pushes weak fry around.\n- Leaving eggs with the adults.\n\nMost failures with threadfins happen in the first two weeks after hatching. If you can get a batch past that point on infusoria and microworms, the rest of the grow-out is usually smooth."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$threadfin-rainbowfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 20 gallon tank works well, in line with the care numbers for this fish. Use a sponge filter, a sand or bare bottom, and a few plants or spawning mops for the eggs.\n\nKeep water soft and acidic: pH 5.5 to 7, GH 1 to 10, and 73 to 79°F. Many breeders use pH around 6 and low hardness for spawning.\n\nLeaf litter, such as dried oak or almond leaves, adds cover and lowers pH slightly. Keep the tank in a quiet spot. Corydoras feel safer with some cover and spawn more readily in a calm tank."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a group", "text": "Females are larger and broader when seen from above, especially when full of eggs. Males are slimmer. Use 2 males to 1 female, or keep a group of 6 or more.\n\nCondition with frozen bloodworms, brine shrimp and quality sinking foods for 2 to 3 weeks. Keep water clean; Adolfo's corydoras can be sensitive to poor water quality.\n\nIf you're unsure of sexes, keep a group of 6 or more and let them sort themselves out. Groups also spawn more readily than single pairs, because the chasing seems to stimulate them."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "A large water change with slightly cooler water often triggers spawning, the way the rainy season does in the wild. Males chase the female, then a male and female form the classic T position. The female holds a few eggs in her pelvic fins and then sticks them on glass, plants or decor.\n\nAdolfo's corydoras usually lay modest numbers, commonly reported from a few to a few dozen eggs, often spread over a day or two.\n\nIf a water change doesn't trigger spawning, try repeating it every few days, or lowering the temperature by a couple of degrees with the change. Barometric pressure changes before storms also seem to trigger corydoras spawns."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "Move eggs gently with a finger or credit card edge to a hatching container with tank water and an air stone. At 73 to 77°F, the eggs commonly hatch in about 3 to 5 days.\n\nCory eggs are prone to fungus. Remove white eggs daily, and many breeders add a few almond leaves or a little methylene blue to the hatching container."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "- First few days: fry live on their yolk; don't feed yet.\n- Week 1 of feeding: microworms and fine powdered sinking foods twice a day.\n- Weeks 2 to 4: add baby brine shrimp and crushed sinking pellets.\n- Water changes: 10 to 20% every 2 to 3 days with soft water.\n- Temperature: 73 to 77°F.\n\nA thin layer of sand in the fry container helps, but keep it clean of leftover food."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry reach about half an inch to 3/4 inch by 2 months. Their black and orange markings develop as they grow. Keep water clean and feed small meals often, as losses usually come from dirty water rather than lack of food.\n\nMove fry to a larger tank with a sand bottom once they're about half an inch. Keep feeding a mix of baby brine shrimp, microworms and crushed sinking foods."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Hard or alkaline water.\n- Leaving eggs in the main tank.\n- Overfeeding fry, which fouls the water.\n- No cool water change to trigger spawning.\n- Too few males in the group.\n\nAdolfo's corydoras are often wild caught. Give new fish several weeks to settle and feed well before expecting spawns. Captive-bred fish often settle and spawn more readily."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$adolfos-cory$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Substrate spawner: lays on glass or smooth surfaces; male guards eggs", "sexing": "Mature males grow bristles on the sides of the head; females lack them", "group": "One pair, or 2 males to 2 or 3 females", "breeding_tank": "30 gallons or more, strong flow, smooth surfaces, sponge filter", "conditioning": "Vegetables, algae wafers and frozen foods for 2 to 3 weeks", "trigger": "Strong flow, clean water and good conditioning", "spawn": "Commonly reported from several dozen to over 100 eggs", "eggs": "Commonly hatch in about 6 to 9 days at 75 to 79°F", "fry": "Start feeding soon after hatching; delicate in the first weeks", "first_foods": "Green water, fine algae and vegetable powders, microworms and baby brine shrimp", "grow_out": "About an inch to 1.5 inches by 2 months", "parents": "Male guards eggs until hatching; fry are on their own after that"}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$royal-whiptail$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A small 5 to 10 gallon tank works. Use a gentle sponge filter, a dark bottom, fine plants or spawning mops, and cover the sides to keep it dim.\n\nThe care numbers list pH 6 to 7, GH 1 to 10 and 70 to 81°F. For spawning, use very soft, acidic water: about pH 5.5 to 6 and GH around 1 to 2, at about 75 to 77°F. RO or rain water is commonly used to get it soft enough.\n\nSet up the breeding tank a week or two early, so it's stable. Peat or almond leaves help lower pH and tint the water. Clean everything well, since soft water has little buffering and bacteria and fungus can grow quickly."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a pair", "text": "Females are rounder, which makes their blue stripe look bent. Males are slimmer with a straight stripe. Choose adult fish that are at least 6 months old.\n\nCondition with live or frozen foods like daphnia and brine shrimp for about 2 weeks. Move a pair or 2 males and 1 female into the breeding tank in the evening.\n\nIf a pair doesn't spawn within a few days, return them to the main tank, keep conditioning, and try again in a couple of weeks."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "Spawning usually happens in the early morning. The male chases the female into the plants and the pair release eggs that stick lightly to the plants or fall to the bottom.\n\nCommonly reported spawns are about 60 to 130 eggs. Remove the adults once they've finished, since they eat eggs.\n\nSome breeders cover the tank with dark paper or a cloth, leaving only a little light. This keeps eggs and fry from light and helps the adults feel secure."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "Keep the tank dark. Neon eggs and fry are sensitive to light. At 75 to 77°F, the eggs commonly hatch in about a day.\n\nRemove white, fungused eggs with a pipette. Keep the water still and clean, and avoid big changes.\n\nSoft, acidic water helps keep fungus down, but it also has little buffering, so keep an eye on pH. Avoid adding anything that could cause sudden changes."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "- Days 1 to 5: fry hang on plants and glass and use up their yolk.\n- Week 1 of swimming: infusoria or a fine fry powder several times a day.\n- Week 2: add microworms and newly hatched baby brine shrimp.\n- Weeks 3 to 6: baby brine shrimp and crushed flake.\n- Water changes: 10% every few days, matched carefully.\n- Temperature: 75 to 77°F.\n\nKeep the light low for the first week or two, then slowly increase it.\n\nUse an infusoria culture started a week before spawning, so you have food ready when the fry need it."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Expect about half an inch by 2 months. The blue and red stripes develop as fry grow. Slowly raise hardness to normal keeping levels over a few weeks.\n\nGrow fry in a tank with gentle flow and some cover. Keep feeding small meals several times a day. Neon tetras grow slowly, reaching adult size in about 6 months."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Hard water; neons rarely spawn without very soft water.\n- Bright light on eggs and fry.\n- Leaving the adults with the eggs.\n- Feeding baby brine shrimp too early.\n\nPatience matters with neons. Many breeders need several attempts before their first successful spawn, and getting water soft enough is usually the key."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$neon-tetra$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Silver dollars need a big tank. The care numbers for this fish list 75 gallons as a minimum, and breeding groups do better in larger tanks. Use a sponge or canister filter, dim light and fine leaved plants or floating plants for cover.\n\nThe care numbers list pH 6 to 7.5, GH 4 to 15 and 75 to 82°F. Many breeders use soft, slightly acidic water for spawning, about pH 6 to 6.5, at around 79 to 82°F.\n\nPlastic or hardy plants are a good choice, since silver dollars eat most soft plants. Floating plants also help the fish feel secure. Keep a tight lid on, since they can jump when startled."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a group", "text": "Males have a longer, lobed anal fin with a red edge. Females have a plain, straight anal fin. Keep a school of 6 or more so pairs can form.\n\nCondition with vegetables, plant-based foods and some frozen foods like bloodworms for 2 to 3 weeks. Silver dollars eat plants, so live plants in the breeding tank may be eaten.\n\nSilver dollars mature at around a year old and several inches long. Keep a mixed group and be patient; pairs form on their own once the fish are settled and mature."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you'll see", "text": "Males chase females and the pair swim side by side, releasing eggs that drop among plants and to the bottom. Spawning often happens in the morning.\n\nSpawns are commonly reported in the hundreds, sometimes up to about 2000 eggs. Adults usually don't eat many eggs, but moving the eggs or adults gives better results.\n\nIf you want to raise a lot of fry, siphon eggs from the bottom into a separate tank or move the adults after spawning. Leaving eggs in a busy tank with other fish usually means few survive."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "At 79 to 82°F, the eggs commonly hatch in about 3 days. Fry then rest on the bottom while they use up their yolk.\n\nRemove white, fungused eggs. A little methylene blue or almond leaves can help prevent fungus spreading.\n\nSilver dollar eggs are not sticky, so they fall to the bottom. If you siphon them into a separate tank, use water from the breeding tank to avoid any shock."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "- First few days of swimming: infusoria or a fine fry powder.\n- Weeks 1 to 3: newly hatched baby brine shrimp several times a day.\n- Weeks 3 to 6: crushed flake and blanched vegetables.\n- Water changes: 20% every few days.\n- Temperature: 79 to 82°F.\n\nFry usually start swimming roughly a week after hatching, once the yolk is used up.\n\nFry grow fast and eat a lot. Keep the water clean with regular changes, and remove uneaten food every day."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Expect about an inch by 2 months. Fry grow quickly with good feeding and clean water. Plan ahead: silver dollars reach about 6 inches, and a big spawn needs a lot of space.\n\nFeed small amounts several times a day and keep water clean. Move fry to larger tanks as they grow, since crowded fry grow slowly.\n\nSilver dollars grow fast in their first year, so check that grow-out tanks have enough filtration and space. Splitting large batches over several tanks keeps water quality up and growth even."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- A tank that's too small for a breeding group.\n- Hard, alkaline water.\n- Not planning for hundreds of fry.\n- Leaving fungused eggs to spread.\n\nMost failed attempts come from tanks that are too small or crowded. Silver dollars need space and calm to breed well."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$silver-dollar$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Substrate spawner (open spawner on flat stones or in shallow pits)", "sexing": "Males larger with longer front dorsal rays; females show a pink to red belly. Not foolproof", "group": "One bonded pair, ideally formed from a group of 6 juveniles", "breeding_tank": "20 gallons, sponge filter, flat stone, soft water, no tankmates", "conditioning": "Two to three weeks of frozen bloodworms, brine shrimp and quality small pellets", "trigger": "Stable 82 to 85°F water, soft acidic conditions and a clean flat surface", "spawn": "Commonly 100 to 300 eggs", "eggs": "Hatch in about 2 to 3 days at 82 to 85°F", "fry": "Free swimming roughly 3 to 5 days after hatching; very small", "first_foods": "Infusoria or vinegar eels, then microworms and newly hatched brine shrimp", "grow_out": "Roughly 1 inch in about 2 to 3 months with frequent water changes", "parents": "Good pairs guard eggs and fry; young pairs often eat early clutches"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 20 gallon tank is plenty for one pair. Use a mature sponge filter so fry are not sucked in, a thin layer of fine sand or a bare bottom, and one or two flat stones or slate pieces as spawning sites. A few plants and a piece of driftwood help the pair feel secure.\n\nKeep the water at 82 to 85°F, which sits within the 78 to 85°F range rams live in, and aim for pH 5.5 to 6.5 with GH under about 8. Rams are very sensitive to nitrate and ammonia, so the tank should be fully cycled and you should plan on 25 to 30 percent water changes at least twice a week. Keep the tank free of other fish so the pair are not stressed into eating eggs."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a pair", "text": "The easiest way to get a compatible pair is to grow up 6 young rams and let two pair off on their own. Males are usually larger, with the second and third dorsal spines extended. Females tend to be rounder and show a pink, red or orange belly, especially when ready to spawn. Many keepers also say females have blue spangles in the black dorsal spot, but this is not reliable on its own.\n\nCondition for two to three weeks with small meals two or three times a day: frozen bloodworms, brine shrimp, daphnia and a good small pellet. Remove uneaten food quickly, since leftovers foul soft water fast."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "A ready pair cleans a flat stone or digs a shallow pit, shimmering and tail slapping at each other. The female develops a short, blunt breeding tube and lays rows of small eggs, with the male following to fertilize them. Clutches are commonly 100 to 300 eggs.\n\nAfterward the pair take turns fanning the eggs and chasing away anything that comes near. Do not move decorations or do big water changes for a few days."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "At 82 to 85°F the eggs hatch in about 2 to 3 days. Good parents pick off white, fungused eggs. If your pair keeps eating clutches, many breeders move the stone to a small hatching container filled with water from the same tank, with gentle aeration beside the eggs. A light dose of methylene blue in the hatching container helps prevent fungus.\n\nThe wrigglers hang on the stone or get moved into pits for several days while they use up their yolk. Roughly 3 to 5 days after hatching they become free swimming."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Ram fry are tiny. For the first few days feed infusoria, vinegar eels or a fine powdered fry food several times a day, then add microworms and newly hatched brine shrimp. By the end of the second week most fry can take baby brine shrimp as their main food.\n\nKeep the temperature steady at 82 to 85°F and do small 10 to 20 percent water changes every day or two with matched, aged water. Siphon leftover food from the bottom daily. Sudden swings in temperature or a buildup of waste are the most common reasons a whole batch is lost."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "From week three, add crushed flake and micro pellets alongside the brine shrimp. Move the fry to a larger grow out tank once they are about half an inch, and keep up frequent water changes. Expect them to reach roughly 1 inch in about 2 to 3 months, with color showing as they grow. Growth is slower and losses are higher if nitrates are allowed to climb."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Breeding rams in a community tank where they are stressed and eat eggs\n- Running the water too cool, or letting temperature swing\n- Letting nitrate build up by skipping water changes\n- Giving up after one or two eaten clutches; young pairs often improve\n- Overfeeding fry and leaving food to rot on the bottom"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$german-blue-ram$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Egg scatterer over plants and substrate", "sexing": "Males have larger, pointed pectoral fins with a thick second ray; females larger and plumper", "group": "A group of 4 to 6 with at least 2 males", "breeding_tank": "55 gallons or more, soft sand, fine plants or spawning mops, tight lid", "conditioning": "Months of cool winter water, then frozen bloodworms, worms and sinking foods", "trigger": "Seasonal cool period then warming into the 70s°F, with large fresh water changes", "spawn": "Large clutches, commonly hundreds and sometimes thousands of eggs", "eggs": "Commonly hatch in about 2 to 3 days at around 72 to 75°F", "fry": "Tiny; free swimming a few days after hatching", "first_foods": "Infusoria and fine fry food, then baby brine shrimp after about a week", "grow_out": "Fast; often 2 inches within a few months", "parents": "No care; adults eat eggs, so remove adults or eggs after spawning"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Why breeding them is uncommon", "text": "Weather loaches are temperate fish that spawn seasonally. In heated aquariums held at one temperature all year they rarely breed. Commercial farms often use hormone injections, which home breeders should not attempt. The natural way is to give them a real winter and spring.\n\nThis is also a species you must never release. It is invasive in many places, and some US states restrict keeping it, so check local rules."}, {"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use at least 55 gallons, matching the minimum for adults that can reach 8 inches. Soft sand lets them burrow. Add bunches of fine plants or spawning mops for eggs. A tight lid is essential because weather loaches are skilled escape artists. Good filtration and gentle current keep oxygen high. Outdoor ponds in mild climates often succeed where tanks fail.\n\nIf you keep a pond, a shallow planted margin with fine leaved plants gives eggs somewhere to land, and fry may grow up unnoticed among the plants. In a tank, place spawning mops in a corner near the surface as well as on the bottom, because these loaches often spawn up in the water column."}, {"heading": "Sexing and conditioning", "text": "Sexing adults is fairly easy. Males have larger, more pointed pectoral fins, and the second ray of each pectoral fin is thickened with a small bony plate at its base. Females are usually larger and noticeably plumper, with smaller, rounded pectorals.\n\nGive the group a cool winter period of several weeks in the 50s to low 60s°F, within their 50 to 77°F range. Feed lightly during that time, then warm gradually and feed heavily with frozen bloodworms, earthworm pieces and sinking pellets.\n\nAn unheated tank in a cool room, or a sheltered garage that stays in the 50s°F, is often enough to give the winter rest. Many keepers also see the fish become restless before storms, which is how they earned the name weather loach. That restlessness alone does not mean they are about to spawn."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "As water warms into the low to mid 70s°F, often after a big cool water change, males chase females and one wraps around a female's body near the surface or among plants. Eggs are scattered over plants and substrate, and clutches commonly run to hundreds of eggs and can reach thousands. Spawning activity tends to be short and easy to miss."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fry", "text": "Remove the adults or move the mops to a separate tank as soon as you see eggs, since adults eat them. Eggs commonly hatch in about 2 to 3 days at 72 to 75°F. Remove any white, fungused eggs to keep them from spreading.\n\nThe fry hang on surfaces at first, then swim a few days later. Feed infusoria and fine powdered fry food, then add newly hatched brine shrimp after about a week. Do 20 percent water changes every couple of days.\n\nUse an air driven sponge filter in the fry tank, keep the water around 72 to 75°F, and add a few floating plants to grow infusoria. Fry spend much of their time on the bottom, so make sure food reaches them by feeding near the substrate and in several spots."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry grow quickly and are not fussy. By weeks three and four they take crushed flake, micro pellets and chopped frozen foods. Spread them out as they grow; many reach about 2 inches within a few months. Plan ahead for numbers, as this species gets large and needs big tanks.\n\nBy two to three months juveniles look like small versions of the adults and can be kept with peaceful fish. Sort out any runts and give them their own space so they catch up."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Keeping them heated all year with no seasonal cooling\n- Leaving adults with the eggs\n- No lid, leading to loaches on the floor\n- Producing more fry than you can rehome responsibly\n- Ever releasing fish or fry outdoors into the wild"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$dojo-loach$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Substrate spawner, usually on a leaf or flat surface", "sexing": "Males larger with lyre shaped, filament tipped tails; females smaller, rounded tails", "group": "1 male to 2 or 3 females", "breeding_tank": "30 gallons, soft acidic water, broad leaves, dim light, sponge filter", "conditioning": "Frozen and live bloodworms, brine shrimp and daphnia for 2 to 3 weeks", "trigger": "Very soft water around pH 5.5 to 6.0 and GH under 2, at 80 to 84°F", "spawn": "Commonly around 60 to 150 eggs", "eggs": "Commonly hatch in about 2 to 3 days at 80 to 84°F", "fry": "Free swimming about 5 to 7 days after hatching", "first_foods": "Newly hatched brine shrimp and microworms", "grow_out": "About 2 to 3 months to around 1 inch", "parents": "Female guards eggs and fry; male stays away. Leave fry with her a few weeks"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 30 gallon tank works for a small harem and gives the females space. Use fine sand, broad leaved plants and some leaf litter, plus driftwood for cover. Keep lighting dim. A sponge filter is safest for fry.\n\nThe water is the key. In the wild these fish live in blackwater as low as pH 4.5; in the aquarium aim for 5.5 to 6.5 with very low hardness. For breeding, lower slowly to around pH 5.5 with GH under 2 and very low conductivity, which in practice means RO or rain water. Keep it at 80 to 84°F. Leaf litter adds tannins that many breeders believe help.\n\nTest your water before you start rather than guessing. A TDS or conductivity meter is helpful because very low readings matter as much as pH. Lower the hardness gradually over a week or two so the adults adjust safely. Floating plants help keep the light soft and give the female a sense of security."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a harem", "text": "Males are larger and develop a lyre shaped tail with long filaments, plus bright blue and red markings. Females are smaller with rounded tails, and when ready to spawn they show bold checkerboard markings and often bright red pelvic fins.\n\nKeep 1 male with 2 or 3 females. Condition with frozen and live bloodworms, brine shrimp and daphnia for 2 to 3 weeks. These are carnivores and do not do well on dry food alone.\n\nBuying 6 to 8 juveniles and letting them grow up together is a good way to end up with a natural harem. Remove extra males once the dominant one is clear, since subordinate males get harassed. Make sure each female has a cave or leaf area of her own, and give the group a few weeks to settle before expecting eggs."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "The male displays with fins spread. The female chooses a leaf or flat surface, cleans it, and lays her eggs, commonly around 60 to 150. After spawning she drives the male away and guards the eggs alone. Females sometimes move eggs or larvae to a different leaf or into small pits."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus", "text": "At 80 to 84°F eggs commonly hatch in about 2 to 3 days. If your water is too hard, eggs often turn white and fungus within a day, which is the most common failure. If that happens, lower hardness and pH before the next spawn rather than adding chemicals.\n\nThe larvae hang on the leaf or in pits for several days, then become free swimming roughly 5 to 7 days after hatching."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Feed newly hatched brine shrimp and microworms two or three times a day from the first day of free swimming. The female leads the school around and protects them. Do 10 to 15 percent water changes every two or three days with the same soft, acidic water, matched in temperature. Siphon uneaten food daily.\n\nThe female keeps her fry close at first and gradually lets them wander farther. Any sudden movement or reflection can make her move them, which is normal. If she seems overly stressed, add more cover rather than removing her, since fry left with their mother usually grow better and feed more confidently than fry raised alone."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "After 3 to 4 weeks move the fry to their own tank so the female can rest. Keep feeding brine shrimp and add crushed frozen foods and fine pellets. Expect around 1 inch in roughly 2 to 3 months. Juveniles can tolerate slightly harder water, but change it gradually."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Water that is not soft or acidic enough, causing fungused eggs\n- Only one female, so the male harasses her constantly\n- Bright light and no cover\n- Big, sudden water changes during brooding\n- Feeding only dry food\n- Removing the female too early, before fry are feeding well on their own"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"q": "What pH do checkerboard cichlids need to breed?", "a": "Most breeders use very soft water around pH 5.5 to 6.0 with very low hardness. Eggs often fungus in harder water."}, {"q": "How do you tell male and female checkerboard cichlids apart?", "a": "Males are larger with lyre shaped, filament tipped tails. Females are smaller with rounded tails and bold checker markings when breeding."}, {"q": "Do checkerboard cichlids guard their eggs?", "a": "Yes. The female guards the eggs and fry alone and usually chases the male away."}, {"q": "How long do checkerboard cichlid eggs take to hatch?", "a": "Commonly about 2 to 3 days at 80 to 84°F."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$checkerboard-cichlid$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Most breeders use a 30 to 40 gallon bare bottom tank for a pair, which makes cleaning easy. Add a spawning cone, a length of PVC pipe or a piece of slate leaned against the glass. Use one or two air driven sponge filters and a heater sized to hold 84 to 86°F steadily, inside the site's 82 to 86°F care range.\n\nFor breeding, use soft water with pH around 6 to 6.5 and low hardness, within the library range of pH 6 to 7. Very soft water helps eggs develop, so many breeders blend RO water with tap water. Keep the tank in a quiet spot with few people walking past."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a pair", "text": "Discus are very hard to sex by appearance. Males are sometimes larger with a thicker lip and bigger forehead, but none of this is reliable. The best way is to grow up a group of 6 or more young discus and watch for two fish that pair off, clean a surface together and chase others away. When they spawn, the female's breeding tube is blunt and thick, and the male's is smaller and pointed.\n\nCondition with several small meals a day: beef heart based mixes, frozen bloodworms, brine shrimp and a high quality discus pellet. Daily water changes of 25 to 50 percent are normal for discus breeders."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "The pair clean the cone thoroughly, then start trembling and bowing at each other. The female makes passes up the cone laying lines of eggs, and the male follows to fertilize them. Clutches are commonly 100 to 300 eggs. Both parents then fan the eggs and remove any that go bad."}, {"heading": "Eggs and wrigglers", "text": "At 84 to 86°F the eggs hatch in about 2 to 3 days. The parents often move the wrigglers to a different spot on the cone. Roughly 2 to 4 days after hatching, the fry become free swimming and head straight for the parents.\n\nYoung pairs commonly eat their first few clutches. Patience usually pays off, and many pairs get it right after a few tries. Some breeders place a mesh cone guard around the eggs so the parents can fan but not eat them, then remove it once the fry swim."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "For the first days of free swimming the fry eat only the slime on their parents' skin. From about day 5 to 7, start offering newly hatched brine shrimp several times a day while they stay with the parents. Keep the temperature steady and do daily water changes, siphoning carefully around the fry.\n\nThe parents' skin can become worn as the fry grow. Most breeders separate the fry at about 3 to 4 weeks, once they are eating brine shrimp well."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "After separation, feed baby brine shrimp, then crushed flake and fine discus pellets, and later beef heart mixes, five or six times a day. Large daily water changes are what drive good growth. Even with good care, expect several months for juveniles to reach around 2 inches and show strain color, and stunting is common if water quality slips.\n\nRaise fry in a bare bottom tank so you can siphon every scrap of food, and spread them out as they grow. Overcrowded fry tanks are the most common cause of stunted discus with large eyes and small bodies. Stick to one strain per pair so the young breed true to their parents."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Assuming two fish are a pair before they spawn together\n- Hard water, which leads to eggs that never hatch\n- Too few water changes, causing stunted growth\n- Separating fry before they eat brine shrimp well\n- Moving a pair or the cone mid spawn"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$discus-domestic-strains$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use at least 40 gallons, the minimum for this species. Build rockwork with narrow crevices and add several large snail shells. Calvus are Lake Tanganyika fish, so keep the water hard and alkaline: pH 7.8 to 8.6, GH 10 to 25, and 76 to 82°F. Use good filtration but avoid strong flow.\n\nShells should be large enough for the female to fit inside but too small for the male. Large escargot or whelk shells are popular, and narrow gaps between stacked rocks work too. Use sand or fine substrate, which lets fish adjust their shells. Calvus are calm but predatory, so avoid tankmates small enough to eat. Peaceful Tanganyikan fish such as shell dwellers or Cyprichromis can share a large tank, but a species tank makes it easier to raise fry."}, {"heading": "Sexing and pairing", "text": "Adult males are much larger than females, often close to twice the length of a female. Females stay small enough to fit into shells. Young calvus are hard to sex, so many breeders grow up a group of 5 or 6 and let pairs form.\n\nOnce a pair forms, remove extras unless the tank is large. Calvus grow slowly, and it can take a couple of years to reach breeding size.\n\nCalvus can be kept as a pair or as a male with two females in a larger tank. Females in a shared tank need plenty of shells and crevices to avoid each other. Many keepers find established pairs stay together for years."}, {"heading": "Conditioning", "text": "Feed mysis shrimp, brine shrimp, krill and a good carnivore pellet once or twice a day. Avoid overfeeding, as leftovers foul the water. Good feeding helps the female fill with eggs.\n\nCalvus are carnivores but can suffer from a diet too rich in fatty foods. Mix meaty frozen foods with quality pellets, and keep one fasting day a week to help digestion. Feed from the surface so food reaches the calmer fish."}, {"heading": "Spawning and eggs", "text": "The female chooses a shell or narrow crevice and lays her eggs inside, where the larger male cannot reach. The male fertilizes them from the entrance. Clutches are commonly reported up to around 200 eggs.\n\nAt 78 to 80°F, eggs commonly hatch in about 3 days. The fry stay inside the shell and become free swimming roughly 8 to 10 days after spawning.\n\nYou may not see spawning at all. Signs include the female spending more time near one shell and the male guarding the area. Avoid moving shells or decor during this time. Keep the lights on a regular schedule and avoid big water changes for a week or so."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "When the fry emerge, feed newly hatched brine shrimp and crushed flake two or three times a day. The female usually guards the fry, and many breeders leave them with the parents.\n\nKeep up 20 to 30 percent weekly water changes, matching hardness and pH carefully. Calvus fry are sensitive to sudden water changes.\n\nAdd fine powdered fry food or crushed flake near the shell. Fry stay close to cover for a while, so make sure food reaches them by feeding in several spots. Keep tankmates to a minimum while fry are small."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Calvus are notoriously slow growers. Feed frozen and live foods, plus small pellets. Expect several months to reach 1 inch, and much longer to reach adult size. Patience pays off, as well grown calvus are always in demand.\n\nYou can leave fry with the parents for a couple of months if the tank is large enough. Move them to their own grow out tank when they are around half an inch. Keep the same hard, alkaline water there and feed small meals several times a day. Expect it to take a year or more before young calvus look like miniature adults."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Expecting fast growth from fry\n- Too few shells or crevices for the female\n- Soft, acidic water unsuited to Tanganyikan fish\n- Overfeeding rich foods\n- Mixing calvus with aggressive tankmates"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$calvus-cichlid$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use at least 55 gallons, with a long footprint. Cyprichromis swim in the middle and upper water, so open swimming space matters more than decor. Stack some rockwork at the back for males to claim, and leave the front open. A tight lid is important, as they can jump when startled.\n\nKeep the water hard and alkaline like Lake Tanganyika: pH 7.8 to 8.6, GH 10 to 25, and 76 to 82°F. Good filtration and steady water changes keep conditions stable.\n\nA 4 to 6 foot tank is ideal for a proper school, and many breeders keep them in 75 gallons or larger. Shell dwellers or a small group of peaceful bottom dwellers can share the tank, but avoid fish that dominate the upper water. A steady light schedule helps, since sudden lights on can startle the school into the glass. Some keepers leave a dim room light on before the tank lights come on."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a group", "text": "Keep a school of 8 or more, with more females than males. Males develop bright colors in the fins and tail, which vary by population. Females are plainer, silvery or brownish. Keeping one population together avoids mixing color forms.\n\nFeed small frozen foods such as cyclops, brine shrimp and mysis, along with a quality flake, two or three small meals a day. Cyprichromis have small mouths and do best on fine foods.\n\nSchools of 12 to 20 often look and behave most natural, and many breeders keep several males in a large school so no single male harasses females constantly. Males in a big group spread their displays out and each defends a small patch of open water near the rocks."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "Males claim small territories in open water near rocks and display to females with fins spread. When a female is ready, she follows the male. Spawning happens in midwater: the female releases eggs, which she catches in her mouth, and the male fertilizes them. Over the next days she will show a full, slightly bulging throat from holding."}, {"heading": "Holding females", "text": "Females commonly hold the eggs for about 3 weeks at 76 to 80°F. Broods are small, commonly around 10 to 20 fry. Holding females usually keep feeding lightly, but you may see them eat less.\n\nMany breeders move a holding female to a separate tank after about 2 weeks, so the fry are released somewhere safe. Move her gently in a container rather than a net to avoid stress. Some breeders leave fry to survive in the colony among the rocks, but fewer make it that way.\n\nA small floating breeder box or a 10 to 20 gallon tank with the same water works for the release. Keep the light soft and give her a few rocks for cover. Do not strip eggs or fry from her mouth unless you know the technique well; a calm female left alone usually releases on her own."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry are released fully formed, roughly half an inch long, and free swimming. Feed newly hatched brine shrimp and crushed flake two or three times a day. Do 20 to 30 percent water changes weekly, matching hardness and pH carefully.\n\nReturn the female to the group once the fry are released and she is eating well.\n\nFry tend to hang in midwater rather than hiding on the bottom, which is unusual among mouthbrooders and makes them easy to watch. Add fine foods where they swim, and use a sponge filter in the fry tank so food is not swept away. Several small feedings a day work better than one large one."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry grow steadily on brine shrimp and fine foods. Expect a few months to reach around 1.5 to 2 inches. Keep them in their own grow out tank until they can join the colony without being eaten. Males start showing color within a few months."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Too small a school or tank\n- Too many males\n- Netting holding females roughly\n- Mixing different color populations\n- Soft or acidic water\n- Skipping a lid on a tank with a nervous school"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$cyprichromis$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use at least 55 gallons, matching the minimum for this species. Build rockwork caves across the tank so fish can break sight lines. Keep water hard and alkaline: pH 7.8 to 8.6, GH 10 to 20, and 75 to 81°F. Strong filtration and weekly water changes keep water clean.\n\nA long tank, 4 feet or more, gives subordinate fish room to escape, and many keepers prefer 75 gallons or larger for a full colony. Arrange the rockwork into several separate piles so each male can hold a territory without seeing every other fish. Duboisi come from slightly deeper water than some Tropheus and are often said to be a little calmer, but aggression still needs managing."}, {"heading": "Choosing a colony", "text": "Tropheus duboisi are hard to sex. Males are often larger and more dominant, and females smaller. The safest approach is to buy a colony of 10 or more juveniles and let them grow up together. Remove extra males once they mature, keeping one or two.\n\nTropheus are aggressive toward their own kind. Larger groups spread aggression. Avoid adding single new fish later, which can cause fights.\n\nSome breeders check vents on larger juveniles to confirm sex, though this takes practice. When in doubt, keep the group together and remove extra males only once you see clear dominance and spawning activity. Signs of trouble include one fish hiding constantly, torn fins or a pale color; remove that fish to its own tank before it is badly hurt."}, {"heading": "Diet and conditioning", "text": "Tropheus are herbivores. Feed spirulina based flakes and pellets, and let algae grow on rocks. Avoid high protein foods like bloodworms and beef heart, which can lead to bloat, a serious problem in Tropheus. Feed small amounts two or three times a day.\n\nIf a fish stops eating, hangs at the surface or shows a swollen belly, act quickly, as bloat can spread through a colony. Clean water with frequent changes is the best prevention. Many breeders do 25 to 30 percent water changes weekly and avoid sudden diet changes. A small amount of frozen mysis once a week is about as rich as most experienced keepers go."}, {"heading": "Spawning and holding", "text": "Spawning happens near rocks. The female lays a few large eggs, picks them up, and the male fertilizes them. Broods are small, commonly around 5 to 15 eggs.\n\nFemales commonly hold for about 4 weeks at 76 to 80°F. Holding females may hide and eat little. Many breeders leave them in the colony, as stress from moving can cause the female to spit the eggs early.\n\nYou can spot a holding female by her full, squared off throat and the way she avoids eating or chews without swallowing. Note the date you first see her holding so you know when release is due. If you choose to move her near the end of the hold, do it gently in a container and leave her alone in a quiet tank with some cover. Return her to the colony soon after release, since Tropheus can have trouble rejoining a group after a long absence."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry are released fully formed, roughly half an inch long. They are black with white spots. Feed crushed spirulina flake and fine vegetable based foods. In a colony with good rockwork, many fry survive. You can also move fry to their own tank for faster growth.\n\nThe fry tank should have the same hard, alkaline water and plenty of small rocks. Do 20 to 30 percent water changes weekly, matching hardness and pH. Feed small amounts often and remove leftovers."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry grow steadily on vegetable foods. Expect a few months to reach around 1.5 inches. Juveniles keep their spots until they start changing into adult colors. Feed small amounts often and keep water clean.\n\nDuboisi juveniles are very popular with buyers for their spotted pattern, and grow out colonies can be kept together until they are big enough to rehome. Raise them as a group, since isolated juveniles are more likely to be bullied when introduced later."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Feeding high protein foods, which causes bloat\n- Too small a group, leading to fights\n- Adding single new fish later\n- Moving holding females roughly\n- Mixing different Tropheus species or color forms"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$tropheus-duboisi$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use at least 125 gallons, matching the minimum for this species, as adults can reach 13 inches. Build large caves with rock or pipe and use sand as substrate. Keep water hard and alkaline: pH 7.8 to 8.6, GH 10 to 20, and 75 to 82°F. Strong filtration and weekly water changes keep conditions stable.\n\nFrontosa are calm fish that dislike fast movement and bright light. Use moderate lighting and place the tank where people do not constantly walk past. Large rock caves or wide PVC pipe sections give holding females somewhere to retreat. Frontosa can be kept with other calm Tanganyikans, but tankmates small enough to fit in an adult's mouth will be eaten."}, {"heading": "Choosing a colony", "text": "Males are usually larger with a bigger hump on the head and longer fins, but these signs are not reliable on younger fish. Venting, checking the shape of the genital openings, is more reliable but takes practice. Many breeders buy 6 to 10 juveniles and let them grow up.\n\nFrontosa are slow to mature, often taking 2 to 3 years to breed. Keep one male with 3 to 5 females.\n\nOnce a dominant male emerges, he will display with fins spread and claim a cave. Extra males can be removed or kept in a very large tank. Look for several females spending time near the male's cave as a sign the colony is maturing."}, {"heading": "Diet and conditioning", "text": "Feed quality pellets, krill, mysis and shrimp once or twice a day. Avoid feeder fish, which can bring disease. A varied diet helps females fill with eggs.\n\nFeed moderate amounts so the fish clean up within a couple of minutes. Overfeeding fouls the water and can lead to health problems. Krill and mysis are good conditioning foods. Remove leftover food promptly, as frontosa are slow eaters compared with many cichlids."}, {"heading": "Spawning and holding", "text": "Spawning often happens in caves or at night, and you may not see it. The female lays eggs, picks them up, and the male fertilizes them. Clutches are commonly around 20 to 50 eggs.\n\nFemales commonly hold for about 4 to 6 weeks at 76 to 80°F. Holding females may hide and eat little. Many breeders leave them in the colony, as frontosa are generally calm.\n\nA holding female has a swollen throat and stays in or near a cave. Write down the date you first notice her holding so you know when release is due. If you want to move her for the release, do so near the end of the hold and gently, in a container. Many breeders say frontosa hold longer than most mouthbrooders, so be patient and avoid disturbing her."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry are released fully formed, roughly half an inch long. Feed newly hatched brine shrimp and crushed pellets two or three times a day. In a colony with good caves, many fry survive. Moving fry to their own tank helps them grow faster.\n\nFrontosa fry are big for cichlid fry and very hardy. Do 25 to 30 percent water changes weekly in the fry tank, matching hardness and pH. Use a sponge filter or prefilter so fry are not pulled in. Fry hold together in a loose group near the bottom and become more confident as they grow."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Frontosa grow slowly. Expect several months to reach 1.5 to 2 inches. Keep fry in a grow out tank with regular water changes and good feeding.\n\nFrontosa do not show their stripes and hump until they are much older. Keep fry together in a larger tank as they grow, and upgrade tank size as needed. Many breeders keep juveniles in 55 or 75 gallon tanks before moving them to larger tanks. Expect it to take a year or more to reach a size that buyers want, and several years before they breed themselves."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Expecting breeding from young fish\n- Too small a tank\n- Too many males\n- Feeding feeder fish\n- Moving holding females roughly"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$frontosa$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use at least 75 gallons, matching the minimum for this species. Build rockwork caves across the tank. Keep water hard and alkaline: pH 7.8 to 8.6, GH 10 to 25, and 76 to 82°F. Strong filtration and weekly water changes keep conditions stable.\n\nA 4 foot or longer tank gives subordinate fish room to escape. Many keepers prefer 100 gallons or more for a full colony. Arrange the rockwork into several piles so each male can hold his own space without seeing every other fish. Strong, clean water flow and high oxygen suit these fish, which come from the surf zone of Lake Tanganyika. Keep tankmates to other robust Tanganyikans or none at all."}, {"heading": "Choosing a colony", "text": "Tropheus moorii are hard to sex. Males are often larger and more dominant. Buy a colony of 12 or more juveniles of one population and let them grow up together. Remove extra males once they mature.\n\nTropheus fight with their own kind. Larger groups spread aggression. Avoid adding single new fish later.\n\nThere are many color forms of Tropheus moorii from different locations, and keeping one form per tank avoids crossbreeding. Some breeders check vents on larger juveniles to confirm sex, though this takes practice. Many keepers overstock slightly to spread aggression further. Watch for fish hiding constantly, with torn fins or pale coloring, and remove them to recover."}, {"heading": "Diet and conditioning", "text": "Tropheus are herbivores. Feed spirulina based flakes and pellets and let algae grow on rocks. Avoid high protein foods like bloodworms and beef heart, which can lead to bloat. Feed small amounts two or three times a day.\n\nIf a fish stops eating, hangs at the surface or shows a swollen belly, act quickly, as bloat can spread through a colony. Clean water and a steady diet are the best prevention. Many breeders do 25 to 30 percent water changes weekly. Avoid sudden diet changes, and keep frozen foods such as mysis to rare treats at most."}, {"heading": "Spawning and holding", "text": "Spawning happens near rocks. Broods are small, commonly around 5 to 15 eggs. Females commonly hold for about 4 weeks at 76 to 80°F. Holding females may hide and eat little. Many breeders leave them in the colony to avoid stress.\n\nSpawning often starts with the male circling the female and quivering near rocks. The female lays a few eggs at a time and picks them up, and the male fertilizes them. You can spot a holding female by her full throat and the way she avoids food. Note the date you first see her holding so you know when release is due. If you choose to move her near the end of the hold, do it gently in a container and return her to the colony soon after release."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry are released fully formed, roughly half an inch long. Feed crushed spirulina flake and fine vegetable based foods. In a colony with good rockwork, many fry survive. Moving fry to their own tank speeds growth.\n\nThe fry tank should have the same hard, alkaline water and plenty of small rocks. Do 20 to 30 percent water changes weekly, matching hardness and pH. Fry eat algae from rocks along with prepared foods, so let a little algae grow. Avoid rich foods for fry too."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry grow steadily on vegetable foods. Expect a few months to reach around 1.5 inches. Keep water clean and feed small amounts often.\n\nJuveniles often show different colors from adults, and color develops as they grow. Grow out colonies can be kept together until they are big enough to rehome. Raising them as a group helps them stay calm, as isolated juveniles are more likely to be bullied when introduced later. Expect to wait a year or so before they breed themselves."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Feeding high protein foods\n- Too small a group\n- Adding single new fish later\n- Moving holding females roughly\n- Mixing color forms"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$tropheus$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Maternal mouthbrooder (female holds the eggs)", "sexing": "Subtle; males often have more pointed fins with yellowish or gold edging", "group": "A group of 4 to 6 so pairs can form, or one pair in a species tank", "breeding_tank": "20 gallons, very soft acidic water, leaf litter, dim light, gentle filter", "conditioning": "Live and frozen foods such as daphnia, brine shrimp and small bloodworms", "trigger": "Very soft water near pH 5.5 to 6, warm 80 to 84°F, calm surroundings", "spawn": "Small broods; breeders commonly report a few dozen eggs or fewer", "eggs": "Female commonly holds for about 14 days at 80 to 84°F", "fry": "Released free swimming and well formed", "first_foods": "Newly hatched brine shrimp and microworms, with infusoria as backup", "grow_out": "Slow; several months to reach about 1 inch", "parents": "No care after release; move the female or fry to protect the young"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 20 gallon tank, the minimum for this species, works well for a small group or pair. Use a dark substrate, plenty of leaf litter, driftwood and floating plants to keep the light dim. Filtration should be very gentle, ideally an air driven sponge filter.\n\nChocolate gouramis come from peat swamp waters. The library range is pH 5.5 to 6.5 and GH 1 to 5, at 78 to 84°F. For breeding, aim for the soft, acidic end: pH around 5.5 to 6, very low hardness and conductivity, and 80 to 84°F. This usually means RO or rain water with leaf litter for tannins. Change water carefully, in small amounts, with water matched for temperature and chemistry."}, {"heading": "Sexing and choosing fish", "text": "Sexing is subtle. Males often have a more pointed dorsal fin and yellowish or gold edges on the dorsal and anal fins, while females tend to be plainer. These differences are easier to see in mature, well conditioned fish. Many breeders keep a group of 4 to 6 and let pairs form, then move a pair to a species tank. Avoid tankmates; chocolate gouramis are shy and easily outcompeted.\n\nBuy from a source that keeps them in soft water if you can, since fish moved suddenly from hard water often struggle. Quarantine new fish carefully in the same soft water before adding them, because chocolate gouramis are prone to skin and gill infections when stressed."}, {"heading": "Conditioning", "text": "Feed small live and frozen foods: daphnia, brine shrimp, small bloodworms and mosquito larvae. Many specimens refuse dry food at first. Feed small portions two or three times a day and remove leftovers, as decaying food quickly harms these sensitive fish. Watch for any signs of disease, which spreads quickly in this species, and keep the tank stable rather than chasing perfect numbers."}, {"heading": "Spawning and holding", "text": "Spawning usually happens near the bottom. The male courts the female with fins spread, and after the eggs are released and fertilized, the female collects them in her mouth. Breeders commonly report broods of a few dozen eggs or fewer.\n\nThe female commonly holds for about 14 days at 80 to 84°F. During this time she eats little or nothing and may hide. Disturbance can make her swallow or spit the brood, so keep the tank quiet and avoid moving her if you can.\n\nWrite down the date you first notice the female holding so you know when release is due. If you must move her, do it near the end of the hold, gently, in a container of tank water rather than a net, into a small tank with identical water."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "When the fry are released, they are free swimming and fairly well developed. Feed newly hatched brine shrimp and microworms several times a day, with infusoria as a backup for the smallest fry. Keep water quality excellent with small, careful water changes every couple of days, matched for temperature and chemistry.\n\nMany breeders move the female to her own tank before release so the fry are safe, or move the fry out after release. Fry are sensitive to changes, so treat their tank just as carefully as the adults' tank."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry grow slowly. Continue with brine shrimp and add crushed frozen foods over time. Expect several months to reach about 1 inch. Keep the water soft and acidic, and avoid overcrowding. Losses are common even for skilled breeders, so expect to try several times.\n\nA small, mature grow out tank with leaf litter, floating plants and an established sponge filter is better than a bare new tank. Feed several small meals a day and keep the population modest so water quality stays high."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Hard or neutral water\n- Big, sudden water changes\n- Busy tanks with active tankmates\n- Moving a holding female too early\n- Feeding only dry foods"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$chocolate-gourami$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Cave spawner; male builds a small bubble nest inside the cave", "sexing": "Males have brighter fins with colored edges; females plainer with darker stripes", "group": "One pair in a species tank", "breeding_tank": "10 gallons, very soft acidic water, small caves, leaf litter, dim light", "conditioning": "Live foods such as daphnia, cyclops, microworms and baby brine shrimp", "trigger": "Very soft, acidic water near pH 5.5, low light and a suitable cave", "spawn": "Small clutches; breeders commonly report a few dozen eggs or fewer", "eggs": "Commonly hatch in about 1 to 2 days at 77 to 80°F", "fry": "Free swimming about 4 to 7 days after hatching; very small", "first_foods": "Infusoria or paramecium, then microworms and baby brine shrimp", "grow_out": "Several months to reach about half an inch", "parents": "Male guards eggs; remove adults or fry once fry are free swimming"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 10 gallon tank, the minimum for this species, is enough for a pair. Use a dark substrate or bare bottom, leaf litter, small caves such as tiny clay pots or film canisters, and floating plants to keep the light dim. Use a gentle sponge filter.\n\nLicorice gouramis come from blackwater peat swamps. The library range is pH 5.5 to 6.5 and GH 1 to 5, at 75 to 82°F. For breeding, aim for the soft, acidic end: pH around 5.5, very low hardness and conductivity, and 77 to 80°F. This usually means RO or rain water with leaf litter. Change water carefully, in small amounts, with matched water.\n\nPlace two or three caves around the tank so the male can choose. Many breeders lay the caves on their side with the opening facing the front so they can glance in without disturbing the fish. Keep the tank in a quiet room with no vibration or sudden light changes."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning a pair", "text": "Males have brighter fins with colored edges, which look more striking when they display. Females are plainer, with darker horizontal stripes. Keep one pair in a species tank so the male can claim a cave without competition.\n\nMany fish sold as licorice gouramis are other Parosphromenus species, which look very similar. Keep fish from one source together and avoid mixing species or populations.\n\nFeed small live foods such as daphnia, cyclops, microworms and baby brine shrimp two or three times a day. Many licorice gouramis refuse dry food. Feed small portions and remove leftovers, since decaying food harms these sensitive fish quickly."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "The male claims a small cave and builds a small bubble nest on the ceiling. He displays to the female with fins spread, and the pair spawn upside down in the cave. Eggs stick to the ceiling and the male guards them. Clutches are small; breeders commonly report a few dozen eggs or fewer.\n\nYou may not see spawning at all, since it happens inside the cave. A male hanging near the cave entrance and chasing the female away is a good sign."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fry", "text": "At 77 to 80°F eggs commonly hatch in about 1 to 2 days. The fry hang in the cave for a few more days, becoming free swimming about 4 to 7 days after hatching. The male usually guards the brood until then. Avoid checking the cave often, as disturbance can make the male eat the eggs.\n\nOnce the fry are free swimming, many breeders move the adults out so the fry are safe."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "The fry are very small. Offer infusoria or paramecium several times a day for the first week, then add microworms and newly hatched brine shrimp. Keep water quality excellent with small, careful water changes every couple of days, matched for temperature and chemistry. A lightly planted tank with leaf litter grows microorganisms between feedings.\n\nKeep the fry tank covered to hold warmth and humidity, as many labyrinth fry are sensitive to cold air while their labyrinth organ develops. Start brine shrimp only once the fry can clearly take it, usually after the first week."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry grow slowly. Continue with brine shrimp and microworms, adding crushed frozen foods such as cyclops over time. Expect several months to reach about half an inch, and longer before males show their adult colors. Keep the water soft and acidic, and avoid overcrowding."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Hard or neutral water\n- Bright light and no cover\n- Checking the cave constantly\n- Mixing similar looking Parosphromenus species\n- No live foods ready for tiny fry"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$licorice-gourami$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "How cuckoo catfish breed", "text": "When mouthbrooding cichlids spawn, cuckoo catfish rush in and release their own eggs among the cichlid eggs. The cichlid female picks up the catfish eggs with her own. Breeders report that the catfish eggs hatch first, and the young catfish eat the cichlid eggs or fry in the female's mouth. The cichlid then releases catfish young instead of her own.\n\nCuckoo catfish come from Lake Tanganyika, where they use local mouthbrooding cichlids as hosts. In aquariums they have been bred with a range of African mouthbrooders. This strategy means you cannot breed them without a regularly spawning host colony."}, {"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use at least 50 gallons, matching the minimum for this species. Build rockwork caves for the catfish and space for the cichlids to spawn. Keep the water hard and alkaline: pH 7.8 to 8.6, GH 10 to 25, and 75 to 82°F. Strong filtration and weekly water changes keep water clean.\n\nA long tank of 75 gallons or more gives room for both a host colony and a group of catfish. Use sand and rockwork with plenty of caves and gaps, as the catfish are shy during the day. Keep lighting moderate and feed some food after lights out so the catfish eat well."}, {"heading": "Choosing catfish and hosts", "text": "Keep 3 or more cuckoo catfish, matching the minimum group size. Females are larger and plumper, while males are slimmer and show a visible genital papilla near the vent.\n\nHosts are mouthbrooding cichlids that spawn regularly. Lake Tanganyika and Lake Malawi mouthbrooders are commonly used. Choose a host species that breeds readily in your tank.\n\nMany breeders keep 5 or more catfish so several are ready whenever the hosts spawn. Choose host cichlids that are compatible with the catfish and with your water. Avoid very aggressive or very large cichlids that may harm the catfish. Keep host species pure and avoid mixing species that could crossbreed."}, {"heading": "Conditioning", "text": "Feed sinking pellets, frozen bloodworms and brine shrimp once or twice a day, usually after lights out when catfish are most active. Make sure the catfish get enough food, as cichlids may eat most of it."}, {"heading": "Spawning: what you will see", "text": "When host cichlids spawn, the catfish rush in and swim through the spawning site, releasing eggs. This is quick and easy to miss. The host female then holds the eggs as usual.\n\nYou may notice catfish gathering around spawning cichlids, darting into the spawning site and then quickly retreating. Over time, you may notice host females releasing catfish young instead of cichlid fry, which confirms success."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "The host female releases the young after holding. Many breeders move holding females to a separate tank before release so the catfish young are safe. Feed baby brine shrimp and crushed sinking foods. Do weekly water changes, matching hardness and pH.\n\nCatfish young hide in rockwork and grow steadily. Expect several months to reach about 2 inches.\n\nNote the date each host female starts holding. Moving a host female to a separate tank near the end of her hold lets you see what she releases. Catfish young are usually darker and spotted, quite different from cichlid fry. Return the host female to the colony once she has released and is eating well.\n\nKeep catfish young in a tank with sand and small caves, and feed several times a day, including after lights out."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- No regularly spawning host cichlids\n- Too few catfish\n- Not feeding enough for the catfish\n- Leaving host females in the main tank before release\n- Soft or acidic water"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$synodontis-multipunctatus$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Cave spawner (male guards eggs and fry inside the cave)", "sexing": "Males have broader heads and more bristles on pectoral spines and cheeks; females rounder from above", "group": "1 male to 2 or 3 females, or several of each with a cave for every male", "breeding_tank": "40 gallon long tank, strong flow, several snug caves facing the current", "conditioning": "Meaty foods like bloodworms, shrimp, mysis and quality sinking pellets for 4 to 8 weeks", "trigger": "Settled adults, warm water, strong current, high oxygen and regular large water changes", "spawn": "Commonly reported around 15 to 40 eggs per clutch", "eggs": "Hatch in about 5 to 7 days at 82 to 84°F, guarded by the male", "fry": "Live off a large yolk sac for about 10 to 14 days more, then leave the cave and graze", "first_foods": "Sinking pellets, crushed wafers, frozen bloodworms and baby brine shrimp", "grow_out": "Slow: often several months to reach about 1 inch", "parents": "Male guards well; if he keeps ejecting eggs, hatch them in a tumbler or mesh basket"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 40 gallon long tank, in line with our care data, is plenty for one male and a couple of females. What matters most is water quality, current and caves. Run a canister or strong sponge filter plus a powerhead so water moves across the caves, and add an air stone for oxygen.\n\nKeep the temperature around 82 to 84°F, within the 78 to 86°F range in our care data. Soft to moderately hard water, pH 6.0 to 7.5, works. Offer several clay or slate caves with one open end, just wide enough for the male to turn in, placed so the entrance faces the flow."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning breeders", "text": "Start with adult fish, usually 3 inches or more and a couple of years old. Mature males show broader, heavier heads and more noticeable bristles (odontodes) on the pectoral spines and behind the gill covers. Females look rounder and fuller from above and have narrower heads. Young fish are hard to sex, so buying 4 to 6 juveniles and growing them up is a good way to get pairs.\n\nThese are meat eaters at heart. Feed sinking pellets, frozen bloodworms, mysis and chopped shrimp daily, with a vegetable wafer now and then. Give conditioning 4 to 8 weeks and keep up weekly water changes of 30 to 50 percent."}, {"heading": "What spawning looks like", "text": "A male claims a cave and spends most of his time there. When a female is ready, he lures her in and they spawn inside, sometimes over a day or more. Afterward the female leaves and the male stays at the entrance, fanning the clutch with his fins.\n\nMany breeders find big water changes with slightly cooler water and a stronger current help start things. Don't keep checking the cave with a flashlight; a disturbed new male may eject or eat the eggs."}, {"heading": "Eggs and the male's care", "text": "Clutches are commonly reported at about 15 to 40 large yellowish eggs, stuck together in a clump. At 82 to 84°F they hatch in roughly 5 to 7 days. The male keeps fanning and cleaning them the whole time.\n\nIf a male repeatedly kicks out eggs, gently move the clump to an egg tumbler or a mesh breeder box in strong flow, and remove any white, fungused eggs. A drop of air in a tumbler is enough to keep eggs rolling without damaging them."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Newly hatched fry carry a large orange yolk sac and stay in the cave for another 10 to 14 days or so. Do not feed until the yolk is nearly gone, or you will only foul the water.\n\n- Week 1 after the yolk is used: crushed sinking pellets, wafer crumbs and frozen baby brine shrimp, a little twice a day.\n- Weeks 2 to 4: add chopped bloodworms and small sinking pellets.\n- Do daily small water changes or 30 to 50 percent every few days, and siphon leftovers.\n\nKeep fry at the same warm temperature with good flow. A bare bottom tank or a breeder net in the main tank makes cleaning easy."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Hypancistrus are slow. Expect several months for fry to reach about an inch and longer to reach a size people will buy. Feed several small meals a day and keep water very clean, because poor water stunts them quickly. Give growing fish small caves or pieces of pipe so they can hide. Keep L333 lines separate from other Hypancistrus so you never produce hybrids, and label your fish honestly when you pass them on."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Caves that are too big or too small. The male should fit snugly with just enough room to turn.\n- Weak flow and low oxygen. These are river fish and often won't spawn in still water.\n- Feeding fry before the yolk sac is gone.\n- Keeping several Hypancistrus types together, which risks hybrid fry.\n- Disturbing a new male so he abandons or eats the clutch."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$king-tiger-pleco$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use at least a 30 gallon tank with fine sand, stacked rocks and a few caves, like a small Lake Tanganyika setup. Our care data lists 75 to 82°F, pH 7.8 to 8.6 and hard water (GH 10 to 25), and breeding attempts should stay right in that range. Many keepers aim for about 77 to 79°F.\n\nBecause the adults give no care, many breeders place a shallow dish filled with glass marbles or coarse gravel near the caves. Eggs scattered over it fall between the marbles where the adults can't reach them. Gentle filtration and a sponge prefilter keep eggs and fry out of the intake."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning breeders", "text": "Sexing is difficult. Mature males are usually slimmer, and when you look closely at the vent, males show a small pointed genital papilla. Ripe females are noticeably plumper. The easiest route is to keep a group of 4 to 6 adults and let them sort themselves out.\n\nThese are omnivores. Feed sinking pellets, frozen bloodworms, brine shrimp and mysis, plus some algae or vegetable based food, once or twice a day for several weeks. Keep the group well fed so they are less likely to hunt eggs."}, {"heading": "What spawning looks like", "text": "Spawning in this species is not well documented in home tanks. Reports describe fish chasing each other around the rockwork, often at night or early morning, and eggs turning up on the substrate or in the marble dish afterward. You may only discover a spawn when you see eggs or tiny fry.\n\nCheck the egg trap each morning with a flashlight. Regular water changes with matched hard water are the most common factor hobbyists mention before a spawn."}, {"heading": "Eggs", "text": "Collect any eggs you find into a small hatching container with water from the breeding tank, an air stone and a matching temperature. Breeders report that eggs hatch in roughly 2 to 3 days at about 77 to 79°F.\n\nInfertile eggs turn white and grow fungus quickly. Remove them with a pipette every day so the fungus doesn't spread to good eggs. Gentle aeration helps keep the remaining eggs healthy."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Newly hatched fry live on their yolk sac for a few days. Once they start moving around and the yolk is gone, begin feeding.\n\n- First 2 weeks: baby brine shrimp and microworms, two or three small feedings a day.\n- Weeks 3 to 6: add finely crushed sinking pellets and frozen foods.\n- Change 20 to 30 percent of the water every few days with hard, matched water, and siphon leftover food.\n\nFry are shy, so give them small hiding places such as pieces of pipe or shells."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Juveniles of this species are commonly reported to grow very slowly. Plan on many months before they look like small adults. Keep them in a dedicated grow out tank with steady water quality, several small meals a day and plenty of cover. Keep the young away from other Synodontis so there is no question about what they are when you pass them on. Once they reach about an inch, move them onto small sinking pellets and frozen foods, and keep water changes regular so hardness and pH stay steady."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Leaving eggs with the adults, who will eat them.\n- Soft or acidic water. This is a hard water lake species.\n- Mixing with other Synodontis, especially the cuckoo catfish, and causing confusion over fry.\n- Overfeeding tiny fry, which fouls their small container."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$synodontis-lucipinnis$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set intro = $g$Otocinclus are rarely bred in home tanks, and almost all of them sold are wild caught. A well fed group in a mature, planted tank will sometimes lay small eggs on leaves and glass. The hard part is the fry: they are tiny, need constant algae and biofilm, and are lost easily to dirty water or starvation. That is why breeding them is an expert level project, and why every raised batch is worth logging.$g$, facts = $g${"method": "Egg depositor: single eggs stuck to leaves and glass, no parental care", "sexing": "Females larger and broader, males slimmer with a small genital papilla", "group": "A group of 6 or more adults", "breeding_tank": "20 gallon or larger mature planted tank with algae, biofilm and a sponge filter", "conditioning": "Plenty of algae and biofilm plus blanched zucchini and algae wafers for several weeks", "trigger": "Large, slightly cooler water changes after good feeding", "spawn": "Reports vary, commonly a few dozen eggs laid over a day or two", "eggs": "Commonly reported to hatch in about 2 to 3 days at 75 to 78°F", "fry": "Very small fry that start grazing within a few days", "first_foods": "Algae and biofilm on surfaces, green water, powdered algae foods", "grow_out": "Several months to reach about an inch", "parents": "No care; adults don't usually hunt fry but compete with them for algae"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use a 20 gallon or larger tank that has been running for months, with live plants, rocks and glass covered in soft algae and biofilm. The fry depend on this growth, so a new, clean tank is the wrong choice.\n\nRun a sponge filter so fry are not sucked up. Our care data lists 72 to 79°F, pH 6.0 to 7.5 and soft to medium water. Many breeders keep about 75 to 78°F. Leave the back and sides of the glass uncleaned to grow food."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning breeders", "text": "Buy a group of at least 6 healthy, well fed fish and quarantine them, since wild caught otos often arrive thin. Females are larger and rounder from above. Males are slimmer and, up close, show a small genital papilla behind the vent.\n\nFeed heavily for several weeks: natural algae, blanched zucchini or cucumber, and algae wafers, with an occasional small meaty treat. Fat, rounded bellies are a sign the fish are ready. Thin fish will not spawn, and many die in the first weeks, so get them strong before trying to breed."}, {"heading": "What spawning looks like", "text": "Spawning is often triggered by a large water change with slightly cooler water, imitating rain. Males chase females, and pairs take up a T position similar to Corydoras. The female then places single, sticky, clear to pale eggs on plant leaves, wood or glass. This can continue over a day or two.\n\nYou may only notice the small eggs afterward. Look on the undersides of broad leaves and on the glass near the water line."}, {"heading": "Eggs", "text": "Eggs are commonly reported to hatch in about 2 to 3 days at 75 to 78°F. Adults usually leave the eggs alone, so many breeders simply leave them in the planted tank. If you prefer to move them, cut the leaf or gently roll eggs off the glass with a finger and place them in a container of tank water with a light air stone.\n\nRemove any white, fuzzy eggs each day so fungus doesn't spread."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry are tiny and spend their time grazing on surfaces. Starvation is the most common cause of loss, so food must be available all day.\n\n- Days 1 to 7: algae and biofilm on surfaces, green water and powdered algae food.\n- Weeks 2 to 4: add finely crushed algae wafers, blanched vegetables and a little baby brine shrimp or microworms.\n- Change 10 to 20 percent of water every couple of days, matching temperature and chemistry closely.\n\nKeep rocks and leaves from the main tank cycling into the fry tank to supply fresh algae."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry grow slowly and take several months to reach about an inch. Keep feeding several times a day, keep nitrates low and avoid sudden swings. Losses are common in the first month even in good conditions, so a batch that survives two months is a real result. Keep a steady supply of algae rocks or blanched vegetables in the grow out tank. Once the young reach about half an inch they become much hardier and can share a quiet planted tank with small, peaceful fish."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Trying to breed thin, newly imported fish.\n- A spotless new tank with no algae for fry.\n- Big, sudden water changes in the fry tank.\n- Strong filters without a sponge cover.\n- Assuming every oto is the same species. Several look alike, so record what you have as best you can."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$otocinclus$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Substrate spawner: eggs laid on open glass or wood, guarded by the male", "sexing": "Mature males grow bristles (odontodes) along the sides of the snout; females lack them", "group": "One pair or a small group with more than one female", "breeding_tank": "30 gallons or more, gentle current, mature with algae, wood and plants", "conditioning": "Algae, biofilm, algae wafers and blanched vegetables for several weeks", "trigger": "Good conditioning and cooler water changes; eggs often placed near the water flow", "spawn": "Commonly reported around 40 to 60 eggs", "eggs": "Commonly reported to hatch in about 7 to 10 days at 75 to 78°F", "fry": "Thin, tiny fry that start grazing soon after hatching", "first_foods": "Biofilm, green water, powdered algae food and blanched vegetables", "grow_out": "Slow; several months to reach 2 inches", "parents": "Male guards eggs; after hatching move fry or keep them where food is plentiful"}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$twig-catfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Cave spawner (male guards eggs and fry inside the cave)", "sexing": "Males have broader heads and bristles behind the gill covers; females rounder from above", "group": "1 male to 1 or 2 females, or a group with a cave for every fish", "breeding_tank": "30 gallon tank, strong flow, extra aeration, snug slate or clay caves", "conditioning": "Frozen bloodworms, mysis, chopped shrimp and meaty sinking pellets for 6 to 8 weeks", "trigger": "Warm, very clean, well oxygenated water with strong current and steady water changes", "spawn": "Small clutches, commonly reported around 7 to 15 large eggs", "eggs": "Hatch in about 5 to 7 days at 82 to 84°F", "fry": "Live on a yolk sac for about another 2 weeks before feeding", "first_foods": "Crushed meaty sinking pellets, frozen baby brine shrimp, chopped bloodworms", "grow_out": "Very slow; many months to reach 1.5 to 2 inches", "parents": "Male guards well once experienced; young males may eject eggs, so be ready with a tumbler"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 30 gallon tank, in line with our care data, is enough for a pair or trio. These fish come from the fast flowing, warm Rio Xingu, so think current and oxygen. Use a strong filter plus a powerhead, and add air stones.\n\nFor spawning, keep the water at 82 to 84°F, inside the 79 to 86°F range in our care data, with soft water and pH 6.0 to 7.2. Provide several slate or clay caves just wide enough for an adult to turn around in, with one open end facing the flow. A sand or bare bottom is easiest to keep clean."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning breeders", "text": "Use adults of at least 2.5 to 3 inches, which often means fish 2 or more years old. Mature males have broader, heavier heads and noticeable bristles (odontodes) behind the gill covers and on the pectoral spines. Females are rounder through the belly when seen from above, with slimmer heads.\n\nZebras are meat eaters. Feed frozen bloodworms, mysis, chopped shrimp and good sinking pellets once or twice a day. Do 30 to 50 percent water changes weekly. Many breeders condition for 6 to 8 weeks before expecting spawns."}, {"heading": "What spawning looks like", "text": "The male claims a cave and calls a female in. Spawning happens inside and can take a day or more. When it is done, the female leaves and the male stays in the cave, often blocking the entrance and fanning with his fins.\n\nAvoid shining lights into the cave. Young or nervous males may abandon or eat their first few clutches, and that usually improves with experience."}, {"heading": "Eggs and hatching", "text": "Clutches are small, commonly reported at about 7 to 15 large, pale yellow eggs. At 82 to 84°F, eggs hatch in about 5 to 7 days.\n\nIf the male ejects eggs, move them to an egg tumbler or a mesh basket hung in strong flow, and remove any white or fuzzy eggs. Many breeders leave clutches with good fathers, since males do a better job than most tumblers."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry carry a large yolk sac and stay in the cave for roughly another 2 weeks. Begin feeding when the yolk is nearly gone and fry move out to graze.\n\n- Weeks 1 to 2 of feeding: crushed meaty sinking pellets and frozen baby brine shrimp, twice a day.\n- Weeks 3 to 6: chopped bloodworms and small sinking pellets.\n- Change 20 to 30 percent of the water every 2 to 3 days and siphon leftovers.\n\nKeep fry as warm and well oxygenated as the adults."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Zebra plecos grow slowly. It takes many months for fry to reach 1.5 to 2 inches, the size most people want to buy. Several small meals a day and very clean water make the biggest difference. Provide small caves or pipe pieces in the grow out tank. Keep zebras away from other Hypancistrus at all times, since they can hybridize, and pure L046 lines are what breeders want.\n\nHealthy young zebras are active, bold feeders with crisp black and white bands. If fry look pinched or hide constantly, check temperature, oxygen and nitrates before anything else."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Caves that are too large or too small.\n- Low oxygen or weak current.\n- Water that is too cool for this warm water species.\n- Housing with other Hypancistrus, which risks hybrids.\n- Overfeeding tiny fry before the yolk is gone, which fouls the water."}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"q": "How many eggs do zebra plecos lay?", "a": "Clutches are small, commonly about 7 to 15 eggs. Young pairs often lay fewer."}, {"q": "How long do zebra pleco eggs take to hatch?", "a": "About 5 to 7 days at 82 to 84°F. Fry then live on their yolk for around 2 more weeks."}, {"q": "How do you sex zebra plecos?", "a": "Adult males have broader heads and more bristles behind the gill covers and on the pectoral fins. Females are rounder when viewed from above."}, {"q": "Why are zebra plecos so expensive?", "a": "Brazil has long banned wild exports and the species is listed under CITES, small clutches and slow growth limit captive supply, and demand stays high."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$zebra-pleco$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Egg scatterer, no parental care", "sexing": "Females deeper and rounder in the belly; males slimmer", "group": "One pair, or a small group of 3 to 4 of each sex", "breeding_tank": "5 to 10 gallon bare tank, spawning mops or fine plants, dim light", "conditioning": "Live or frozen foods like brine shrimp, daphnia and blackworms for 1 to 2 weeks", "trigger": "Very soft, acidic water near pH 5.5 to 6.0, dim light, usually spawning at dusk", "spawn": "Commonly reported in the low hundreds", "eggs": "Hatch in about 24 to 36 hours at 78 to 82°F; eggs are light sensitive", "fry": "Free swimming about 4 to 5 days after spawning; extremely small", "first_foods": "Infusoria or paramecium, then vinegar eels or microworms, then baby brine shrimp", "grow_out": "About 2 to 3 months to reach 0.75 to 1 inch", "parents": "Adults eat eggs; remove them right after spawning"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use a small bare tank of 5 to 10 gallons. Cover the bottom with spawning mops, java moss or a mesh grid so eggs fall out of reach. Use a gentle sponge filter or just an air stone. Cover the sides with paper or dark material, as cardinals spawn in dim light.\n\nWater is the key. Use reverse osmosis or rain water with almost no hardness (GH and KH near zero) and a pH of about 5.5 to 6.0, which sits inside the 5.5 to 6.8 range in our care data. Peat or leaf litter helps soften and acidify. Keep the temperature at about 78 to 82°F."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning breeders", "text": "Pick mature fish over an inch long. Females are deeper and rounder, especially from the side, while males are slimmer with a straighter belly line. Separate males and females for 1 to 2 weeks and feed live or frozen brine shrimp, daphnia and chopped blackworms several times a day.\n\nPut a well rounded female and the best male into the breeding tank in the evening. Some breeders use small groups of each sex instead."}, {"heading": "What spawning looks like", "text": "Cardinals usually spawn at dusk or in the dark. The male chases the female into the plants, and the pair release small adhesive eggs that stick to plants or fall through the mesh. Spawns are commonly reported in the low hundreds of eggs.\n\nRemove the adults as soon as you see eggs, or the next morning, because they will eat them."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus prevention", "text": "Eggs and fry are very light sensitive, so keep the tank dark. Eggs hatch in about 24 to 36 hours at 78 to 82°F, and the fry hang on surfaces until they become free swimming around day 4 to 5 after spawning.\n\nInfertile eggs turn white and fuzzy. In very soft, acidic water fungus is less of a problem, which is one reason the low pH matters. Siphon out bad eggs gently if you can do it without disturbing the rest."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry are tiny and need the smallest foods for the first week.\n\n- Days 1 to 7 of free swimming: infusoria or paramecium cultures, several times a day.\n- Week 2: vinegar eels or microworms.\n- Week 3 on: newly hatched baby brine shrimp.\n\nRaise the light slowly over the first two weeks. Start small water changes of about 10 percent every couple of days after the first week, using the same soft water, and slowly allow a little more hardness as fry grow."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry show the start of their blue stripe after a few weeks. Expect about 2 to 3 months for them to reach 0.75 to 1 inch. Feed small amounts of baby brine shrimp and fine flakes several times a day, and move them to a larger tank with gentle filtration once they are eating well.\n\nKeep the water soft while they grow, and only raise hardness slowly over weeks if you plan to move them into regular tank water. Sorting larger fry from smaller ones helps the slower growers get their share of food."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Water that is too hard. Eggs often fail in anything but very soft water.\n- Bright light on eggs and fry.\n- Leaving the parents in the tank.\n- Starting fry on baby brine shrimp, which is too large at first.\n- Moving fry into hard tap water too quickly, which stresses them badly."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$cardinal-tetra$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "What is known", "text": "Green neons come from blackwater habitats in South America. Spawns in home tanks are uncommon, and detailed reports are few. Breeders who have succeeded generally used methods similar to cardinal tetra breeding. The guidance here follows that approach, and any numbers should be seen as a starting point."}, {"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use a small tank of around 5 gallons with dark sides, a dark bottom, and clumps of java moss or spawning mops. A gentle sponge filter or air stone is enough.\n\nUse soft water from reverse osmosis or rain, with GH near zero and pH about 5.5 to 6.0, within the 5.5 to 6.5 range in our care data. Leaf litter or peat helps. Keep the temperature around 78 to 82°F, inside our care range of 75 to 84°F."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning breeders", "text": "Sexing is difficult in these tiny fish. Ripe females look fuller and rounder from the side, while males stay slim. Condition a group for 1 to 2 weeks on tiny live or frozen foods such as daphnia, cyclops and baby brine shrimp, and keep them in stable soft water.\n\nMove the best looking fish into the breeding tank in the evening, either as a pair or as a small group.\n\nChoose fish that are active, fully colored and eating well. Wild caught green neons can arrive thin, so quarantine new fish and give them a few weeks to settle in soft water before conditioning. A group of 8 or more, as our care data suggests, keeps them calm and confident."}, {"heading": "Spawning and eggs", "text": "Spawning is reported to happen in dim light or early in the morning, with eggs scattered in fine plants or moss. Remove adults as soon as possible.\n\nKeep the tank dark, since eggs and fry are light sensitive. Breeders report hatching in about a day at 78 to 82°F. Remove white, fungused eggs if you can do it without disturbing the rest. Very soft, acidic water helps prevent fungus.\n\nIf you can't see eggs, leave the tank dark and untouched for a few days and then look closely for tiny fry clinging to the glass or moss. Many spawns of small tetras are only noticed this way. Keep the hatching tank at the same temperature and chemistry as the spawning tank."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry are extremely small and need microscopic foods first.\n\n- First week of free swimming: infusoria or paramecium, several times a day.\n- Week 2: vinegar eels or microworms.\n- Week 3 on: newly hatched baby brine shrimp.\n\nDo small, careful water changes of 10 percent every few days with the same soft water. Raise the light slowly as fry grow. A few small snails can help clean up leftover food."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry grow slowly and take several months to reach adult size, usually under an inch. Feed baby brine shrimp and fine crushed foods several times a day. As they color up, you can move them into a larger tank with gentle flow and the same soft, acidic water. Keep detailed notes on water values and dates, as reports for this species are scarce and useful to others."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Hard or neutral water.\n- Bright light on eggs and fry.\n- Food that is too large for the fry.\n- Leaving adults in with the eggs.\n- Big water changes that swing the pH.\n- Using tap water softened only partly. Measure GH and pH rather than guessing."}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"q": "Have green neon tetras been bred in aquariums?", "a": "Yes, but rarely. A small number of hobbyists report spawns in very soft, acidic water."}, {"q": "How do you tell male and female green neon tetras apart?", "a": "It is hard. Ripe females look fuller and rounder, while males stay slim."}, {"q": "What water do green neon tetras need to breed?", "a": "Very soft, acidic blackwater, with a pH around 5.5 to 6.0 and almost no hardness."}, {"q": "What do green neon tetra fry eat?", "a": "Infusoria or paramecium at first, then vinegar eels, and baby brine shrimp after about two weeks."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$green-neon-tetra$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Most breeders use a 30 to 40 gallon tall, bare bottomed tank for a single pair, even though our care data lists 75 gallons for keeping a group. A bare tank is easy to keep spotless. Use one or two sponge filters, a heater and a ceramic spawning cone, slate or a section of pipe stood upright.\n\nKeep the temperature at 82 to 86°F, within our care range of 82 to 86°F. Water should be soft and acidic. Many breeders lower the pH to about 6.0 to 6.5 for spawning, inside our care range of 6.0 to 7.0, with very low hardness. Very hard water often stops eggs from hatching."}, {"heading": "Choosing a pair", "text": "Sexing discus by looks is unreliable. Males are sometimes larger with thicker lips and a steeper forehead, but that's not consistent. The surest way is the breeding tube that appears just before spawning: the female's is short and blunt, the male's thinner and pointed.\n\nThe easiest route is to grow 5 or more young discus together and let a pair form. A pair stays near each other, cleans surfaces together and chases other fish away. Move them to their own tank once they are clearly bonded."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and spawning", "text": "Feed several times a day with beefheart mix, frozen bloodworms, quality pellets and flakes. Do 30 to 50 percent water changes daily or every other day with matched soft water.\n\nBefore spawning, the pair cleans the cone, shivers, and shows their breeding tubes. The female lays rows of eggs up the cone, and the male follows to fertilize them. Spawns are commonly reported around 100 to 300 eggs, sometimes more."}, {"heading": "Eggs and wrigglers", "text": "Both parents fan the eggs and remove bad ones. At 84 to 86°F, eggs hatch in about 2 to 3 days. The hatched wrigglers stay attached to the cone and are moved around by the parents.\n\nAbout 5 to 6 days after spawning, fry become free swimming and swarm onto the parents' sides. Keep the light low and avoid disturbing the pair during this time. Young pairs may eat the eggs a few times before they get it right. Be patient."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "For the first week, fry feed on mucus the parents produce on their skin. This is normal and important.\n\n- Days 5 to 7 of free swimming: start offering newly hatched baby brine shrimp a few times a day.\n- Weeks 2 to 3: baby brine shrimp several times a day, plus finely crushed flake.\n- Week 3 to 4: remove the parents or the fry, as the parents' mucus runs low and fry can nip at them.\n\nKeep doing water changes of 30 to 50 percent daily with matched water, and siphon leftovers carefully."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Discus fry grow fast with lots of food and clean water. Expect roughly 2 to 3 months of heavy feeding for them to reach 1 to 2 inches. Feed several times a day, change water daily, and sort fry by size so smaller ones aren't left behind. A bare tank with sponge filters keeps cleaning simple. Keep water warm, around 82 to 86°F, through grow out.\n\nHealthy fry are round, active and dark eyed. If some stay thin or hang near the surface, they are often not getting enough food, so offer more frequent, smaller meals and move the biggest fish to a separate tank."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Water that is too hard, which stops eggs from hatching.\n- Moving a pair too often, which stresses them.\n- Removing eggs from young pairs too quickly. Let them learn.\n- Infrequent water changes. Discus are very sensitive to poor water.\n- Raising fry without the parents. It is possible but very difficult; leave fry with parents if you can."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$discus$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "Use 20 gallons or more, as our care data suggests, with soft sand so they can dig. Plant densely and add floating plants with long roots, plus caves and leaf litter. Keep the lights dim.\n\nFor spawning, use soft, acidic water with pH around 5.5 to 6.5 and temperatures of 78 to 80°F, within our care range of 75 to 82°F. Use a sponge filter or covered intake to keep fry safe.\n\nA species only tank gives the best chance, since many tankmates will eat eggs and tiny fry. Leaf litter and moss add hiding places and grow microorganisms that young kuhlis can graze on. Keep the lid tight, as kuhlis can squeeze through small gaps."}, {"heading": "Choosing and conditioning breeders", "text": "Sexing is possible with a close look. Males have larger, more paddle shaped pectoral fins with a thicker first ray. Ripe females look fuller, and their green eggs may show through the skin.\n\nKeep a group of 6 or more. Feed frozen bloodworms, blackworms, brine shrimp, daphnia and quality sinking pellets for several weeks. Feeding at night, when they are most active, helps them get enough.\n\nMost kuhlis sold are a mix of similar Pangio species, so try to buy your group from one batch that all look the same. Quarantine new fish and give them time to settle before you expect spawning. Healthy kuhlis are active at night and eat eagerly."}, {"heading": "Spawning", "text": "Spawning is rarely seen, as it often happens at night. Reports describe groups gathering near the surface and laying eggs among floating plant roots. Triggers often mentioned include a cooler water change, raising the water level, or soft rainwater style changes.\n\nIf you find green eggs, gently move the roots or plants into a hatching container with tank water and an air stone.\n\nSpawning is often noticed after a large, soft, slightly cooler water change, which imitates the rainy season. Check floating plant roots in the morning with a flashlight for green eggs. If you never find eggs, look for tiny fry in moss and leaf litter instead."}, {"heading": "Eggs and fungus prevention", "text": "The eggs are bright green and easy to recognize. Breeders report hatching in about 1 to 2 days at 78 to 80°F. Remove white, fungused eggs daily. Keep the hatching container dim, and use very gentle aeration.\n\nIf you leave eggs in the main tank, dense plants and moss give some fry a chance to survive."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Fry are tiny and hide all the time.\n\n- First week: infusoria several times a day.\n- Week 2: microworms.\n- Week 3 on: baby brine shrimp.\n\nDo small water changes of 10 to 20 percent every few days, and siphon carefully. Moss and leaf litter grow microorganisms that fry can graze on.\n\nKeep the fry tank dark and quiet, and add a small piece of moss or a few leaves so they have cover. Fry that hide constantly still need food nearby, so spread food across the bottom rather than in one spot."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Kuhli fry grow slowly, taking several months to reach 1 to 2 inches. Feed baby brine shrimp, microworms and crushed sinking pellets several times a day. Give them soft sand and lots of cover. Keep the water clean and soft through grow out.\n\nOnce the young are about an inch long, they can move into a larger tank with sand and plenty of cover. Keep feeding at night, when they are most active."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Hard or alkaline water.\n- No floating plants for spawning.\n- Bright light, which keeps kuhlis hidden and stressed.\n- Strong filters that suck up fry.\n- Mixing Pangio species, as many look alike in the trade."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$kuhli-loach$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "A marine fish on the Society list", "text": "Clownfish are marine fish, so you will need a stable saltwater setup: specific gravity about 1.023 to 1.026, temperature about 76 to 80°F for everyday care (breeding pairs are often kept at 78 to 82°F), good live rock or filtration, and regular water changes with marine salt mix. They are on the Society list even though Underground Aquarium is mostly freshwater, and marine breeders are very welcome."}, {"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank and pair", "text": "Use 30 gallons or more with live rock and a flat tile or rock near the pair's chosen spot. Anemones aren't required.\n\nYoung clownfish mature as males first, and the largest fish in a pair becomes female. The easiest pair is two young fish, or a larger and smaller fish. The female is the larger of the two. Clark's can be aggressive toward other clownfish, so keep only one pair per tank.\n\nClark's clownfish naturally host many kinds of anemones, but pairs spawn happily without one. Place the tile where the pair spends most of its time, and keep tankmates few and peaceful so the pair feels secure."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and spawning", "text": "Feed mysis, enriched brine shrimp, quality pellets and flakes several times a day. Pairs often spawn every 2 to 3 weeks once settled.\n\nBefore spawning, the pair cleans a flat surface. The female lays rows of orange eggs, and the male fertilizes them. Clutches are commonly a few hundred eggs, sometimes more from large pairs."}, {"heading": "Eggs and hatching", "text": "The male fans and guards the eggs. They darken and show silver eyes as they develop. At 78 to 82°F, they hatch in about 6 to 9 days, usually an hour or two after dark.\n\nOn hatch night, either move the tile to the larval tank before lights out or turn off pumps and use a flashlight to draw larvae to a corner, then scoop them out gently."}, {"heading": "Raising the larvae", "text": "Larvae are about the size of an eyelash and need live food from the first night.\n\n- Days 1 to 7: enriched rotifers kept at a steady density, with green water (live phytoplankton) added to keep the rotifers fed and the water lightly tinted.\n- From around day 5 to 7: add newly hatched brine shrimp nauplii alongside the rotifers.\n- Days 8 to 12: metamorphosis. Larvae turn into tiny clownfish with their first white bars and settle toward the bottom.\n\nUse a 10 to 20 gallon larval tank with black sides, a gentle air stone and no filter for the first days. Siphon the bottom daily and change 10 to 20 percent of the water with matched salt water. Keep the temperature steady around 80°F.\n\nStart cultures at least two to three weeks before you expect a hatch. Keep two or more rotifer cultures going in case one crashes, feed them live phytoplankton or a concentrated algae paste, and enrich them before feeding larvae. Set up a daily brine shrimp hatchery too, so fresh nauplii are ready when larvae can take them. Running out of food in the first week is the most common reason a batch fails."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "After metamorphosis, start weaning onto fine dry foods while still feeding brine shrimp. Expect about 3 to 4 months to reach an inch. Keep water clean, feed several times a day, and sort by size.\n\nMany breeders move juveniles to a grow out tank with a sponge filter and bare bottom for easy cleaning. Watch for the white bars filling in and the body shape rounding out. Small water changes several times a week keep growth steady, and it is normal for some fish in a batch to grow faster than others."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Leaving larvae in the display, where filters and fish take them all.\n- Running out of rotifers or green water in the first week.\n- Strong aeration that batters larvae.\n- Feeding brine shrimp too early, before larvae can eat it.\n- Mixing with other clownfish species in the breeding tank."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$clarks-clownfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "A marine fish on the Society list", "text": "Dwarf seahorses are marine fish from seagrass beds around Florida, the Bahamas, Bermuda and the Gulf of Mexico. They are on the Society list even though Underground Aquarium is mostly freshwater. You need a small, stable saltwater tank with specific gravity about 1.023 to 1.025 and temperatures around 72 to 78°F. Choose captive bred stock."}, {"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank", "text": "A 5 to 10 gallon tank is ideal, as dwarfs need food close by. Use a sponge filter with gentle flow and add lots of hitching posts like artificial plants or seagrass. Live rock can bring in pests, so many keepers use a bare or sand bottom.\n\nKeep tankmates out. Even small shrimp or fish can compete for food or harm fry.\n\nDwarf seahorses are weak swimmers that spend their time holding onto posts, so place hitching posts throughout the tank and keep flow very gentle. A tight lid and a stable room temperature help keep conditions steady. Because they need such frequent feeding, many keepers set up a simple daily brine shrimp hatchery before they buy fish."}, {"heading": "Pairs and courtship", "text": "Sexing is easy in adults. Males have a brood pouch on the belly below the tail, and females don't. Pairs are often formed by keeping a small group together.\n\nCourtship usually happens in the morning. Pairs change color, hold tails, and dance around a hitching post. The female then passes eggs into the male's pouch.\n\nA small colony with several males and females gives the most spawns. Watch the morning displays; pairs that greet each other every day are likely to spawn soon. Well fed females are more likely to produce good broods."}, {"heading": "Pregnancy and birth", "text": "The male's pouch swells as the young develop. Gestation is about 10 to 14 days at 72 to 78°F. Broods are small, commonly a few to a few dozen young.\n\nBirth usually happens at night or early morning. The young are tiny, fully formed seahorses that grab onto hitching posts right away.\n\nMales are often ready to mate again soon after giving birth, so a healthy pair can produce several broods in a row. Count the fry when you can, since broods differ a lot from one birth to the next."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Unlike most seahorses, dwarf fry can eat newly hatched brine shrimp from birth.\n\n- From day 1: newly hatched brine shrimp, several times a day.\n- Week 2 on: enriched brine shrimp to help growth.\n- Change 10 to 20 percent of water every few days, matching salinity.\n\nKeep the food dense enough that fry can catch it easily, but siphon leftovers so the water stays clean.\n\nMake sure the brine shrimp you feed are freshly hatched, rinsed and free of shells, and hatch a new batch every day. Old, unenriched brine shrimp lose food value quickly. Fry should have visibly full bellies after feeding."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Dwarf seahorses are commonly reported to reach maturity in about 3 months. Keep feeding enriched brine shrimp several times a day and keep water clean. Many keepers raise fry with the adults, since adults usually ignore them. Separate them if you see problems."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Hydroids and other pests, often brought in with live food or rock. They can sting and kill dwarf seahorses.\n- Not enough food. Dwarfs need to eat often.\n- Strong flow or filters.\n- Tanks that are too large, which makes food hard to find.\n- Adding tankmates that steal food or bother the seahorses.\n- Letting salinity drift. Top off evaporation with fresh water daily."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$dwarf-seahorse$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "A marine fish on the Society list", "text": "Clownfish are marine fish, so you will need a stable saltwater setup: specific gravity about 1.023 to 1.026, temperature about 76 to 80°F for everyday care (breeding pairs are often kept at 78 to 82°F), good live rock or filtration, and regular water changes with marine salt mix. They are on the Society list even though Underground Aquarium is mostly freshwater, and marine breeders are very welcome."}, {"heading": "Pairing maroon clownfish", "text": "Maroons are very aggressive, and a large female can injure or kill a new male. The size difference between a pair is dramatic: the female is much larger and darker, the male small and often brighter.\n\nTo pair them, use a divider or clear container so the fish can see each other for days or weeks. Choose a small male and a larger female. Watch closely after release and separate them if fighting continues."}, {"heading": "Setting up and spawning", "text": "Use 40 gallons or more with live rock and a tile or flat rock near the pair's home. A bubble tip anemone is often used but isn't required.\n\nFeed mysis, enriched brine shrimp, quality pellets and flakes several times a day. Pairs clean a surface before spawning, and the female lays orange eggs. Clutches are commonly several hundred eggs."}, {"heading": "Eggs and hatching", "text": "The male fans and guards the eggs. They darken and show silver eyes as they develop. At 78 to 82°F, they hatch in about 6 to 9 days, usually an hour or two after dark.\n\nOn hatch night, move the tile to the larval tank or turn off pumps and use a light to collect larvae. Be careful, as maroons defend eggs fiercely and can bite."}, {"heading": "Raising the larvae", "text": "Larvae are about the size of an eyelash and need live food from the first night.\n\n- Days 1 to 7: enriched rotifers kept at a steady density, with green water (live phytoplankton) added to keep the rotifers fed and the water lightly tinted.\n- From around day 5 to 7: add newly hatched brine shrimp nauplii alongside the rotifers.\n- Days 8 to 15: metamorphosis (breeders report maroons can take a little longer than ocellaris). Larvae turn into tiny maroon clownfish with their first white bars and settle toward the bottom.\n\nUse a 10 to 20 gallon larval tank with black sides, a gentle air stone and no filter for the first days. Siphon the bottom daily and change 10 to 20 percent of the water with matched salt water. Keep the temperature steady around 80°F.\n\nStart cultures at least two to three weeks before you expect a hatch. Keep two or more rotifer cultures going in case one crashes, feed them live phytoplankton or a concentrated algae paste, and enrich them before feeding larvae. Set up a daily brine shrimp hatchery too, so fresh nauplii are ready when larvae can take them. Running out of food in the first week is the most common reason a batch fails."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "After metamorphosis, start weaning onto fine dry foods while feeding brine shrimp. Expect 3 to 4 months to reach an inch. Young maroons can be aggressive toward each other, so give them space and sort by size.\n\nMany breeders move juveniles to a grow out tank with a sponge filter and bare bottom for easy cleaning. Watch for the white bars filling in and the body shape rounding out. Small water changes several times a week keep growth steady, and it is normal for some fish in a batch to grow faster than others."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Leaving larvae in the display, where filters and fish take them all.\n- Running out of rotifers or green water in the first week.\n- Strong aeration that batters larvae.\n- Feeding brine shrimp too early, before larvae can eat it.\n- Forcing a pair together without a divider, which can lead to injury or death."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$maroon-clownfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "A marine fish on the Society list", "text": "Clownfish are marine fish, so you will need a stable saltwater setup: specific gravity about 1.023 to 1.026, temperature about 76 to 80°F for everyday care (breeding pairs are often kept at 78 to 82°F), good live rock or filtration, and regular water changes with marine salt mix. They are on the Society list even though Underground Aquarium is mostly freshwater, and marine breeders are very welcome."}, {"heading": "Setting up the breeding tank and pair", "text": "A 20 to 30 gallon tank with live rock and a flat tile or rock near the pair's home is enough. Anemones aren't needed.\n\nClownfish start as males, and the larger fish becomes the female. Two young fish will usually pair up. The female is the larger fish. Pairs can take months to start spawning.\n\nKeep tankmates few and peaceful so the pair feels secure, and place the tile near where they sleep. A stable tank with steady salinity and temperature is more important than any special decor."}, {"heading": "Conditioning and spawning", "text": "Feed mysis, enriched brine shrimp, quality pellets and flakes several times a day. Pairs often spawn every 2 to 3 weeks once settled.\n\nBefore spawning, the pair cleans a flat surface. The female lays rows of orange eggs, and the male fertilizes them. Clutches are commonly a few hundred eggs, sometimes more from large pairs."}, {"heading": "Eggs and hatching", "text": "The male fans and guards the eggs. They darken and show silver eyes as they develop. At 78 to 82°F, they hatch in about 6 to 9 days, usually an hour or two after dark.\n\nOn hatch night, either move the tile to the larval tank before lights out or turn off pumps and use a flashlight to collect larvae."}, {"heading": "Raising the larvae", "text": "Larvae are about the size of an eyelash and need live food from the first night.\n\n- Days 1 to 7: enriched rotifers kept at a steady density, with green water (live phytoplankton) added to keep the rotifers fed and the water lightly tinted.\n- From around day 5 to 7: add newly hatched brine shrimp nauplii alongside the rotifers.\n- Days 8 to 12: metamorphosis. Larvae turn into tiny clownfish with their first white bars and settle toward the bottom.\n\nUse a 10 to 20 gallon larval tank with black sides, a gentle air stone and no filter for the first days. Siphon the bottom daily and change 10 to 20 percent of the water with matched salt water. Keep the temperature steady around 80°F.\n\nStart cultures at least two to three weeks before you expect a hatch. Keep two or more rotifer cultures going in case one crashes, feed them live phytoplankton or a concentrated algae paste, and enrich them before feeding larvae. Set up a daily brine shrimp hatchery too, so fresh nauplii are ready when larvae can take them. Running out of food in the first week is the most common reason a batch fails."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "After metamorphosis, wean onto fine dry foods while still feeding brine shrimp. Expect about 3 to 4 months to reach an inch. Keep water clean, feed several times a day, and sort by size.\n\nMany breeders move juveniles to a grow out tank with a sponge filter and bare bottom for easy cleaning. Watch for the white bars filling in and the body shape rounding out. Small water changes several times a week keep growth steady, and it is normal for some fish in a batch to grow faster than others. Captive bred ocellaris are popular, but make sure your fish are healthy and eating dry food before passing any on."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Leaving larvae in the display, where filters and fish take them all.\n- Running out of rotifers or green water in the first week.\n- Strong aeration that batters larvae.\n- Feeding brine shrimp too early, before larvae can eat it.\n- Expecting a pair to spawn immediately. Many take months to settle."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$ocellaris-clownfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Substrate spawner: eggs stuck to rock or tile near the pair's home, guarded by the male", "sexing": "Not visible by color; the larger, dominant fish is the female, and the smaller is the male", "group": "One bonded pair; two juveniles will usually sort themselves into male and female", "breeding_tank": "Saltwater, 20 to 30 gallons for the pair, plus a 10 to 20 gallon larval tank", "conditioning": "Two or three meals a day of mysis, quality pellets and enriched brine for several weeks", "trigger": "A mature, well-fed pair with a flat spawning site and a stable day and night cycle", "spawn": "Commonly a few hundred eggs, roughly 100 to 500+, increasing as the pair matures", "eggs": "Hatch in about 6 to 10 days at 78 to 82°F, usually soon after the lights go out", "fry": "Tiny larvae drift in open water and settle and color up at about 8 to 12 days", "first_foods": "Enriched rotifers from day 1, then newly hatched brine shrimp from about day 5 to 8", "grow_out": "About 3 to 4 months to reach a small sellable size of around 1 inch", "parents": "Male fans and mouths the eggs; move the egg tile to a hatching tank on hatch night"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "The saltwater setup", "text": "Percula clownfish need full marine conditions: specific gravity around 1.023 to 1.026, temperature about 76 to 80°F for everyday care (breeding pairs are often kept at 78 to 82°F) and stable, clean water. They are on the Society list even though most of the site is freshwater, so plan for two systems. The pair tank can be a simple 20 to 30 gallon tank with live rock, a terracotta tile or flat rock for spawning, and an anemone if you keep one, although many pairs spawn happily without.\n\nThe larval tank is separate: 10 to 20 gallons, bare bottom, black or dark sides, gentle aeration from an airline with no airstone, and no filter for the first week. Most breeders add phytoplankton paste or live algae to tint the water green, which keeps the rotifers fed and helps the larvae see their food."}, {"heading": "Getting a bonded pair", "text": "The easiest route is to buy two small juveniles, or a pair sold as bonded. Clownfish are born without a fixed sex; the larger fish becomes the female and the smaller stays male, and the change from male to female cannot be undone. Two juveniles placed together will usually sort this out within weeks.\n\nCondition with two or three small meals a day of frozen mysis, quality marine pellets and enriched brine shrimp. A young pair may take 6 to 12 months before the first spawn."}, {"heading": "What spawning looks like", "text": "A day or two before spawning the pair will clean a patch of rock or tile intensely, and the female's belly looks rounder. You may see a small breeding tube from each fish. Spawning often happens in the afternoon or early evening, with the female making passes to lay rows of orange eggs and the male following to fertilize them.\n\n- First clutches are often small, under 100 eggs\n- Mature pairs commonly lay a few hundred\n- A healthy pair may spawn every 2 to 3 weeks"}, {"heading": "Eggs and hatch night", "text": "The male fans and mouths the eggs, removing any that go bad. Over 6 to 10 days at 78 to 82°F the eggs turn from orange to dark and then silver as the eyes develop. Hatching happens on the evening of the last night, usually within an hour or two of darkness.\n\nOn the expected night, move the tile to the larval tank with gentle aeration near the eggs, or siphon the larvae out of the parents' tank with a flashlight after dark. Losing a clutch to the filter or the cleanup crew is a very common first mistake."}, {"heading": "Raising the larvae", "text": "Larvae need enriched rotifers at a density high enough that food is always within reach, so start rotifer cultures weeks before the first spawn. Keep the larval tank at the same temperature as the parents and leave it dim but not dark.\n\n- Days 1 to 5: rotifers and green water, no water changes, small top-ups only\n- Days 5 to 8: start newly hatched brine shrimp alongside rotifers\n- Days 8 to 12: larvae settle, show their white bars and become juveniles\n- After settlement: siphon the bottom and change 10 to 20% every day or two"}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Once settled, the juveniles are tough little fish. Feed small meals four or five times a day: baby brine, crushed pellets and frozen cyclops, moving to larger foods as they grow. Expect roughly 3 to 4 months to reach about an inch. Give them enough room and you will see far less fighting and stunting."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Starting rotifer cultures after the eggs are already on the tile\n- Leaving larvae with the parents, where the filter or tank mates take them\n- Strong aeration that batters the larvae\n- Crashing water quality in the larval tank by overfeeding brine too early\n- Expecting every clutch to succeed; most first batches fail, and that is normal"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$percula-clownfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set summary = $g$Breeding uaru takes a large tank, soft warm water and a pair that forms on its own. Fry feed on parent slime at first. An advanced cichlid project.$g$, facts = $g${"method": "Substrate spawner: eggs laid on flat rock or wood, both parents guard", "sexing": "Very hard; papilla shape at spawning time is the only reliable sign", "group": "Grow out a group of 4 to 6 and let one pair form naturally", "breeding_tank": "125 gallons or more, flat stones or slate, soft acidic water", "conditioning": "Vegetable-heavy diet plus some protein for 4 to 8 weeks", "trigger": "Stable soft, warm water at 82 to 84°F and regular water changes", "spawn": "Commonly reported around 100 to 300 eggs", "eggs": "Hatch in about 2 to 4 days at 82 to 84°F; free swimming roughly 4 to 6 days later", "fry": "Fry graze the slime coat on the parents' bodies during the first weeks", "first_foods": "Parental slime first, plus newly hatched brine shrimp once free swimming", "grow_out": "Slow; often 3 months or more to reach about 1 to 1.5 inches", "parents": "Good guardians once settled; first spawns are often eaten, so be patient"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Space and water", "text": "Uaru reach about 10 inches and live in groups, so the site's guide calls for at least 125 gallons and a group of four or more. For breeding, keep the water at the warm end of their range, 82 to 84°F, with pH around 5.5 to 6.5 and soft water. Provide a few large flat stones, slate or smooth wood as spawning sites, and a canister or sponge filter that will not suck up tiny fry.\n\nUaru eat a lot of greens, which means a lot of waste. Plan on large weekly water changes of 30 to 50% with matched temperature."}, {"heading": "Forming a pair", "text": "Sexing uaru by sight is unreliable. Some keepers point to body shape or a slight forehead bump in males, but these signs often mislead. The practical answer is to raise a group of four to six juveniles together and wait for two fish to start claiming and cleaning a stone together. Once a pair has formed, rehome the others or give the pair the tank.\n\nCondition with a diet rich in vegetables, such as blanched zucchini, spinach and algae-based pellets, plus some protein like krill or quality cichlid pellets, for 4 to 8 weeks."}, {"heading": "Spawning", "text": "Before spawning, the pair darkens and both fish scrub a flat surface for a day or more. The breeding tubes become visible: the female's is broad and blunt, the male's narrower and pointed. The female lays rows of eggs and the male follows to fertilize them. Breeders commonly report clutches of around 100 to 300 eggs."}, {"heading": "Eggs and wrigglers", "text": "Both parents fan the eggs and pick out any that turn white. At 82 to 84°F the eggs hatch in about 2 to 4 days. The wrigglers are moved to pits or another surface and stay there for several more days while they use their yolk.\n\nIf you prefer to hatch eggs away from the parents, you lose the slime feeding stage, which makes the fry much harder to raise. Most breeders leave uaru eggs with the pair."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Like discus, uaru fry feed on the mucus on their parents' sides during the first weeks. This is why a settled pair matters so much.\n\n- Free swimming: fry swarm the parents and graze their bodies\n- From the first days of free swimming: add newly hatched brine shrimp two or three times a day\n- Weeks 2 to 4: add crushed flake, powdered algae pellets and microworms\n- Change 20 to 30% of the water every few days, gently, at the same temperature"}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Uaru fry grow slowly. Juveniles develop the spotted juvenile pattern first and take months to show adult coloring. Move them to a grow-out tank at around 4 to 6 weeks if the parents start to spawn again. Feed plenty of greens alongside protein, keep the water clean, and expect at least 3 months before they are a comfortable 1 to 1.5 inches.\n\nUaru are social, so raise the young in a group rather than singly, and give them hiding places such as wood and plants they can't uproot easily. Plan ahead for where they will go: each one grows into a large fish that needs a big tank, and local clubs and members are the best homes."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Buying two fish and hoping they are a pair\n- Hard or alkaline water, which often gives poor fertility\n- Pulling the eggs, so the fry miss the slime feeding stage\n- Too small a tank, which leads to fights and stress\n- Giving up after the first few eaten clutches"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$uaru$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Substrate spawner: eggs on a vertical surface such as a cone, both parents guard", "sexing": "Unreliable by sight; breeding tube shape at spawning is the best guide", "group": "One proven pair, usually chosen from a group of 5 or more raised together", "breeding_tank": "30 to 40 gallon tank per pair, bare bottom, spawning cone, sponge filter", "conditioning": "Beef heart mix, frozen bloodworms and quality granules, several weeks", "trigger": "Very soft water, pH lowered slowly to about 5.5 to 6, warm 84 to 86°F and stability", "spawn": "Commonly about 100 to 300 eggs", "eggs": "Hatch in roughly 2 to 3 days at 84 to 86°F; wrigglers free swim 2 to 3 days later", "fry": "Fry swarm the parents and eat the slime on their sides for the first 1 to 2 weeks", "first_foods": "Parental slime, then newly hatched brine shrimp from about 5 days free swimming", "grow_out": "Around 2 to 3 months to reach roughly 1.5 to 2 inches with heavy feeding", "parents": "Eggs are often eaten in first spawns; most pairs improve with practice"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Breeding tank and water", "text": "The site's care guide lists 82 to 86°F and pH 6 to 7. For breeding wild fish, most breeders go softer and more acidic: pH about 5.5 to 6, lowered slowly, general hardness close to zero, and low conductivity. Wild discus eggs in harder water often fail to develop, so a reverse osmosis unit is close to essential.\n\nUse a 30 to 40 gallon tank per pair with a bare bottom, a sponge filter, a heater set to 84 to 86°F and a spawning cone or upright slate. Keep the tank in a quiet spot, and change 30 to 50% of the water several times a week with matched temperature and chemistry."}, {"heading": "Finding a pair", "text": "You cannot reliably sex discus by shape or color. The usual method is to raise a group of five or more together and watch for two fish that stay together and start cleaning a surface. Once they are paired, move them to their own tank.\n\nCondition with several small meals a day: a beef heart based mix, frozen bloodworms and quality discus granules. Wild fish can be shy feeders at first, so be patient.\n\nKeep the pair tank calm: no busy tank mates, steady lighting and a cover on the front glass if people walk past often. Wild fish that feel exposed tend to eat their eggs. Give a new pair several weeks to settle in before you expect a spawn, and keep water changes regular so the chemistry does not swing."}, {"heading": "Spawning", "text": "A pair about to spawn will clean the cone repeatedly, shake and bow to each other, and show their breeding tubes. The female's tube is broad and blunt, the male's thinner and more pointed. She lays lines of eggs and he follows to fertilize. Breeders commonly report about 100 to 300 eggs."}, {"heading": "Eggs and wrigglers", "text": "At 84 to 86°F eggs hatch in roughly 2 to 3 days. The parents move the wrigglers, which hang on the cone for another 2 to 3 days. Eggs that turn white are infertile or fungused; a few are normal, but a whole clutch going white usually points to water that is too hard, or an inexperienced male.\n\nEgg eating is common with new pairs. Give them several chances before trying anything else. A mesh guard around the cone helps some pairs."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "Discus fry feed on the slime coat on their parents' sides. This is the most important stage, and wild pairs sometimes fail at it at first.\n\n- Days 1 to 5 free swimming: fry graze the parents, no extra food needed\n- From about day 5: add newly hatched brine shrimp several times a day\n- Weeks 2 to 3: remove the parents gently once fry are eating well on their own\n- Throughout: small, frequent water changes, keeping the temperature steady"}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Young discus need lots of food and lots of clean water. Feed five or more small meals a day of baby brine, then a beef heart mix and fine granules as they grow. Change water daily if you can. With good care they can reach around 1.5 to 2 inches in 2 to 3 months, but stunting is common if water quality slips."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Using tap water that is too hard for wild fish eggs\n- Disturbing the pair during the first spawns\n- Pulling the parents too early, before fry eat brine well\n- Skipping water changes in the grow-out tank\n- Mixing wild and tank-bred strains when your goal is wild-type stock"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$wild-discus$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "The breeding tank", "text": "Butterflyfish live at the surface, so surface area matters more than depth. A 30 gallon or larger tank, long and shallow, works well. Cover part of the surface with floating plants, keep flow gentle and fit a tight lid, because these fish jump.\n\nThe site's care guide lists 75 to 84°F and pH 6 to 7.5. For breeding, breeders report better results at about 77 to 82°F with soft, slightly acidic water, around pH 6.5."}, {"heading": "Sexing and conditioning", "text": "Sexing is possible with a close look at the anal fin. In males the rear edge is curved and the central rays form a tube-like structure; in females the edge is straight. This difference is well described, but it can be hard to see on young fish.\n\nCondition with a varied insect diet: crickets, fruit flies, mosquito larvae and small floating foods, offered daily for several weeks. Butterflyfish rarely take food from the bottom, so offer it at the surface.\n\nA well-conditioned female looks fuller through the belly. Keep only one male per tank if you can, since males may squabble at the surface. Avoid fast or fin-nipping tank mates during breeding attempts; quiet, bottom-dwelling companions or a species-only tank work best."}, {"heading": "Spawning", "text": "Pairs embrace near the surface, and eggs are released over several days rather than all at once. Fertilization is thought to happen inside the female. The eggs float, which makes them easy to spot as small round beads among the plants. Numbers vary a lot between reports, so don't worry about matching a figure."}, {"heading": "Eggs", "text": "Collect floating eggs each day with a spoon or small cup and move them to a shallow container with water from the breeding tank, plus gentle aeration or a small airline nearby. Breeders commonly report hatching in about 2 to 3 days at 77 to 82°F. Remove any eggs that turn white or fuzzy to stop fungus spreading. Leaving eggs with the adults usually means losing many of them.\n\nKeep the egg container in dim light at the same temperature as the breeding tank, and change a little of its water daily with water from the main tank. Clean, matched water matters more than anything you add to it."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "This is the hard part. Fry stay right at the surface and, like the adults, will not chase food that sinks. Breeders report the best results with tiny floating live foods.\n\n- First feeding: springtails and other very small floating live foods\n- As they grow: fruit flies, small floating insects and mosquito larvae at the surface\n- Keep the water shallow, a few inches, so food stays near them\n- Small, gentle water changes every few days at the same temperature\n\nNewly hatched brine shrimp sink and are often ignored, which is why many attempts fail at this stage."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Grow-out is slow and not well documented. Keep fry in shallow, covered containers, sort them by size because larger fry may eat smaller ones, and step up food size as they grow. Expect several months before they look like small adults."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Offering sinking foods to fry that only eat at the surface\n- Leaving eggs with the adults\n- Deep containers where fry can't find food\n- No lid, so adults jump during spawning\n- Expecting a full clutch to survive; even a few grown fry is a success\n- Mixing fry of very different sizes, which invites cannibalism"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$african-butterfly-fish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "About this species", "text": "Betta macrostoma comes from a small area of northern Borneo and is protected in its home range. Only buy fish that are legally sourced, ideally captive bred by other hobbyists. This species is rarely bred in home aquariums, so the advice below reflects what breeders report rather than a well-worn method."}, {"heading": "The breeding tank", "text": "Use a 20 to 30 gallon tank with a tight lid, dim lighting, a layer of leaf litter, wood and a few caves. Breeders report success in cool to moderate temperatures, around 72 to 79°F, with very soft, acidic water, about pH 5.5 to 6.5 and very low hardness. Their wild blackwater streams can be far more acidic, but in the aquarium don't go below pH 5.5, and keep a little KH so the pH can't crash. Tannins from leaves help. Use a gentle sponge filter and change water in small, matched amounts.\n\nPrepare your water in advance: reverse osmosis or rain-quality water, softened with leaf litter or peat, and stored at tank temperature. Sudden swings in pH or temperature are more harmful than water that is slightly off target. Keep the room quiet and the tank away from foot traffic, because nervous fish rarely spawn. Floating plants help soften the light and give the fish cover."}, {"heading": "Choosing a pair", "text": "Males are brighter, with orange-red bodies and bold fin edges. Females are duller and more patterned. The real challenge is compatibility; a forced pair may fight. Many breeders raise a small group and remove the extra fish once a pair forms.\n\nCondition with live and frozen foods: daphnia, mosquito larvae, bloodworms and brine shrimp. Avoid overfeeding, which fouls soft water fast.\n\nWatch for the female rounding out and both fish showing stronger color, especially in the evening. Pairs that hold their ground side by side near a cave, without chasing each other, are usually the ones ready to spawn."}, {"heading": "Spawning and holding", "text": "Spawning involves a long embrace. The female collects the eggs and spits them toward the male, who takes them into his mouth. Breeders report the male then holds for roughly 2 to 3 weeks, eating little or nothing.\n\nA holding male has a swollen throat. Leave the tank alone, cover the front if needed, and avoid water changes for the first days. Stress is the main reason males swallow or spit out a brood."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "When the male releases them, the fry are fully formed and large enough to eat right away.\n\n- First weeks: newly hatched brine shrimp and microworms, two or three times a day\n- Small water changes of 10 to 20% every few days, matched carefully\n- Weeks 4 and on: frozen daphnia and crushed foods\n\nMost breeders move the father out, or the fry into a grow-out tank, soon after release."}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Fry grow slowly and need clean, soft water. Keep feeding small meals often and sort by size if any start to bully. Expect several months before they show adult color.\n\nThis species grows into a fairly large betta, so give juveniles room as they grow and avoid overcrowding, which leads to fin damage and stunting. Keep the same soft, acidic water you used for spawning, and change it regularly in small amounts. Because so few are bred, share young fish with other serious keepers so the captive population stays healthy."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Forcing two fish together that don't get along\n- Hard or alkaline water\n- Disturbing a holding male, causing him to swallow the brood\n- Buying fish of unclear origin; choose legal, captive-bred stock\n- Expecting quick results; many pairs take months to spawn, and first broods are often lost"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$brunei-beauty$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set sections = $g$[{"heading": "Why nerite eggs don't hatch into snails", "text": "Neritina natalensis lives in rivers and estuaries in Africa. Adults live in fresh or slightly brackish water, but their larvae are swept downstream and develop in brackish or sea water before young snails travel back up. In a freshwater aquarium the capsules may hatch, but the larvae can't develop, so you never see baby snails.\n\nThis also means nerites won't overrun a tank, which is part of their appeal. Captive raising is rarely reported, and published hobby methods are thin. Treat this guide as an outline of what is known."}, {"heading": "Keeping the adults", "text": "The site's care guide lists 72 to 80°F, pH 7 to 8.5 and moderate to hard water. Hard, alkaline water keeps their shells strong. Feed algae wafers, blanched zucchini and spinach, and leave some algae growing on the glass and rocks.\n\nNerites have separate sexes, but there is no reliable way to tell them apart by eye. Keep a group of five or more to improve the odds of having both. Capsules can appear even from a single snail kept alone, so capsules alone don't prove you have both sexes.\n\nWell-fed adults lay more capsules, so keep algae and vegetables on the menu and supplement calcium if your water is soft. Healthy nerites are active, keep their trapdoor (operculum) closed tightly when disturbed and have smooth, unpitted shells. Pitting or white erosion on the shell usually means the water is too soft or acidic."}, {"heading": "The egg capsules", "text": "Females lay small, white, sesame-seed capsules on hard surfaces: glass, wood, rocks and even other snails. Each capsule holds many tiny eggs. Breeders who have raised nerites usually move capsule-covered rocks or wood into salt water rather than scraping the capsules off, because scraped capsules are easily damaged.\n\nHatch time varies with temperature and is not well documented for this species, so check the larval container daily.\n\nSet up the larval container before you move any capsules: a small tank or jar with an air line, a heater, a light and pre-mixed salt water at the starting salinity. Have a phytoplankton culture running too, since there is no time to start one once larvae appear."}, {"heading": "The larval stage", "text": "Larvae hatch as microscopic swimming veligers. Successful breeders report raising salinity from brackish toward full sea water, and feeding live phytoplankton so the larvae always have food. They need gentle aeration to stay in suspension and stable temperature.\n\n- Keep the larval container in light, with no filter that could catch larvae\n- Add live microalgae daily to keep the water lightly green\n- Do small, careful water changes with matched salinity\n- Watch with a magnifying glass for settled snails on the walls\n\nThe larval stage can last several weeks or longer. Losses are high."}, {"heading": "Raising the baby snails", "text": "Once larvae settle, they become tiny crawling snails grazing on algae films. Breeders report slowly lowering salinity over weeks before moving them back toward fresh water. Give them glass or rocks coated with soft algae, and never let the water quality swing. Growth is slow, and it takes months before they look like small adults.\n\nKeep a dated record as you go: salinity, temperature and what you fed. With so few reports out there, your notes are valuable to other members."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Expecting capsules in freshwater to produce snails\n- Scraping capsules off glass and damaging them\n- Using a filter in the larval container\n- Changing salinity too fast in either direction\n- Not having live phytoplankton ready before the larvae hatch\n- Copper based medicines or plant products, which kill snails"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$nerite-snail$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Cave spawner: male builds an algae nest in a cave, eggs laid among the algae and guarded by him", "sexing": "Hard; males are usually larger with longer pelvic fins", "group": "One male with one or more females in a large enough tank", "breeding_tank": "Saltwater, 30 gallons or more, caves and loose macroalgae", "conditioning": "Mysis, enriched brine and marine pellets two or three times a day", "trigger": "A settled male with a cave and nest material", "spawn": "Clutch size varies; breeders report frequent spawns", "eggs": "Hatch in roughly 5 to 7 days at reef temperatures", "fry": "Tiny larvae live in open water for weeks", "first_foods": "Enriched rotifers, then newly hatched brine shrimp", "grow_out": "Larvae settle in several weeks; months to sell size", "parents": "Male guards nest; larvae must be collected on hatch night"}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$royal-gramma$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Substrate spawner: eggs laid on a vertical surface, both parents guard", "sexing": "Unreliable by sight; breeding tube shape at spawning is the best guide", "group": "Raise a group of 5 or more and let a pair form", "breeding_tank": "Tall tank, 75 gallons or more, upright slate or wood", "conditioning": "Frozen bloodworms, brine shrimp and quality foods for weeks", "trigger": "Very soft, acidic water, pH around 5.5 to 6.0 for spawning, and stable warm temps", "spawn": "Commonly reported in the low hundreds", "eggs": "Hatch in about 2 to 3 days at 84 to 86°F", "fry": "Wrigglers become free swimming about 4 to 6 days after hatching", "first_foods": "Newly hatched brine shrimp and microworms", "grow_out": "Slow; several months to reach a juvenile size", "parents": "Parents may eat eggs; many breeders remove eggs to hatch separately"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Tank and water", "text": "Altums are taller than common angelfish and need a tall tank, ideally 24 inches deep or more. The site's care guide lists at least 75 gallons, 82 to 86°F, pH 5.5 to 6.5 and very soft water.\n\nFor breeding, breeders report the best results at the soft, acidic end: pH around 5.5 to 6.0 and hardness close to zero, lowered slowly over a couple of weeks. Reverse osmosis water is close to essential. Provide upright slate, wood or broad leaves as spawning sites, and keep the tank calm with dim lighting. Use a sponge filter or a gently flowing canister, and change water often with matched temperature and chemistry. Peat or leaf litter helps keep the water soft and slightly stained."}, {"heading": "Forming a pair", "text": "Sexing altums by eye is unreliable. The best approach is to raise a group of five or more and wait for two fish to start claiming a spawning site together. Once a pair forms, move them to their own tank or move the others.\n\nCondition with frozen bloodworms, brine shrimp and quality flake or granules. Wild-caught altums can be shy feeders at first, so be patient.\n\nAltums are sensitive to stress, so keep the group in a quiet room with steady lighting and few tank mates. Peaceful dither fish such as small tetras can help them feel secure. Pairs can take a long time to form, sometimes years, so don't rush the process or split a group too early."}, {"heading": "Spawning", "text": "Before spawning, the pair cleans a vertical surface. The breeding tubes show: the female's is broad and blunt, the male's thinner and pointed. The female lays rows of eggs and the male follows to fertilize them. Clutches are commonly reported in the low hundreds.\n\nSpawning usually takes an hour or two. Both parents then fan and guard the eggs. With a young pair, it is common for the first few clutches to be eaten or to fail, which is why many breeders choose to hatch the eggs separately."}, {"heading": "Eggs and wrigglers", "text": "At 84 to 86°F eggs hatch in about 2 to 3 days. Wrigglers become free swimming about 4 to 6 days after hatching. Eggs that turn white are infertile or fungused; a whole clutch going white usually points to water that is too hard.\n\nMany breeders remove the egg slate to a separate container with water from the breeding tank, gentle aeration and a few drops of methylene blue to prevent fungus."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "- Once free swimming: newly hatched brine shrimp and microworms, several times a day\n- Weeks 2 to 4: add crushed flake and frozen foods\n- Small, frequent water changes with matched temperature and chemistry\n- Sponge filtration so fry are not sucked up"}, {"heading": "Growing out", "text": "Altum fry grow slowly and need clean, soft water. Feed several small meals a day and keep water quality high. It takes several months before they look like small altums, and longer before they reach full height. Keep the grow-out tank tall as they get bigger, since cramped altums often develop bent fins. Share young fish with other altum keepers so this rarely bred species becomes more available."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Water that is too hard for altum eggs\n- A tank that is too short for their tall bodies\n- Disturbing the pair during spawning\n- Skipping water changes in the grow-out tank\n- Expecting quick results; pairs can take years to form\n- Moving a pair to a new tank just as they settle, which often sets them back months"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"q": "Are altum angelfish hard to breed?", "a": "Yes. They need very soft, acidic water and a tall tank, and captive spawns are rare."}, {"q": "How do you tell male and female altum angelfish apart?", "a": "There's no reliable outward sign. The breeding tubes seen at spawning are the best guide."}, {"q": "What pH do altum angelfish need to breed?", "a": "Breeders report the best results with pH around 5.5 to 6.0 and hardness close to zero."}, {"q": "How long do altum angelfish eggs take to hatch?", "a": "About 2 to 3 days at 84 to 86°F, with fry free swimming about 4 to 6 days later."}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$altum-angelfish$g$;
update public.breeding_guides set facts = $g${"method": "Egg scatterer: eggs scattered among plants, no parental care", "sexing": "Males have a thicker, broader anal fin; females are often plumper", "group": "A group of mature adults, ideally with more than one of each sex", "breeding_tank": "75 gallons or more, dense plants, tight lid", "conditioning": "Meaty foods such as worms, shrimp and fish, several times a week", "trigger": "Breeders report cooler, softer water changes to copy the rainy season", "spawn": "Commonly reported as a few hundred eggs or fewer", "eggs": "Hatch in roughly 3 to 4 days at 77 to 82°F", "fry": "Larvae have feathery external gills and stay near the bottom", "first_foods": "Newly hatched brine shrimp and microworms", "grow_out": "Several months to reach a juvenile size", "parents": "No parental care; adults eat eggs and fry, so remove them"}$g$::jsonb, sections = $g$[{"heading": "Tank and water", "text": "Senegal bichirs grow to around 12 inches. The site's care guide lists at least 75 gallons, 72 to 82°F and pH 6.5 to 7.5. For breeding, provide dense plants or spawning mops where eggs can land, and a tight lid since bichirs can escape through small gaps.\n\nBichirs are hardy, and they tolerate a wide range of water. For breeding, some keepers lower the hardness and pH slightly, while keeping within the site's range. Give the group plenty of space and hiding places such as wood and caves, since males can squabble when they are in breeding condition."}, {"heading": "Sexing and conditioning", "text": "Males have a thicker, broader anal fin, which they use to cup the eggs during spawning. Females are often plumper. A group of mature adults gives the best chance of a pair.\n\nCondition with meaty foods: worms, shrimp and pieces of fish, several times a week. Well-fed females look noticeably fuller. Mature fish are needed, and bichirs can take a few years to reach breeding size. Young fish under several inches are unlikely to spawn."}, {"heading": "Triggering a spawn", "text": "Breeders report spawns after large, slightly cooler and softer water changes that copy the rainy season. Keep this up for a few weeks and watch for chasing and the male following the female closely.\n\nSome breeders also drop the temperature slightly for a period, then raise it again with fresh, softer water. There is no proven recipe, and many keepers try for months before seeing anything. Keep notes on what you change and when."}, {"heading": "Spawning", "text": "During spawning, the male cups his anal fin to collect the eggs and fertilizes them, then scatters them among plants. Clutches are commonly reported as a few hundred eggs or fewer. Remove the eggs or the adults right away, since adults readily eat them.\n\nSpawning tends to happen at night or early morning, so check the plants and mops each morning. The eggs are small and sticky, and easy to miss among dense plants. A spawning mop that you can lift out makes collecting them much easier."}, {"heading": "Eggs and larvae", "text": "Eggs commonly hatch in roughly 3 to 4 days at 77 to 82°F. The larvae have feathery external gills, which they lose as they grow. They look more like tiny salamanders than fish at first. They stay near the bottom and use their yolk for several days before feeding.\n\nKeep the eggs in a separate container with water from the main tank, gentle aeration and dim light. Remove any eggs that turn white or fuzzy to stop fungus spreading. Larvae should be kept in shallow water, a few inches deep, so they can reach the surface easily, since bichirs gulp air as they grow."}, {"heading": "Raising the fry", "text": "- Once feeding: newly hatched brine shrimp and microworms, several times a day\n- Weeks 2 to 4: add chopped bloodworms and blackworms\n- Small, frequent water changes with matched temperature\n- Sort by size, since larger fry eat smaller ones\n\nGrowth is slow, and it takes several months to reach a juvenile size."}, {"heading": "Common mistakes", "text": "- Leaving eggs with the adults\n- No lid, so bichirs escape\n- Mixing fry of very different sizes\n- Expecting quick results; spawns are rare and unpredictable\n- Overfeeding larvae, which fouls the shallow water quickly\n- Deep water for larvae, which makes it harder for them to reach the surface\n- Forgetting that adult tank mates will hunt any fry that escape into the main tank"}]$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where slug = $g$senegal-bichir$g$;
-- The king tiger guide is about L333 (as on the Society list); the library's king tiger page is L066, a different fish.
update public.breeding_guides set species_slug = null where slug = 'king-tiger-pleco';

-- Glossary
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Ceramic disc diffusers: a fine porous disc in a glass or plastic holder. They make a mist of tiny bubbles and need a fair amount of pressure to push gas through.\n- Inline diffusers: fit on the canister filter outflow hose, keeping equipment out of sight.\n- Reactors: chambers where water and CO2 mix fully. Very efficient, good for large tanks.\n- Bubble ladders: simple, cheap and visible, but less efficient.", "heading": "Types of diffusers"}, {"text": "Put an in-tank diffuser low in the tank under or near the filter outflow, so current carries the bubbles around before they reach the surface. The longer bubbles stay in the water, the more CO2 dissolves. Use a CO2-rated check valve on the line and confirm levels with a drop checker.", "heading": "Placement"}, {"text": "Most planted tanks aim for about 20 to 30 ppm CO2, which turns a standard drop checker green. Start with a low bubble rate and raise it slowly over a week or two. Many keepers turn CO2 on 1 to 2 hours before the lights and off about an hour before lights out. Fish gasping at the surface means too much: turn it down and add aeration right away.", "heading": "Getting the dose right"}, {"text": "Ceramic discs clog with algae and biofilm, which makes bubbles bigger. Soak the disc in a dilute bleach or hydrogen peroxide solution, rinse thoroughly, and soak it in dechlorinated water before reuse.", "heading": "Cleaning"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$co2-diffuser$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Planted tanks: 6 to 8 hours a day is a common starting point.\n- Fish-only tanks: 6 to 10 hours, and about 8 is plenty for most.\n- New planted tanks: start near 6 hours and increase slowly once plants are growing well.\n\nIf algae takes over, cutting an hour or two off the photoperiod is often the first fix.", "heading": "How long to run lights"}, {"text": "Mechanical timers with pins are cheap and simple but can drift and get noisy. Digital timers are more precise and remember settings after outages on most models. Many LED lights now include built-in programs with sunrise and sunset ramping, which eases fish into the day and avoids the startle of lights snapping on in a dark room. Whatever type you choose, set it once and leave it, since constant schedule changes do more harm than any single choice.", "heading": "Types of timers"}, {"text": "Time the lights for when you actually watch the tank, often afternoon and evening. If you inject CO2, set it to start 1 to 2 hours before the lights. Some keepers use a midday break, called a siesta, to fight algae. It is debated, and many tanks do fine with one steady block of light.", "heading": "Setting a good schedule"}, {"text": "Use a timer rated for the light's power draw, plug it into a grounded outlet, and leave a drip loop in every cord so water can't run into the plug. Check the clock after power outages, as some timers reset.", "heading": "Safety tips"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "About 6 to 8 hours for planted tanks and 6 to 10 hours for fish-only tanks.", "q": "How many hours should aquarium lights be on?"}, {"a": "Yes. Fish need darkness to rest, and constant light causes heavy algae growth.", "q": "Is it bad to leave aquarium lights on 24/7?"}, {"a": "Whenever you'll enjoy the tank most. Fish adjust to the schedule as long as it stays consistent.", "q": "Should aquarium lights be on during the day or night?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$light-timer$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Usually adapted to a wider range of pH and hardness than wild fish\n- Already eats flakes, pellets and frozen foods\n- Fewer wild parasites, though quarantine is still smart\n- No collection pressure on wild habitats\n- Often easier to find in bright color strains and long-finned forms", "heading": "Why many keepers prefer it"}, {"text": "Captive-bred does not automatically mean healthy. Some mass-produced strains suffer from inbreeding, weaker immune systems, deformities or shorter lifespans. Fancy guppies, bettas and some angelfish lines are known for this. Fish raised on farms in very different water may still need careful acclimation, and some farm fish are given hormones or dyes to boost color, a practice many hobbyists avoid supporting.", "heading": "The downsides"}, {"text": "These labels are used loosely. Captive-bred usually means spawned in captivity. Farmed or pond-raised fish are bred in large outdoor ponds, mostly in Florida and Southeast Asia. Some sellers call a fish tank-raised when it was collected wild as a juvenile and grown out in captivity, which is not the same thing, so ask if it matters to you.", "heading": "Captive-bred vs tank-raised vs farmed"}, {"text": "Look for active fish with full color, clean fins and rounded bellies. Local breeders and hobbyist clubs often sell healthier, less inbred fish than mass farms, and can tell you the water they were raised in. Quarantine still applies: give any new fish 4 to 6 weeks in a separate tank before it joins your display.", "heading": "Choosing good stock"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "For most beginners, yes. They tend to be hardier in tap water and eat prepared food, though wild-caught fish can show stronger natural color.", "q": "Are captive-bred fish better than wild-caught?"}, {"a": "Ask the seller. Common store species like guppies, platies, mollies and most bettas are almost always captive-bred, while some tetras and many rare species are still collected wild.", "q": "How can I tell if a fish is captive-bred?"}, {"a": "Yes. They can still carry ich, bacteria and internal parasites from the farm or store, so quarantine for 4 to 6 weeks is a good habit.", "q": "Do captive-bred fish still need quarantine?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$captive-bred$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "Hardy species accept a broad pH and hardness range, handle moderate temperature changes, eat prepared foods readily and resist common diseases. Many have been bred in captivity for generations, so they are adapted to tap water. Examples include zebra danios, white cloud mountain minnows, platies, many barbs, bristlenose plecos, some corydoras and many livebearers. Hardy plants include java fern, anubias, java moss and cryptocorynes.", "heading": "What makes a fish hardy"}, {"text": "- It does not mean a fish can live in a bowl or tiny tank\n- It does not replace cycling; ammonia and nitrite still burn gills\n- It does not mean any size or temperament fits any tank\n- It does not mean tolerating constant poor water. Hardy fish often show stress later, through disease or shortened life\n\nSome hardy fish, like common goldfish and many plecos, grow large and need big tanks even though they are tough.", "heading": "What it does not mean"}, {"text": "A fish can be hardy and still a poor beginner choice. Tiger barbs are tough but nippy. Convict cichlids are nearly indestructible but aggressive breeders. Look at temperament, adult size and group needs as well as toughness.", "heading": "Hardy vs easy"}, {"text": "Hobbyists once added a few hardy fish to cycle new tanks. Many now consider fishless cycling kinder, since even hardy fish are harmed by ammonia. If you do cycle with fish, stock lightly and test daily, changing water whenever ammonia plus nitrite goes above 0.25 ppm.", "heading": "Using hardy fish wisely"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$hardy$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "This works for most hardy fish when your water is similar to the store's.\n\n- Turn off the tank light to lower stress.\n- Float the sealed bag in the tank for about 15 minutes so the temperature evens out.\n- Open the bag and add about half a cup of tank water every 5 minutes for 20 to 30 minutes.\n- Net the fish into the tank and throw the bag water away instead of pouring it in.", "heading": "The float method"}, {"text": "Use this for shrimp, sensitive fish, wild-caught fish or when your water is very different from the seller's. After floating, pour the fish and their water into a clean bucket. Run airline tubing from the tank to the bucket, tie a loose knot or use a valve so it drips about 2 to 4 drops per second, and let the water volume double, then double again. That usually takes 45 to 90 minutes for fish and 1 to 2 hours for shrimp.", "heading": "The drip method"}, {"text": "Fish that spent many hours in a bag build up ammonia, but the bag water's pH drops from their breath, which keeps that ammonia in a less toxic form. Once you open the bag, the pH rises and the ammonia becomes more harmful. For fish shipped overnight, many keepers float to match temperature, then move them fairly quickly into tank water rather than doing a long acclimation in old bag water.", "heading": "The ammonia trap with shipped fish"}, {"text": "Keep the lights off for a few hours and skip feeding until the next day. Watch for hiding, clamped fins or rapid breathing. Ideally, every new fish goes into a quarantine tank for 4 to 6 weeks first, and you acclimate them into that tank the same way.", "heading": "After release"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "About 35 to 45 minutes total for most fish using the float method, and 45 minutes to 2 hours with the drip method for sensitive fish and shrimp.", "q": "How long should you acclimate fish?"}, {"a": "No. Store water can carry ammonia, diseases and medications. Net the fish out and discard the bag water.", "q": "Should I put the store water in my tank?"}, {"a": "Common causes are a big difference in pH or hardness, temperature shock, ammonia from a long shipment, stress, or disease the fish already carried.", "q": "Why did my fish die after acclimation?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$acclimation$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- White, green or reddish threads about a quarter to half an inch long sticking out of the skin, often near fins\n- A red, swollen sore at the base of each thread\n- Twin egg sacs at the end of mature females, giving a forked look\n- Fish rubbing against decor, clamped fins, lethargy or loss of appetite", "heading": "Signs to watch for"}, {"text": "Anchor worms, from the genus Lernaea, are copepods. They come in on new fish, with water from bags, live foods from ponds or plants from outdoor setups. Only adult females attach permanently. The young larvae swim free in the water for days to weeks before finding a host, which is why the whole tank needs treating, not just the visible parasites. Warm water above about 77°F speeds up their life cycle.", "heading": "How fish get them"}, {"text": "- Remove visible worms with tweezers, gripping close to the skin and pulling slowly so the head comes out. Swab the wound with an antiseptic. Get help the first time if you can, since it is stressful for the fish.\n- Treat the whole tank with an antiparasitic that targets crustaceans, such as one containing diflubenzuron, repeating as the label directs to catch new larvae.\n- These medications kill shrimp, crayfish and other crustaceans, so move invertebrates out first. Snails are generally safer but check the label.\n- Watch the wounds for infection, and use an antibacterial treatment if they turn white or fuzzy.", "heading": "Treatment"}, {"text": "Quarantine all new fish for at least 4 weeks and inspect them closely. Avoid adding pond water or wild-collected live food to indoor tanks.", "heading": "Prevention"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$anchor-worms$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set faq = $g$[{"a": "Four to six weeks is the common recommendation. Two weeks only catches fast diseases like ich, so treat 4 weeks as the minimum.", "q": "How long should I quarantine new fish?"}, {"a": "It is not required, but it is the best way to keep disease out of an established tank. One sick new fish can wipe out a whole display.", "q": "Do I really need a quarantine tank?"}, {"a": "Yes, but you will need daily ammonia testing and frequent water changes. Keeping a sponge filter seeded in your main tank is much easier.", "q": "Can I quarantine fish without a cycled filter?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$quarantine$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Faded or darkened color\n- Hiding more than usual or pacing the glass\n- Clamped fins\n- Loss of appetite\n- Rapid breathing or hanging at the surface\n- Scratching against decor\n- Stress stripes or blotches in species like bettas and cichlids", "heading": "Signs of a stressed fish"}, {"text": "- Ammonia, nitrite or high nitrate\n- Temperature or pH swings, including from large untreated water changes\n- Aggressive or fin-nipping tankmates\n- A tank that is too small, overcrowded or bare, with nowhere to hide\n- Keeping schooling fish alone or in groups under 6\n- Shipping, netting and moving between tanks\n- Loud vibration, constant tapping, or lights left on around the clock\n\nShort-term stress from a move usually passes within days. Ongoing stress from a poor setup is the bigger danger, because it wears fish down slowly.", "heading": "Common causes"}, {"text": "Start by testing your water and fixing anything off. Keep the temperature steady with a properly sized heater, and do regular water changes of 20 to 30 percent with dechlorinated, temperature-matched water. Research every species before you buy: right tank size, compatible tankmates, preferred water and group size. Add plants, rocks or wood so fish can break line of sight. Give a regular light schedule of about 6 to 8 hours with darkness at night.", "heading": "How to reduce it"}, {"text": "Dim the lights for the first day, acclimate slowly, and feed lightly. Some fish hide for a few days after arrival, which is normal. Rearranging decor slightly when adding fish to a tank with territorial species can reduce aggression toward newcomers.", "heading": "Helping new fish settle"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$stress$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Floating at the top, often tilted or upside down\n- Sinking to the bottom and struggling to rise\n- Swimming sideways, head-down or tail-down\n- A curved back or swollen belly\n- Struggling to stay level after eating", "heading": "Signs to watch for"}, {"text": "- Overfeeding and constipation, especially with dry floating foods\n- Gulping air while eating at the surface\n- Body shape: round fancy goldfish have compressed organs\n- Cold water, which slows digestion\n- Bacterial infection or internal parasites\n- Injury from a fall or fight\n- Genetic defects in some fish", "heading": "Common causes"}, {"text": "- Fast the fish for 1 to 3 days. This alone fixes many mild cases.\n- Raise the temperature slightly within the species' range, such as to the upper 70s for bettas or around 72°F for goldfish, to help digestion.\n- After fasting, offer a small amount of a high-fiber food. Shelled, cooked peas are a long-standing remedy; some keepers question them, but a small piece is fine for goldfish and other plant-eaters. Skip peas for meat-eaters like bettas.\n- Soak dry food before feeding and switch goldfish to sinking pellets.\n- Lower the water level in a hospital tank so a struggling fish can reach the surface easily.\n- If the fish has other signs like clamped fins, red streaks or a swollen belly with raised scales, treat for infection with an antibacterial.", "heading": "How to treat it"}, {"text": "Some fish, especially round goldfish, have permanent buoyancy problems. They can still live well with shallow water, smooth surfaces to rest on, small soaked meals and a careful diet. Watch for sores on fish that rest at the surface or bottom constantly.", "heading": "Living with chronic cases"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "Many cases caused by diet or constipation clear up within days with fasting and better food. Cases caused by body shape or injury may be permanent.", "q": "Can swim bladder disease be cured?"}, {"a": "A small piece of cooked, shelled pea can help constipated goldfish and other plant-eating fish pass waste. Skip it for meat-eaters like bettas. It is a fiber remedy, not a cure for infection or injury.", "q": "Do peas help swim bladder disorder?"}, {"a": "No. It is usually caused by diet, body shape or injury. If an infection is behind it, other fish could be at risk.", "q": "Is swim bladder disorder contagious?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$swim-bladder-disorder$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set faq = $g$[{"a": "Local fish store, usually an independent aquarium shop.", "q": "What does LFS stand for in fishkeeping?"}, {"a": "A good LFS usually has healthier stock and better advice, but quality varies at both. Judge the specific store by its tanks.", "q": "Is it better to buy fish from an LFS or a chain store?"}, {"a": "Yes. Quarantine any new fish for 4 to 6 weeks if you can, no matter where you bought it.", "q": "Should I quarantine fish from a local fish store?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$lfs$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- New tank that is not fully cycled\n- Adding too many fish at once\n- Overfeeding or uneaten food rotting\n- A dead fish, snail or large amount of decaying plant matter\n- Cleaning or replacing all filter media at once, or rinsing it in chlorinated tap water\n- Power outage that stops the filter for hours\n- Medications that harm beneficial bacteria", "heading": "Common causes"}, {"text": "Fish gasp at the surface, breathe rapidly, sit near the bottom with clamped fins or show red or inflamed gills. Water may turn cloudy or smell off. A test reading anywhere above 0 ppm in an established tank is a warning. Ammonia is more toxic at higher pH and temperature, so 0.5 ppm at pH 8 is much more dangerous than the same reading at pH 6.5.", "heading": "Signs to watch for"}, {"text": "Do a 30 to 50 percent water change with temperature-matched, dechlorinated water, and repeat daily until ammonia reads 0. Use a water conditioner that binds ammonia to make it less toxic for about 24 hours. Stop feeding for a day or two. Find and remove the source, whether a dead animal or rotting food. Add aeration, since stressed fish need oxygen.", "heading": "What to do right away"}, {"text": "Cycle tanks fully before adding fish, add new fish gradually, feed lightly and clean filter media in old tank water only. Test weekly, and always test after adding fish or after any disruption.", "heading": "Preventing future spikes"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$ammonia-spike$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- A running tank with filter and heater, filled with dechlorinated water.\n- An ammonia source: pure ammonia with no soaps or scents, ammonium chloride, or a pinch of fish food left to rot.\n- A liquid test kit for ammonia, nitrite and nitrate.\n- Optional: bottled bacteria or media from a healthy tank.", "heading": "What you need"}, {"text": "Dose ammonia to about 2 ppm and test every day or two. When ammonia starts dropping, nitrite will appear. Redose ammonia to 2 ppm whenever it falls to near zero.\n\nOnce nitrate shows up and nitrite begins falling, keep going. The tank is ready when a 2 ppm dose turns into 0 ppm ammonia and 0 ppm nitrite within 24 hours. Do a large water change to lower nitrate, then add fish. Write down each test result so you can see the pattern and know when nitrite has peaked.", "heading": "Step by step"}, {"text": "Raise the temperature to around 78 to 82°F, keep the pH above 7, and run good surface agitation for oxygen. Seeded media can cut weeks off the process. Without help, expect 4 to 8 weeks.", "heading": "Tips to go faster"}, {"text": "Overdosing ammonia far above 4 ppm can stall the cycle. If nitrite stays sky high for more than a week, do a water change to bring it down. Keep dosing ammonia right up until fish go in, or the colony will start to shrink. Also check that your ammonia source contains nothing but ammonia and water; if it foams when shaken, it has surfactants and should not be used.", "heading": "Common problems"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$fishless-cycling$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Cleaning the filter in tap water or replacing all the media at once.\n- Adding many fish in one go.\n- A filter that sat off for hours during a power outage.\n- Some medications, especially certain antibiotics, dosed in the main tank.\n- A big jump in feeding, or a dead fish or snail rotting unnoticed.", "heading": "Common causes"}, {"text": "Cloudy white water, fish gasping at the surface, red gills, clamped fins or sudden lethargy. A test kit is the only sure sign: any ammonia or nitrite reading above 0 ppm in an established tank means the bacteria are behind. Cloudy water alone can also be a harmless bacterial bloom, which is why testing matters more than looks.", "heading": "Signs to watch for"}, {"text": "Change 25 to 50 percent of the water and repeat daily or every other day while ammonia plus nitrite is above 0.25 ppm. Use a dechlorinator that binds ammonia.\n\nFeed lightly, every other day if needed, and do not clean the filter again until readings are back at zero. Adding bottled bacteria or seeded media can speed recovery. Skip adding new fish until the tank tests clean for about a week.", "heading": "How to fix it"}, {"text": "Rinse media in removed tank water, replace no more than a third at a time, stock new fish gradually, and test for a few days after any big change. If the power goes out for hours, rinse the filter sponge in tank water before restarting it, since stagnant media can release a burst of waste.", "heading": "How to prevent it"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$mini-cycle$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Pressurized CO2 with a regulator, solenoid, bubble counter and diffuser or reactor\n- A drop checker to confirm CO2 levels, usually aiming for lime green, roughly 20 to 30 ppm\n- A strong planted-tank light on a timer\n- A nutrient-rich active substrate or root tabs\n- A dosing routine for macro and micro nutrients\n- Good flow so CO2 and nutrients reach every plant", "heading": "What you need"}, {"text": "Turn CO2 on 1 to 2 hours before the lights and off about an hour before they go off. Keep a 6 to 8 hour photoperiod, and dose fertilizer several times a week or daily. Change 30 to 50 percent of the water weekly to reset nutrient levels. Expect to trim stems every week or two, since fast growth is the point.", "heading": "Running it day to day"}, {"text": "Too much CO2 suffocates fish. Signs include fish gasping at the surface or hanging near the outflow in the morning. Raise CO2 slowly over several days, watch your fish after each change, and keep good surface movement so oxygen stays high. A solenoid on a timer prevents CO2 running all night when plants aren't using it.", "heading": "Fish and CO2 safety"}, {"text": "The biggest mistake is turning the light up before CO2 and fertilizer are dialed in. Others include fluctuating CO2, too little flow, and letting dead leaves rot in place. When algae shows up, fix the balance first, lowering light if needed, rather than reaching for chemicals.", "heading": "Common mistakes"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "No. Many plants grow well in low-tech tanks. CO2 matters when you want fast growth, carpets and demanding species under strong light.", "q": "Do I need CO2 for a planted tank?"}, {"a": "About 20 to 30 ppm is the common target for a planted tank, and most community fish tolerate it with good surface agitation. Watch for gasping and back off if you see it.", "q": "How much CO2 is safe for fish?"}, {"a": "Usually CO2 is too low or unstable for the light intensity, or flow is poor. Lower the light, stabilize CO2 and check that nutrients aren't running out.", "q": "Why does my high-tech tank have so much algae?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$high-tech-tank$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Freshwater: about 1.000\n- Brackish: about 1.002 to 1.018, depending on species\n- Fish-only saltwater: about 1.023 to 1.026\n- Reef tanks: about 1.025 to 1.026, which equals roughly 35 ppt\n\nWhat matters most is keeping it stable. Corals and invertebrates react badly to swings of even a few thousandths.", "heading": "Target ranges"}, {"text": "Refractometers are the most popular tool. Calibrate with 35 ppt calibration fluid rather than RO water, since that checks accuracy right where you need it. Place a few drops on the prism, close the cover and read the line.\n\nSwing-arm hydrometers float an arm in a water sample. They're cheap but can stick, collect salt crust or read wrong with bubbles. Glass floating hydrometers are another option. Digital meters exist but need regular calibration too.\n\nWhatever tool you use, check it against calibration fluid every month or two, and rinse it with fresh water after each reading so salt crust doesn't skew the next one.", "heading": "How to measure it"}, {"text": "Evaporation removes only water, so salinity rises between top-offs. Top off with fresh RO or dechlorinated water daily or with an automatic top-off unit. Mix new saltwater a day ahead with a heater and pump, then check specific gravity and temperature before a water change.", "heading": "Keeping it stable"}, {"text": "- Calibrating with tap or RO water and assuming the high end is accurate\n- Reading cold, freshly mixed water without temperature compensation\n- Topping off with saltwater instead of fresh water\n- Changing salinity quickly when adjusting brackish fish", "heading": "Common mistakes"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$specific-gravity$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Ammonia and nitrite: should always read 0 ppm in a cycled tank\n- Nitrate: under 20 ppm, and change water before it reaches 40 ppm\n- pH: most community fish handle 6.5 to 7.8\n- Temperature: most tropical fish 74 to 80 F\n- GH: about 4 to 12 dGH for most community fish\n- KH: 3 dKH or more helps keep pH steady\n\nThese are general guides. Check the needs of each species you keep.", "heading": "The core parameters"}, {"text": "Fish adapt to a wide range, especially captive-bred ones, but they handle sudden changes poorly. A steady pH of 7.6 is better than chasing 6.8 with chemicals and causing daily swings. Make changes slowly, keep water changes regular and consistent, and match temperature and conditioner every time.\n\nWhen you do need to change a parameter, move it a little at a time over days or weeks, and retest after each step.", "heading": "Stability beats perfection"}, {"text": "Start by testing your tap water. If it's hard and alkaline, livebearers, rainbowfish and many cichlids will be easy. If it's soft and acidic, tetras, rasboras and many catfish suit it. Choosing fish that fit your water is far easier than reshaping your water to fit the fish.", "heading": "Choosing compatible fish"}, {"text": "Write down test results with dates, along with water changes, feeding and new additions. A log shows trends like slowly rising nitrate or falling KH before fish suffer, and it makes it much easier to get useful help when something does go wrong.", "heading": "Keeping a log"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "For most community tanks: 0 ammonia, 0 nitrite, nitrate under 20 ppm (never above 40), pH 6.5 to 7.8 and temperature 74 to 80 F.", "q": "What are the ideal water parameters for a freshwater aquarium?"}, {"a": "Usually not. Most fish adapt to a stable pH, and chemical adjustments often cause dangerous swings.", "q": "Should I adjust my pH to match my fish?"}, {"a": "Do regular water changes, avoid overfeeding and overstocking, and keep KH high enough to buffer pH.", "q": "How do I keep water parameters stable?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$water-parameters$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "Freshwater fish are generally hardier, cheaper and easier to find than marine fish. Equipment is simpler, with no need for salt mixing, protein skimmers or salinity checks. Mistakes are more forgiving, and dechlorinated tap water works for most species.", "heading": "Why start with freshwater"}, {"text": "- A tank of 20 gallons or more if possible, since bigger tanks are more stable\n- A filter rated for your tank size\n- A heater for tropical fish, set around 76 to 78 F\n- A thermometer, light and lid\n- Substrate, decor or plants\n- Water conditioner and a liquid test kit\n- A gravel vacuum and bucket used only for the aquarium", "heading": "Basic equipment"}, {"text": "Community tanks with peaceful mixed species are the most popular. Other options include planted tanks, species-only tanks, cold water goldfish tanks, shrimp tanks, African cichlid tanks with hard alkaline water, and blackwater tanks with soft, tea-colored water. Each has different water and stocking needs.", "heading": "Types of freshwater setups"}, {"text": "Set up the tank, add conditioned water and run the filter. Cycle it for about 4 to 8 weeks so beneficial bacteria can handle waste, testing ammonia and nitrite along the way. Then add a few hardy fish, feed lightly and do weekly water changes of 20 to 30 percent.\n\nResist the urge to fill the tank all at once. Add a few fish every couple of weeks, test the water after each addition, and only add more once ammonia and nitrite stay at zero.", "heading": "First steps"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$freshwater$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set faq = $g$[{"a": "Most people mean 10 gallons or less, though some use the term for anything under about 20 gallons.", "q": "What size is a nano tank?"}, {"a": "They can be, but parameters change fast. A 20 gallon or larger tank is more forgiving for a first tank.", "q": "Are nano tanks good for beginners?"}, {"a": "Usually one betta, or a shrimp colony with a few snails. Most schooling fish need 10 gallons or more.", "q": "How many fish can I put in a 5 gallon tank?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$nano-tank$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Egg scatterers: tetras, danios, barbs and rasboras spray eggs over plants or gravel and leave them, often eating them right away.\n- Egg depositors: corydoras, many killifish and ricefish stick eggs on glass, plants or mops and leave them unguarded.\n- Substrate spawners: angelfish, discus and many other cichlids lay on a cleaned rock, leaf or slate and guard the eggs and fry.\n- Cave spawners: plecos and many dwarf cichlids lay inside caves, usually guarded by a parent.\n- Bubble nesters: bettas and gouramis place eggs in a floating nest.\n- Mouthbrooders: carry eggs in the mouth.\n- Egg buriers: annual killifish lay eggs in mud, and the eggs survive the dry season until rain returns.", "heading": "Types of egg layers"}, {"text": "For scatterers, give eggs somewhere to fall out of reach: a layer of marbles, a mesh grid above the bottom, a spawning mop or a thick clump of moss. Remove the adults after spawning. For species that guard their eggs, it is often best to leave the parents alone in their own tank, since some pairs learn to raise fry well.", "heading": "Protecting the eggs"}, {"text": "Most aquarium eggs hatch in about 1 to 5 days depending on species and temperature, though some, such as pleco eggs, can take a week or more. Unfertilized eggs turn white and fuzzy with fungus within a day or two, and fungus can spread to good eggs. Remove white eggs with a pipette, keep gentle water flow over the clutch, and consider hatching eggs in a separate container if fungus is a regular problem.", "heading": "Hatching and fungus"}, {"text": "Newly hatched fry live off their yolk sac for anywhere from about a day to a week, depending on species. Once they swim freely, most need very tiny first foods such as infusoria or microworms before moving on to baby brine shrimp.", "heading": "Feeding the fry"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$egg-layer$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "Males have a modified anal fin called a gonopodium that delivers sperm. Females can store sperm for several months, so one mating can produce several broods, even with no male present. Gestation usually takes about 3 to 5 weeks depending on species and temperature, with mollies often taking longer, and a brood can range from a few fry to 100 or more from a large female.", "heading": "How livebearers reproduce"}, {"text": "- A growing belly that becomes boxy or squared off just before birth\n- A dark gravid spot near the vent that gets larger and darker\n- Hiding, staying near the heater or bottom, or refusing food\n- Restless swimming in one corner shortly before dropping fry", "heading": "Signs of pregnancy and birth"}, {"text": "Keep 1 male for every 2 or 3 females so no single female is harassed constantly. If you don't want fry, keep a single sex or keep a few hungry tankmates that will eat most of them. Dense plants like moss and floating plants help some fry survive if you do want to raise them. Mollies and platies also appreciate harder, more alkaline water, around 7.5 to 8.2.", "heading": "Keeping things under control"}, {"text": "Livebearer fry are born large enough to eat crushed flakes and baby brine shrimp right away. Feed small amounts several times a day, keep the water clean with small, frequent changes, and expect most fry to reach a safe size in about 4 to 6 weeks.", "heading": "Fry care"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "Guppies, endlers, platies and swordtails usually take about 3 to 5 weeks. Mollies often take longer, and cooler water stretches it out for all of them.", "q": "How long are livebearers pregnant?"}, {"a": "She can give birth without a male present by using stored sperm from an earlier mating, sometimes for several broods.", "q": "Can a female guppy get pregnant without a male?"}, {"a": "Yes, adults often eat fry. Dense plants, a breeder box or a separate tank improve survival.", "q": "Do livebearers eat their babies?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$livebearer$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "The checker holds a few milliliters of indicator fluid separated from the tank water by a small pocket of air. CO2 passes through that air gap into the fluid and changes its pH, which changes its color. Because only gas crosses over, things like fertilizers and driftwood tannins don't throw off the reading.", "heading": "How it works"}, {"text": "- Blue: too little CO2, roughly under 15 ppm\n- Green: the usual target, around 20 to 30 ppm\n- Yellow: too much CO2, roughly 40 to 50 ppm or more, a danger to fish\n\nUse the 4 dKH reference solution, not tank water, for an accurate reading. Tank water in the checker makes the color meaningless.", "heading": "Reading the colors"}, {"text": "Place the drop checker low in the tank, away from the CO2 diffuser and out of any direct bubble stream, ideally on the side opposite the diffuser. Replace the fluid every 2 to 4 weeks, since it slowly drifts. Look at it against a white background to judge the color more easily.", "heading": "Setup tips"}, {"text": "A drop checker takes about 1 to 2 hours to react to changes, so it shows the average level over the past while, not what is happening right now. Adjust your bubble rate in small steps and wait several hours before judging. For faster readings, some keepers also track the pH drop: a fall of about 1 full point from degassed water roughly equals 30 ppm.", "heading": "Lag time and limits"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$drop-checker$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Most plecos and many other suckermouth catfish\n- Kuhli loaches and many other loaches\n- Pictus catfish and upside-down catfish\n- Black ghost knifefish and other knifefish\n- Many shrimp, snails and crayfish become more active after dark\n\nSome species, like corydoras, are crepuscular, most active at dawn and dusk.", "heading": "Common nocturnal fish"}, {"text": "Day-active tankmates eat most food before nocturnal fish come out. Drop sinking pellets, wafers or frozen food in shortly after lights go out, or 15 to 30 minutes before. Target caves and hiding spots, and check that bellies look rounded rather than sunken. Remove uneaten food the next morning if it is still there.", "heading": "Feeding them"}, {"text": "Give each nocturnal fish a cave, driftwood hollow or tube to rest in during the day. A tank with plenty of cover, dim areas under floating plants and fewer hyperactive tankmates helps them venture out sooner. Many become more visible over time as they settle in.", "heading": "Helping them feel secure"}, {"text": "A dim blue or red light lets you watch without fully disrupting their night, since many fish see red light poorly. Keep it low and avoid running bright lights around the clock. Most tanks do well with 6 to 8 hours of light and a real dark period, which both day and night species need. Avoid tapping the glass or switching bright room lights on suddenly at night, since startled nocturnal fish can crash into decor.", "heading": "Watching them at night"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "Yes. Fish need a regular dark period for rest and normal behavior. Most tanks do well with lights on about 6 to 8 hours a day, so they get a long, regular dark period.", "q": "Do fish need darkness at night?"}, {"a": "Most plecos are nocturnal and hide in caves or under wood during the day. Look for them after lights out.", "q": "Why do I never see my pleco?"}, {"a": "A very dim light for an hour or two is fine for viewing, but leaving it on all night can disturb sleep patterns. Most keepers put it on a timer.", "q": "Is a moonlight on all night OK for fish?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$nocturnal$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "Ich, short for Ichthyophthirius multifiliis, is a single-celled parasite and the most common disease in freshwater aquariums. It shows up as white spots that look like grains of salt on the body, fins and gills. It usually arrives with new fish or plants and takes hold when fish are stressed by a temperature drop, poor water or shipping. Saltwater fish get a similar-looking but different parasite, Cryptocaryon.", "heading": "What ich is"}, {"text": "- White pinhead spots on skin, fins and gills\n- Flashing: fish scratching against rocks and decor\n- Clamped fins and rapid breathing\n- Hiding and loss of appetite", "heading": "Symptoms"}, {"text": "The spots you see are parasites buried under the skin, where medication can't reach them. Each one eventually drops off, forms a cyst on the bottom and releases hundreds to thousands of free-swimming young that hunt for a new fish. Treatment only kills that free-swimming stage, so you have to keep treating long enough to catch every generation, typically 10 to 14 days and at least 3 days after the last spot disappears.", "heading": "Why treatment takes time"}, {"text": "- Raise the temperature slowly to around 82 to 86°F if all your fish tolerate it, never higher; heat speeds up the life cycle so treatment works faster. Add aeration, since warm water holds less oxygen.\n- Use an ich medication and follow the label. Many ich medications harm shrimp, snails and other invertebrates, so check the label or move them first. Remove carbon from the filter while treating.\n- Some keepers use aquarium salt instead, but scaleless fish, many plants and invertebrates don't tolerate it.\n- Vacuum the gravel to pick up cysts, and keep up with water changes.\n- Treat the whole tank, not just the fish showing spots.", "heading": "How to treat ich"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "Yes. If one fish has it, the parasite is in the tank. Treat the whole aquarium.", "q": "Is ich contagious?"}, {"a": "Sometimes healthy fish fight off a light case, but it often explodes. Treating early is much safer.", "q": "Can ich go away on its own?"}, {"a": "Quarantine new fish for at least 4 weeks, ideally 4 to 6, avoid sudden temperature swings and keep water quality high.", "q": "How do I prevent ich?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$ich$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "New tank syndrome is the wave of sick and dying fish in an aquarium that hasn't built up its beneficial bacteria yet. Without those bacteria, ammonia and then nitrite climb to toxic levels within days of adding fish. The tank looks fine, and the water is often crystal clear, but it is poisoning the fish.", "heading": "What new tank syndrome is"}, {"text": "- Fish gasping at the surface or hanging near the filter\n- Red or inflamed gills\n- Lethargy, clamped fins, not eating\n- Cloudy water from a bacterial bloom in the first week or two\n- Test kit showing ammonia or nitrite above 0", "heading": "Signs"}, {"text": "- Cycle the tank before adding fish (fishless cycling), feeding it an ammonia source until it can turn a dose into 0 ppm ammonia and 0 ppm nitrite within about a day.\n- Seed the filter with media or sponge from a healthy tank.\n- Add fish a few at a time over weeks, not all at once.\n- Feed lightly and test every few days for the first two months.", "heading": "How to prevent it"}, {"text": "Test daily. Whenever ammonia plus nitrite goes above 0.25 ppm, do a 30 to 50 percent water change with dechlorinated water, feed lightly and use a conditioner that detoxifies ammonia and nitrite. Don't clean or replace the filter media. It usually settles in 4 to 8 weeks.", "heading": "If fish are already in the tank"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$new-tank-syndrome$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Platies, mollies and swordtails: colorful livebearers that eat anything; mollies do best in harder water\n- Zebra and leopard danios: very tough, active, best in groups of 6 or more\n- White Cloud Mountain minnows: unheated tanks around 60 to 72°F\n- Corydoras catfish: peaceful bottom dwellers, kept in groups\n- Harlequin rasboras and many tetras: once the tank is fully cycled\n- A single betta: in 5 gallons or more with a heater", "heading": "Good choices for a first tank"}, {"text": "Common plecos grow to over 12 inches. Goldfish are hardy but get large and very messy, needing much more space than a small bowl. Oscars and other big cichlids outgrow most starter tanks quickly. Bala sharks and iridescent sharks are schooling fish that reach a foot or more. Always check adult size before buying.\n\nAlso be wary of fish that need very specific water. Discus, wild bettas, many Caridina shrimp and most saltwater species are best left until you have experience.", "heading": "Fish that are often sold to beginners but shouldn't be"}, {"text": "Cycle the tank before adding fish, or use a fish-in cycle with a few hardy fish and daily testing. Add fish a few at a time over several weeks so the beneficial bacteria can keep up. A 20 to 30 gallon tank is actually easier than a tiny one, because water quality stays more stable.", "heading": "Setting yourself up to succeed"}, {"text": "Once you have kept a tank stable for a few months, you can try more sensitive fish like cardinal tetras, apistogrammas or shrimp.", "heading": "Next steps"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$beginner-fish$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- 10 to 20 gallons: a betta or a honey or dwarf gourami\n- 20 to 40 gallons: a pair of German blue rams, a pair of apistogrammas, a pair of kribensis or, toward the upper end, a pearl gourami\n- 40 to 75 gallons: a group of rainbowfish or a bristlenose pleco with character, and from about 55 gallons a pair or small group of angelfish\n- 75 gallons and up: a group of discus, a severum, or a larger peaceful cichlid", "heading": "Popular choices by tank size"}, {"text": "Check the adult size, not the size in the store. Make sure the fish fits your water: discus and rams want warm, soft water, roughly 80 to 86°F, while many rainbowfish are fine in harder water. Consider personality too. Gouramis and cichlids interact with their keepers, which is part of the appeal.", "heading": "How to choose"}, {"text": "Build the community around the centerpiece. Avoid fin nippers like tiger barbs with long-finned fish such as angelfish and bettas. Remember that angelfish will eat small fish like neon tetras as they grow. Pick dither fish, such as a school of medium tetras or rasboras, that are too big to be eaten and calm enough not to stress the main fish.\n\nAdd the centerpiece last when possible, after the dither fish have settled, so it isn't the only fish in the tank while you finish stocking.", "heading": "Planning tankmates"}, {"text": "Some centerpiece species, like many gouramis, fight with their own kind in small tanks. Others, like angelfish or discus, do better in groups. Research before deciding whether to buy one or several.", "heading": "One or several?"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "A honey or dwarf gourami, a betta or, in a 20 long, a pair of German blue rams are common choices for a 20 gallon community.", "q": "What is a good centerpiece fish for a 20 gallon tank?"}, {"a": "Sometimes, if they are compatible and the tank is big enough. Two male gouramis or two territorial cichlids in a small tank often fight.", "q": "Can you have two centerpiece fish?"}, {"a": "Yes, they are a classic choice, with about 55 gallons, ideally a tall tank, as a sensible minimum for a pair or small group, but they will eat small fish like neon tetras.", "q": "Are angelfish good centerpiece fish?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$centerpiece-fish$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Tank: 5 gallons or more, cycled and stable for at least a few weeks\n- Temperature: 65 to 80°F, around 72 to 76°F is ideal\n- pH: about 6.5 to 8.0\n- GH: about 6 to 10; KH: about 2 to 8\n- Size: about 1 to 1.5 inches\n- Lifespan: around 1 to 2 years", "heading": "Care basics"}, {"text": "They graze biofilm and soft algae all day. Add a small amount of shrimp food, blanched spinach or crushed algae wafer a few times a week, and remove leftovers within a few hours. Overfeeding is a common reason colonies crash.", "heading": "Feeding"}, {"text": "A healthy colony breeds on its own. Females carry eggs for about 3 to 4 weeks, and babies hatch fully formed. Moss, plants and a sponge filter or pre-filter keep the young safe. Selectively bred colors tend to revert toward wild brown over generations, so many keepers remove dull shrimp to keep colors strong.", "heading": "Breeding"}, {"text": "Small, peaceful fish like otocinclus, chili rasboras and pygmy corydoras work best. Most other fish, including bettas and many tetras, will eat babies and sometimes adults. Copper in medications and snail treatments is deadly to shrimp, and some plant fertilizers contain more than trace amounts, so check every product. Acclimate new shrimp slowly, by drip if possible, over 1 to 2 hours.\n\nColors range from red to yellow, orange, blue, green and black. Mixing color lines produces wild-type brown babies over time, so keep one color per tank if you want them to stay bright.", "heading": "Tankmates and dangers"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$cherry-shrimp$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Shrimp: dwarf shrimp like cherry and crystal shrimp, plus larger amano and ghost shrimp\n- Snails: nerite, mystery, ramshorn, Malaysian trumpet and rabbit snails\n- Crayfish and dwarf crayfish, which are messy and often eat plants and slow fish\n- Freshwater crabs, many of which need land areas and escape-proof lids\n- Clams and mussels, which filter feed and are hard to keep alive long term", "heading": "Common freshwater inverts"}, {"text": "Most inverts want stable temperature and parameters more than any exact number. Shrimp and snails need some hardness to build shells and molt, so a GH of 6 to 10 degrees and KH of 2 to 8 suits many common species, while crystal shrimp prefer softer water. Ammonia and nitrite must be at zero, and nitrate ideally under 20 ppm.\n\nThey graze biofilm and algae, but most also need supplemental foods like blanched vegetables or sinking invert foods with calcium.", "heading": "Water and diet needs"}, {"text": "Many fish see small shrimp as food, so pair them with tiny, peaceful species or give them dense plants and moss. Never use copper-based medications around inverts, and check fertilizers and treatments for warnings. Acclimate slowly, often by drip over an hour, since sudden changes can cause failed molts.", "heading": "Tankmates and safety"}, {"text": "Adding shrimp to an uncycled tank, keeping them with larger fish, and making big sudden water changes with very different water are the main causes of losses. Moderate, regular changes with matched temperature work best.", "heading": "Common mistakes"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$invertebrate$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "Young shrimp molt every week or two, adults roughly every 3 to 6 weeks. The shell splits between the head and tail and the shrimp backs out, leaving a whole clear ghost of itself behind. The molt is not a dead shrimp; a dead one is usually pink, white or opaque and does not move.\n\nLeave the old shell in the tank. Shrimp eat it to recycle calcium and minerals.", "heading": "What a normal molt looks like"}, {"text": "A shrimp stuck partway out with a white ring around its middle, often called the white ring of death, is having trouble. Common causes:\n\n- GH too low, so there are not enough minerals to build a new shell\n- GH too high or sudden swings, which can make the shell too hard to split\n- Big water changes with very different water\n- Poor diet without calcium and iodine\n- Copper or other toxins in the water", "heading": "Failed molts"}, {"text": "Keep GH around 6 to 10 degrees for most neocaridina and amano shrimp, or 4 to 6 for many caridina. Test tap water and match it closely during changes. Do smaller water changes of 10 to 20 percent weekly rather than big infrequent ones. Feed a varied diet with mineral-rich foods, and provide moss, plants and caves so freshly molted shrimp can hide.", "heading": "How to support healthy molting"}, {"text": "Female shrimp molt just before mating and release scent that sends males racing around the tank. Berried females carry eggs until they hatch, and a new molt soon after hatching is normal.", "heading": "Molting and breeding"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$molting$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "Fish waste, uneaten food, decaying plants and dead animals all break down into ammonia. Big-bodied and messy fish like goldfish, plecos, oscars and cichlids produce far more waste than the same length of slim tetras. Feeding is the part you control most directly, since every bit of food that goes in eventually becomes waste.", "heading": "What adds to bioload"}, {"text": "- Beneficial bacteria in the filter and substrate convert ammonia to nitrite and then nitrate\n- Live plants absorb ammonia and nitrate as nutrients\n- Water changes physically remove nitrate and dissolved waste\n- Water volume dilutes waste, so bigger tanks are more forgiving\n\nA tank is in balance when ammonia and nitrite stay at 0 ppm and nitrate rises slowly enough that your normal water changes keep it under about 20 ppm.", "heading": "What handles bioload"}, {"text": "Ammonia or nitrite readings above zero in a cycled tank, nitrate that climbs past 40 ppm within a week, cloudy water, algae blooms and fish gasping at the surface are common signals. Low oxygen often comes with high bioload because waste breakdown uses oxygen too.", "heading": "Signs the bioload is too high"}, {"text": "Add new fish slowly, a few at a time, so bacteria can catch up. Feed sparingly. Upgrade filtration or increase water changes for heavy-waste species. Research adult size and waste output before buying, not just the size at the store. When unsure, stock lighter than you think you can.", "heading": "How to manage it"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$bioload$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Adult size, not store size\n- Minimum tank length for active swimmers\n- Water needs: temperature, pH and hardness that all species share\n- Temperament: peaceful, semi-aggressive, fin-nippers or territorial\n- Swimming level: top, middle or bottom, to use the whole tank\n- Group size: schooling fish usually need 6 or more\n- Waste output: goldfish and large cichlids need far more filtration", "heading": "What to consider"}, {"text": "The old one inch of fish per gallon rule is a rough guide at best. It treats one 12 inch adult oscar the same as eight 1.5 inch neon tetras, which is badly wrong. Body mass, activity level and territory matter more than length. Use it only as a sanity check for small, slim community fish.", "heading": "Why simple rules fall short"}, {"text": "Add fish in small groups over weeks, giving bacteria time to catch up. Start with hardy species, often a school of peaceful midwater fish, then add bottom dwellers, and add centerpiece or more territorial fish last so they don't claim the whole tank early. Test ammonia and nitrite a few days after each addition.", "heading": "Stocking in order"}, {"text": "Mixing fish that need very different temperatures, buying single schooling fish, choosing fish based only on color, and putting fin-nippers like tiger barbs with long-finned fish like bettas are the classic errors. Ask about each fish's adult needs before buying. Then buy from a store or breeder that quarantines its stock, and quarantine new fish yourself for 4 to 6 weeks when you can.", "heading": "Common mistakes"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "Roughly a dozen or so small fish, such as 8 neon tetras and 6 small corydoras, depending on species and filtration. Run your list through the Tank Builder to check.", "q": "How many fish can I put in a 20 gallon tank?"}, {"a": "Add 3 to 6 small fish at a time in a cycled tank, then wait a week or two before adding more.", "q": "How many fish should I add at once?"}, {"a": "Only for small, slim fish. It underestimates the needs of large, bulky or messy species.", "q": "Is the inch per gallon rule accurate?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$stocking$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "Most tanks do well with a partial water change of 20 to 30 percent once a week. Heavily stocked tanks or messy fish may need 40 to 50 percent weekly, or two changes a week. Lightly stocked planted tanks can sometimes stretch to every two weeks. Let your nitrate readings guide you: aim to keep nitrate under about 20 ppm, and never let it pass 40 ppm.", "heading": "How much and how often"}, {"text": "- Unplug heaters and filters if the water level will drop below them\n- Siphon water out with a gravel vacuum, cleaning a section of substrate as you go\n- Wipe glass and rinse filter sponges in the removed tank water if needed\n- Prepare new water at about the same temperature, within 2 F\n- If you fill straight from the tap, add dechlorinator for the full tank volume, not just the new water\n- Refill slowly, then plug equipment back in", "heading": "Step by step"}, {"text": "Changing all the water at once swings temperature, pH and hardness, which shocks fish and can trigger failed molts in shrimp. A partial change keeps those swings small. Fish in tanks that have gone months without changes are adjusted to poor water, so restore them with several smaller changes, about 10 to 15 percent every two or three days, rather than one huge one.", "heading": "Why partial and not all"}, {"text": "Forgetting dechlorinator, refilling with water much colder or warmer than the tank, rinsing filter media under tap water, and replacing filter cartridges at the same time as a big change all cause problems. Keep a dedicated bucket that never touches soap.", "heading": "Common mistakes"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$water-change$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Keep the stock light: a few hardy fish, not a full tank.\n- Feed small amounts once a day or every other day.\n- Test ammonia and nitrite daily with a liquid kit.\n- Change 25 to 50 percent of the water whenever ammonia plus nitrite goes above 0.25 ppm.\n- Use a dechlorinator that also binds ammonia and nitrite temporarily.\n- Keep the temperature steady and toward the warmer end of what your fish are comfortable with, since the bacteria grow faster in warmth.", "heading": "How to do it safely"}, {"text": "Bottled bacteria products can shorten the process. A handful of filter media or a squeezed sponge from a healthy tank works even better. Live plants help too, since they take up ammonia directly.\n\nExpect the cycle to take 4 to 8 weeks. It may feel slower than fishless cycling because frequent water changes keep ammonia low, which is the point. Avoid adding shrimp or snails until the cycle is finished, since invertebrates are very sensitive to ammonia and nitrite.", "heading": "Speeding it up"}, {"text": "Gasping at the surface, red or inflamed gills, lethargy, clamped fins and refusing food all suggest ammonia or nitrite stress. Test right away and do a water change if readings are up.", "heading": "Signs your fish are struggling"}, {"text": "The tank is cycled when ammonia and nitrite stay at 0 ppm for about a week without water changes and nitrate is rising. Add new fish a few at a time over the next weeks so the bacteria can keep up. Keep testing weekly after that, since a newly cycled tank can still slip if you overfeed or add too many fish at once.", "heading": "When it is done"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "As many as it takes to keep ammonia plus nitrite at 0.25 ppm or lower. That can mean daily changes early on.", "q": "How many water changes during a fish-in cycle?"}, {"a": "No. Wait until ammonia and nitrite stay at zero, then add fish gradually.", "q": "Can I add more fish during a fish-in cycle?"}, {"a": "Hardy species that tolerate some stress, like zebra danios, white cloud minnows or platies. Avoid sensitive fish and shrimp.", "q": "What fish are best for a fish-in cycle?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$fish-in-cycling$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "- Ammonia: released by fish gills, waste, uneaten food and decay. Harmful even at low readings over time, and more toxic at higher pH and temperature.\n- Nitrite: produced when the first group of bacteria eats ammonia. Also toxic, it blocks fish blood from carrying oxygen.\n- Nitrate: produced when a second group eats nitrite. Much safer, but it builds up and should be kept under about 20 ppm and never allowed past 40 ppm.", "heading": "The three stages"}, {"text": "The bacteria live on surfaces, mostly inside the filter, but also on gravel, decor and plants. They need oxygen and steady flow, which is why the filter should run all the time.\n\nIn a new tank these bacteria do not exist yet. Growing them is called cycling and takes about 4 to 8 weeks. Fishless cycling with a bottled ammonia source is the safest way to grow them before fish arrive.", "heading": "Where it happens"}, {"text": "The cycle stops at nitrate in most aquariums. You remove it with regular water changes, usually 20 to 30 percent a week and more for heavily stocked tanks. Live plants and algae also use some, and a small amount is turned into nitrogen gas in low-oxygen spots.", "heading": "How nitrate leaves the tank"}, {"text": "Feed only what fish eat in a minute or two, add fish gradually, and clean filter media in removed tank water. Test ammonia, nitrite and nitrate regularly. In an established tank, ammonia and nitrite should always read 0 ppm. A liquid test kit is more accurate than paper strips for these readings. If ammonia or nitrite ever shows up, do a water change and look for the cause, such as overfeeding, a dead fish or a recently cleaned filter.", "heading": "Keeping the cycle healthy"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "Ammonia and nitrite should be 0 ppm. Nitrate is best kept under about 20 ppm, with a water change before it reaches 40 ppm.", "q": "What are safe levels of ammonia, nitrite and nitrate?"}, {"a": "Usually 4 to 8 weeks to establish, faster if you seed with media from a mature tank.", "q": "How long does the nitrogen cycle take in a new tank?"}, {"a": "No. The bacteria live on surfaces, not in the water, so dechlorinated water changes do not harm them.", "q": "Do water changes reset the nitrogen cycle?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$nitrogen-cycle$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "Fish waste, decaying food and the nitrogen cycle all produce acids. Carbonate and bicarbonate absorb those acids, keeping pH stable until the buffer gets used up. Once KH runs low, pH can drop quickly, sometimes overnight. That's why stable KH matters more for most fish than hitting an exact pH number.", "heading": "How buffering works"}, {"text": "- Most community tanks: about 3 to 8 dKH (roughly 50 to 140 ppm)\n- Soft-water fish like many tetras and discus: about 1 to 4 dKH, kept stable\n- Caridina shrimp: around 0 to 1 dKH, with extra care\n- Livebearers and goldfish: about 8 to 12 dKH\n- African rift lake cichlids: 10 dKH or higher\n\nTest KH every week or two, especially in older tanks with long gaps between water changes.", "heading": "Recommended KH levels"}, {"text": "Regular water changes with tap water usually restore KH. Crushed coral or aragonite in the filter or substrate dissolves slowly to raise KH and pH. Baking soda (sodium bicarbonate) raises KH quickly; a common guide is about 1 teaspoon per 50 gallons to raise roughly 1 dKH, added slowly over a day or two. Commercial buffer products work too, but follow the label and avoid big jumps.", "heading": "Ways to add buffering"}, {"text": "Chasing pH with chemicals that lower or raise it without addressing KH leads to swings that are worse than a stable but imperfect number. Don't combine many different pH products. Raise KH gradually, by no more than 1 to 2 dKH a day, and remember that CO2 injection lowers pH temporarily without changing KH.", "heading": "Common mistakes"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$buffer$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "Aim to keep nitrate under 20 ppm and do a water change before it reaches 40 ppm. Keep it as low as you can for sensitive fish, shrimp and fry. Levels above 80 ppm are a clear sign of overdue maintenance and can cause lethargy, faded color and long-term health problems.\n\nTest your tap water too. Some tap water contains 10 to 40 ppm of nitrate already, which changes how low you can realistically go.", "heading": "Safe levels"}, {"text": "- Regular water changes, usually 20 to 30 percent weekly, more for heavily stocked tanks, are the main tool.\n- Vacuum the gravel and clean filters to remove decaying waste.\n- Feed less, and only what fish eat in a minute or two.\n- Add plants, especially fast growers and floating plants, which use nitrate as fertilizer.\n- If tap water is high in nitrate, mix in RO water.", "heading": "How to lower nitrate"}, {"text": "In planted tanks, nitrate is a nutrient, not just a waste product. Many planted tank keepers target 10 to 20 ppm and dose nitrogen fertilizer if it drops to zero, since nitrogen-starved plants yellow from the older leaves first. Zero nitrate in a heavily planted tank isn't a goal by itself.", "heading": "Planted tanks"}, {"text": "Liquid nitrate kits are much more reliable than strips, but one common kit needs its second bottle shaken hard for 30 seconds or more, or it reads falsely low. Read the color in good light against a white background. A sudden jump usually means something died or the tank was overfed.", "heading": "Testing tips"}]$g$::jsonb, faq = $g$[{"a": "Long-term exposure above about 40 ppm harms most fish, and levels over 80 ppm are very high and should be brought down gradually. Sensitive species need it lower.", "q": "What level of nitrate is toxic to fish?"}, {"a": "In most tanks you don't need to. Keeping it under 20 ppm with water changes and plants is the realistic, healthy target.", "q": "How do I get nitrate to zero?"}, {"a": "No. Some conditioners claim to detoxify nitrate for a short time, but none remove it. Only water changes, plants or special media actually remove it.", "q": "Does a water conditioner remove nitrate?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$nitrate$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set faq = $g$[{"a": "During a fishless cycle it often lasts one to three weeks, and sometimes longer. In an established tank with a minor disruption, it usually clears within a few days with water changes.", "q": "How long does a nitrite spike last?"}, {"a": "Zero. Any reading above 0 stresses fish, and 0.5 ppm or more is an emergency.", "q": "What is a safe nitrite level for fish?"}, {"a": "Your ammonia-eating bacteria are working, but the nitrite-eating bacteria haven't caught up yet. It's common during cycling and resolves with time and water changes.", "q": "Why do I have nitrite but no ammonia?"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$nitrite$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "pH measures how acidic or alkaline water is on a scale of 0 to 14, with 7 as neutral. Below 7 is acidic, above 7 is alkaline. The scale is logarithmic, so a pH of 6 is ten times more acidic than 7.", "heading": "What pH measures"}, {"text": "- Most community fish: about 6.5 to 7.8\n- Soft-water fish such as tetras, discus and many South American species: about 6.0 to 7.0\n- African rift lake cichlids: about 7.8 to 8.6\n- Livebearers: about 7.0 to 8.2, with mollies happiest toward the top\n- Reef tanks: about 8.1 to 8.4\n\nStability matters more than hitting an exact number. Most fish adapt to a steady pH slightly outside their ideal range far better than to a pH that keeps changing.", "heading": "Ideal pH for fish tanks"}, {"text": "pH changes how toxic ammonia is: the same ammonia reading is much more dangerous at pH 8 than at pH 6.5. Sudden swings stress fish, and a crash toward acidic levels can stall beneficial bacteria. KH, the water's buffer, is what keeps pH steady.", "heading": "Why it matters"}, {"text": "Change it slowly, no more than a few tenths per day. Driftwood, catappa (Indian almond) leaves and peat lower it gently. Crushed coral, aragonite and limestone raise it. Avoid chasing pH with chemicals over and over; fix the source water or the buffer instead.", "heading": "How to change pH safely"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$ph$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "RO and distilled water have GH and KH near zero. Fish and invertebrates need calcium and magnesium for normal body function, and shrimp need them to molt properly. With no KH, pH can also swing wildly. Using pure RO water straight in a tank is a common and avoidable mistake.", "heading": "Why pure water needs minerals"}, {"text": "- Collect RO or distilled water in a clean container.\n- Add a remineralizer product formulated for your target: GH-only for Caridina shrimp kept on buffering soil, or GH plus KH for Neocaridina, soft-water fish and general community tanks.\n- Stir until fully dissolved, then test GH, KH and TDS.\n- Adjust gradually, and write down how much you used per gallon so you can repeat it exactly.\n- Match temperature, then use it for water changes and top-offs.", "heading": "How to do it"}, {"text": "- Caridina shrimp, such as crystal reds: GH 4 to 6, KH 0 to 1, TDS about 100 to 160\n- Neocaridina shrimp, such as cherry shrimp: GH 6 to 10, KH 2 to 8, TDS about 150 to 250\n- Soft-water fish like discus or wild bettas: GH 2 to 6, KH 1 to 4\n\nThese are typical hobby ranges; breeders vary.", "heading": "Common targets"}, {"text": "Evaporation removes water, not minerals. Top off with plain RO water to replace evaporation, and use remineralized water for water changes, otherwise TDS creeps up. Don't use RO water straight for changes, and avoid making large jumps in GH or TDS, which can cause failed molts in shrimp.", "heading": "Top-offs and mistakes"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$remineralization$g$;
update public.glossary_terms set sections = $g$[{"text": "Match three things: temperament, size and water needs. Avoid fish big enough to swallow tankmates, and avoid pairing fin nippers like tiger barbs with long-finned fish like bettas or angelfish. Then check that temperature, pH and hardness ranges overlap.\n\nThink in layers: a schooling group in the middle, bottom dwellers like corydoras, and maybe one centerpiece fish.", "heading": "Choosing compatible fish"}, {"text": "- 20 gallons: a school of 8 small tetras such as neons, 6 small corydoras such as pandas, and a nerite snail or two\n- 29 gallons: harlequin rasboras, platies, a group of kuhli loaches and a honey gourami\n- 40 gallons or more: a larger tetra school, a single dwarf gourami or a pearl gourami, and a group of corydoras\n\nSchooling fish should be kept in groups of 6 or more.", "heading": "Beginner-friendly combinations"}, {"text": "Cycle the tank first. Add fish in small groups over weeks, starting with the hardiest, so the bacteria keep up. Quarantine new fish for 4 to 6 weeks if you can, since one sick newcomer can infect the whole tank.\n\nWatch new arrivals closely for the first few days for chasing, hiding or torn fins, and be ready to rehome a fish that doesn't fit in. Plenty of plants and decor help break lines of sight and reduce squabbles.", "heading": "Adding fish the right way"}, {"text": "- Buying fish on impulse without checking adult size\n- Keeping schooling fish in groups of two or three\n- Overstocking, which raises waste and aggression\n- Adding a single aggressive fish to a peaceful group", "heading": "Common mistakes"}]$g$::jsonb where slug = $g$community-tank$g$;

-- Course lessons
update public.course_sections set content = $c$Now let's put it all together. A good community tank has a few different "jobs" filled by fish that get along and like the same water.

**A simple plan in four parts:**

1. **A centerpiece fish:** one or a few eye-catching fish, like a honey gourami.
2. **A schooling group:** a group of 6 or more small fish that swim in the middle, like harlequin rasboras.
3. **A bottom crew:** a group of fish that live along the bottom, like corydoras.
4. **Cleanup helpers:** a nerite snail or two to graze on algae.

**Example for a 20-gallon tank:**

- 1 honey gourami
- 6 harlequin rasboras
- 6 panda corydoras
- 1 or 2 nerite snails

All of these are peaceful, stay small, and are comfortable in the mid-70s °F.

**Before you buy,** run your list through the Tank Builder. Then add your fish in small groups over a few weeks, starting with the hardiest ones.$c$ where id = $v$c9348254-f517-400a-b3ec-8a0439936301$v$::uuid;
update public.course_sections set content = $c$**Quarantine** means keeping new fish in a separate tank for a while before adding them to your main tank. It's the single best way to keep disease out.

**Why it matters:** even healthy-looking fish can carry parasites or bacteria. One sick new fish can spread disease to every fish you own. A few weeks in quarantine lets any problem show up where it's easy to treat.

**How to quarantine:**

1. Set up a small tank with a heater and a **sponge filter** that's been running in your main tank, so it already has good bacteria.
2. Put new fish in the quarantine tank instead of your main tank.
3. Watch them closely for **at least 4 weeks**, ideally 4 to 6. Look for spots, fuzz, clamped fins, and whether they eat.
4. Treat any problems right there.
5. Once the full time is up and they've looked healthy for at least the last two weeks, move them to your main tank.

**Plants can bring in hitchhikers too.** Snails, snail eggs, and pests often ride in on new plants. Rinse new plants well and look them over, or keep them in a separate container for a while.

**Don't have room for a quarantine tank?** The same small tank can double as your hospital tank from Fish Health 101.$c$ where id = $v$33c97b86-08c2-4daa-bde7-8fd75c314534$v$::uuid;
update public.course_sections set content = $c$The most counterintuitive beginner fact: **bigger tanks are easier, not harder.** More water dilutes mistakes, so temperature and water chemistry stay stable. A tiny 3 to 5 gallon “desktop” tank swings wildly and punishes small errors. If you can, start with **at least 20 gallons**. It's far more forgiving than the cute little cubes marketed to beginners.

The essential gear: a **filter** rated for your tank size (it's where the helpful bacteria will live), a **heater** (most tropical fish want a stable 74 to 80°F), a **light**, **substrate** (gravel or sand), **water conditioner (dechlorinator)**, a **liquid water test kit** (more accurate than paper strips, and you genuinely need one), and a **siphon and a dedicated bucket** that has never touched soap.

Placement matters: use a stand that can truly hold the weight (water is about **8.3 pounds per gallon**), keep the tank out of **direct sunlight** (it fuels algae), away from drafts and vents, and near an outlet. Rinse the substrate (never use soap), add it, fill the tank, install and start the filter and heater, and then leave it running **without fish.** You're about to find out why.$c$ where id = $v$c5d2f608-4ed0-40ca-a4ae-bc41b7da1ca1$v$::uuid;
update public.course_sections set content = $c$Tap water almost always contains **chlorine or chloramine** to make it safe for people, but those chemicals harm fish and kill the beneficial bacteria you just worked to grow. So **every time** you add tap water, treat it first with a **water conditioner (dechlorinator).** It works instantly. Roughly match the new water's temperature to the tank so you don't shock the fish.

Use your test kit to watch four things: **ammonia: 0**, **nitrite: 0**, **nitrate:** kept low (under about 20 ppm, and never above 40 ppm; this is what water changes remove), and **pH**, where stability matters more than a specific number.

The routine is simple: about a **25% water change once a week**, using your gravel siphon to vacuum debris from the substrate as you drain. Two things to never do: don't replace all the water at once (it wipes out stability), and **never rinse filter media under the tap**, since the chlorine kills your bacteria. When the filter needs cleaning, gently rinse the media in a bucket of old tank water instead.$c$ where id = $v$e6fd6637-2e05-4fb5-ba2b-6dea693dae4a$v$::uuid;
update public.course_sections set content = $c$Fishless cycling grows the bacteria first, by feeding them ammonia yourself, before any fish go in. It's the safest way to start a tank, and no animal suffers while you wait.

**What you need:**

- Pure ammonia with no soap, scent, or surfactants (shake it: if it foams, don't use it), or a bottled ammonia sold for cycling
- A liquid test kit for ammonia, nitrite, and nitrate (strips are less accurate)
- Water conditioner (dechlorinator)
- Your filter running 24/7 and the heater set to about 78 to 82°F, which speeds bacteria growth

**The steps:**

1. Set up the tank fully: substrate, decor, filter, heater. Dechlorinate the water.
2. Add ammonia until the test reads about **2 ppm**. Write down how much it took.
3. Test ammonia and nitrite every 1 to 2 days.
4. When ammonia drops below 1 ppm, dose back up to 2 ppm. Keep doing this.
5. Usually within the first 1 to 3 weeks, nitrite shows up and climbs. The first bacteria are working.
6. Keep dosing ammonia. Eventually nitrite starts dropping and nitrate rises. That's the second group arriving.
7. **The test for done:** dose to 2 ppm, and 24 hours later ammonia AND nitrite both read 0. Pass that two days in a row and you're cycled.
8. Do a big water change (50 to 75%) to bring nitrate down, then add fish within a day or two so the bacteria don't starve.

**Don't overdose.** More ammonia does not cycle faster. Levels above about 4 to 5 ppm, or nitrite left extremely high, can stall the bacteria for weeks. If nitrite goes off the chart, do a partial water change.

**If the cycle stalls:** check pH. Below about 6.5 the bacteria slow way down. A small water change with fresh tap water, or a measured dose of baking soda to raise KH (about 1 teaspoon per 50 gallons raises it roughly 1 dKH), usually gets it moving again.

**Stock gradually.** The colony is sized for the ammonia you were dosing. Add fish in small groups over a few weeks rather than filling the tank at once.$c$ where id = $v$972cf2a9-992a-433d-a2c0-d7a9fdb01bd3$v$::uuid;
update public.course_sections set content = $c$Sometimes fish are already in an uncycled tank: a surprise gift, an impulse buy, or a tank you didn't know needed cycling. You can still get them through it. It just takes daily work for a few weeks.

**The goal:** keep ammonia and nitrite low enough that fish stay healthy, while still leaving enough for the bacteria to grow. Keep ammonia plus nitrite combined at **0.25 ppm or less**, and never let it reach 0.5 ppm.

**Your daily routine:**

1. Test ammonia and nitrite every day, at the same time.
2. If the combined reading is over 0.25 ppm, change enough water to bring it down. A 50% change cuts the level in half.
3. Use a water conditioner whose label says it also detoxifies ammonia and nitrite. Dose for the full tank volume on every change. It protects fish for about 24 hours, so it buys time but doesn't replace water changes.
4. Match the new water's temperature to the tank.

**Make the fish produce less waste:**

- Feed lightly, once a day or every other day. Fish handle hunger far better than ammonia.
- Remove uneaten food after a few minutes.
- Don't add any more fish until the cycle is finished.

**Don't sabotage the bacteria:**

- Don't replace or deep-clean the filter media.
- Avoid medications unless fish are truly sick. Many kill beneficial bacteria.

**Speed it up:** a bottled bacteria product or seeded media from an established tank (Lesson 6) can cut weeks off a fish-in cycle.

You're done when ammonia and nitrite read 0 for a full week with normal feeding, and nitrate climbs slowly between water changes.$c$ where id = $v$558c0b66-17b0-4cbd-8615-bb2da791ac4b$v$::uuid;
update public.course_sections set content = $c$You can't see ammonia or nitrite. A test kit is the only way to know where your tank stands. Log every result in Water Check so you can spot trends instead of guessing.

**Liquid kits beat strips.** Liquid kits (drops in a test tube) are more accurate and cheaper per test. Strips are handy for quick checks but can read high or low, especially once the tub has been open a while.

**What the numbers mean in a cycled tank:**

- **Ammonia:** healthy is 0 ppm. Act on any reading above 0.
- **Nitrite:** healthy is 0 ppm. Act on any reading above 0.
- **Nitrate:** healthy is under 20 ppm. Once it passes 20 ppm, plan a water change, and don't let it go over 40 ppm.
- **pH:** healthy is steady (most fish do well between 6.5 and 7.8). Watch for sudden swings.

**Reading the stage of a cycle:**

- Ammonia high, nitrite 0: bacteria haven't started. Early cycle.
- Ammonia falling, nitrite rising: the first bacteria are working. Mid cycle.
- Ammonia 0, nitrite falling, nitrate rising: the second bacteria are arriving. Nearly there.
- Ammonia 0, nitrite 0, nitrate rising slowly: cycled.

**pH and temperature change how toxic ammonia is.** In higher pH and warmer water, more of it is in the toxic form. The same 0.5 ppm reading is more dangerous at pH 8.2 than at pH 6.8. In hard, alkaline water, treat any ammonia as urgent.

**Testing tips:**

- Follow the kit's shake and wait times exactly. Nitrate bottles need hard shaking.
- Read colors in daylight against a white background.
- Test your tap water once too. Some tap water already has ammonia (from chloramine) or nitrate in it.
- Check expiry dates. Old reagents give false readings.$c$ where id = $v$2cc6d5e5-d038-4954-a98d-68cdc3bddb7a$v$::uuid;
update public.course_sections set content = $c$A cycled tank can lose part of its bacteria and spike ammonia again. That's a mini-cycle. It's usually caused by something the keeper did, which means it's usually avoidable.

**Common causes, and how to avoid them:**

- **Rinsing filter media in tap water.** Chlorine kills the bacteria. Rinse media in old tank water from a water change instead.
- **Replacing all the filter media at once.** That throws out most of the colony. Replace one part at a time, weeks apart.
- **Filter off for hours (like a power outage).** Without flow, the bacteria run out of oxygen and start to die, and the water inside the filter can go bad. After a long outage (more than a few hours), rinse the media in tank water before restarting.
- **Adding many fish at once.** Ammonia jumps faster than bacteria can grow. Add a few fish at a time, weeks apart.
- **Some medications.** Antibiotics can kill nitrifying bacteria. Treat in a hospital tank when you can, and test daily.
- **A dead fish or rotting food.** A sudden ammonia dump. Count your fish daily and remove leftovers.

**Filter cartridges are a trap.** Many filters tell you to swap the cartridge monthly. Doing that throws out your bacteria every month. Rinse it in tank water instead, and only replace it when it's falling apart. Even better, add a sponge or ceramic media that never gets thrown out.

**How to recover:**

1. Test ammonia and nitrite as soon as you suspect a problem.
2. Do a water change to bring ammonia plus nitrite down to 0.25 ppm or less, using conditioner for the full tank volume.
3. Cut feeding way back until readings return to 0.
4. Add bottled bacteria or seeded media to help it bounce back.
5. Test daily until you get several days of zeros.

Most mini-cycles clear in a week or two if you catch them early. That's why the habit that matters most isn't a product. It's testing.$c$ where id = $v$a48c7061-f43c-4f6c-88bf-8e64e7e3c3c5$v$::uuid;

